{"parentUuid":"242ef758-2667-468a-a4cd-990d284882b3","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"a30145bc0fb1a513e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Tx8RgCuC8cUQSiMqsxre3K","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"helpx.adobe.com substance-3d-painter baking mesh maps ambient occlusion curvature thickness position world space normal ID\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"how to bake mesh maps\",\"url\":\"https://helpx.adobe.com/substance-3d-painter/baking/how-to-bake-mesh-maps.html\"},{\"title\":\"Bake Mesh Maps\",\"url\":\"https://helpx.adobe.com/substance-painter/using/baking.html\"},{\"title\":\"helpx.adobe.com\",\"url\":\"https://helpx.adobe.com/substance-3d-painter/baking.html\"},{\"title\":\"Bake Mesh Maps\",\"url\":\"https://helpx.adobe.com/hu/substance-3d-painter/using/baking.html\"},{\"title\":\"Bake Mesh Maps\",\"url\":\"https://helpx.adobe.com/nz/substance-3d-painter/using/baking.html\"},{\"title\":\"Bake Mesh Maps\",\"url\":\"https://helpx.adobe.com/kr/substance-3d-painter/using/baking.html\"},{\"title\":\"mesh map settings\",\"url\":\"https://helpx.adobe.com/substance-3d-painter/baking/mesh-map-settings.html\"},{\"title\":\"substance 3d painter\",\"url\":\"https://helpx.adobe.com/substance-3d-bake/getting-started/software-interface/substance-3d-painter.html\"},{\"title\":\"How to bake mesh maps\",\"url\":\"https://experienceleague.adobe.com/en/docs/substance-3d-painter/using/baking/how-to-bake-mesh-maps\"}]\n\nThe Adobe docs describe mesh map baking in Substance 3D Painter, though the results don't cover every map you listed. Adobe has moved its Substance 3D Painter documentation to Experience League, and HelpX pages redirect there after March 14th, so the newer Experience League page is the one to check first.\n\n**What baking does:** Baking transfers detail from a high-poly mesh onto a low-poly mesh and stores it in a texture. It lets you combine the high level of detail from the high poly mesh with the low performance costs of the low poly mesh. Painter's filters and generators then use these maps to respond to the geometry. Baking can provide information about ambient shadows, where edges of geometry lie, or how thick an area of the mesh is.\n\n**The maps you asked about:**\n\n- **Ambient Occlusion:** It generates a texture that contains the ambient shadows, similar to overcast lighting from the sky on a white surface.\n- **Curvature:** It generates a texture that contains edges and cavities.\n- **Thickness:** The results only mention thickness in general terms, as noted above. I didn't find a dedicated description of the Thickness baker in these results.\n- **Position:** Baking the position map lets Painter's filters and generators find the bottom of the mesh. The example given is an old car that may have rust applied at its bottom because it hasn't moved for a while.\n- **World Space Normal:** This map shows which part of an object faces up or down in the world. Mask generators can use it, for example, to deposit dust on any surfaces that face up.\n- **ID:** It can be used to generate colored areas to make masking and selecting easier with other tools.\n- **Tangent Space Normal (for context):** It generates a tangent-space normal map that describes the normal angle at the surface of the object.\n\n**Getting started:** To bake, you switch to Baking mode. To be able to bake mesh maps, you need to switch to Baking mode. The F8 shortcut works for this. The baking window opens from the \"Bake Mesh Maps\" button in the current project's Texture Set Settings.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"fff2b8f6-5b4c-47fb-a09f-ea43fb22ae0d","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:20:58.650Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"88000032-daf7-4fdd-aa2c-ab5b09a2a6a8","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":null,"branch":null,"default_branch"