{"parentUuid":"179882fe-91d4-48d4-9e5f-b469809593fb","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"a30145bc0fb1a513e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014c2WEx9JGoYd84HJ1dx4DE","type":"tool_result","content":"The Curvature baker produces a map that describes the mesh's cavities and edges. Concave areas render dark, convex areas render bright, and flat areas land at a neutral gray.\n\nIt has four parameters:\n\n- **Algorithm** sets the method used to compute curvature on the mesh.\n- **Details** sets how strong the curvature information is. Higher values bring out more fine detail but make the result less subtle.\n- **Enable Seams** reduces visible seams between UV islands by copying edge texels from one side of a border to the other.\n- **Seams Intensity** controls how aggressively that seam correction is applied. It only matters when Enable Seams is on.\n\nThe baker is available in Substance Designer, the Substance Automation Toolkit, and Substance Painter. The page doesn't list default values or ranges for the parameters, and it doesn't describe typical downstream uses. Curvature maps are commonly used for edge wear and cavity masking, but that's general knowledge rather than something this page covers."}]},"uuid":"c98f5542-0186-4f2b-bb53-10e1eb4f7586","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:21:48.651Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"fec9dba9-0185-48a4-a162-d2a0ec268dc0","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":null,"branch":null,"default_branch":null,"status":null,"visibility":null,"error":"pending"},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"179882fe-91d4-48d4-9e5f-b469809593fb","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"2c05904e-f9a2-4e2b-a2ba-edf611af270d","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"a30145bc0fb1a513e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0195YFUrtfsuVzk1mbcoGL4G","type":"tool_result","content":"The Position baker records where the mesh sits in space and stores that information in a texture. This is useful for working with an object's volume or building gradient masks. It's available in Substance Painter, Substance Designer, and the Substance Automation Toolkit.\n\nIts main parameters are:\n\n- **Mode:** Chooses between storing all three axes in the color channels of the output, or storing one axis as a grayscale image.\n- **Axis:** Picks which axis to store when Mode is set to single-axis.\n- **Normalization Type:** Sets how values are scaled. Bounding-box scaling handles each axis by the mesh's extent along it, while bounding-sphere scaling uses one radius for all axes.\n- **Normalization Scale:** Sets the range of the scaling. Per Material fits values from 0 to 1 within each texture set. Full Scene, the default, treats the whole scene as one space so values stay continuous across objects and materials.\n\nThe page doesn't specifically mention the Position generator or dirt effects at the bottom of an object. Those are common uses for position data, but they aren't covered in this source."}]},"uuid":"ba01afbd-5813-4d0e-aac6-f22b3793bdc1","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:21:48.941Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"fec9dba9-0185-48a4-a162-d2a0ec268dc0","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":null,"branch":null,"default_branch":null,"status":null,"visibility":null,"error":"pending"},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"2c05904e-f9a2-4e2b-a2ba-edf611af270d","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"