{"parentUuid":"f20e112d-42cc-4cc3-bf07-4b69b376d478","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"a30145bc0fb1a513e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01T5fieFURvi9hi6Bwc8HfdT","type":"tool_result","content":"The Ambient Occlusion baker generates an ambient shadow texture that darkens areas where light is likely to be blocked, such as crevices and contact points. It runs on the GPU with a fast algorithm. It is available in Substance Designer and the Substance Automation Toolkit.\n\nIts parameters are:\n\n- **Normal Map:** An optional input that adds fine surface detail to the calculation.\n- **World Space:** Tells the baker the input normal map is in world space rather than tangent space. It has no effect unless a normal map is provided.\n- **Invert Normal:** Computes the map with flipped normals, which can produce a thickness-style map.\n- **Use Unselected Mesh Parts:** Lets unselected parts of the mesh contribute to the occlusion.\n- **Quality:** Sets the number of passes: Low (3), Medium (5, the default), High (10), or Very High (16). More passes take longer.\n- **Precision Bias:** Lower values increase precision but can introduce artifacts.\n- **Distance Fade:** Controls how far the occlusion spreads.\n\nThe page warns that older GPUs may not be supported, and that high-resolution bakes on low-end or mobile GPUs can crash.\n\nThe page doesn't explain how Painter generators or filters use the result, so I can't describe that from this content."}]},"uuid":"d0650db2-af00-4597-8845-8cea0823391f","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:21:49.323Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"fec9dba9-0185-48a4-a162-d2a0ec268dc0","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":null,"branch":null,"default_branch":null,"status":null,"visibility":null,"error":"pending"},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"f20e112d-42cc-4cc3-bf07-4b69b376d478","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"6f1a5cea-24eb-4095-90ab-5e21f78ddaca","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"a30145bc0fb1a513e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DvhLvDcffPn8omXqBAjRPE","type":"tool_result","content":"The Thickness baker estimates how thick a model is at each point on its surface. It fires rays from the surface into the interior, similar to ambient occlusion, and records how far they travel before hitting the other side. Dark values mark thin areas and bright values mark thick areas. The output is typically used in subsurface scattering (SSS) shaders to fake light passing through thin regions, or as a mask for other texture effects. It's available in Substance Painter, Substance Designer, and the Substance Automation Toolkit.\n\n**Key parameters:**\n\n- **Secondary Rays:** Number of rays per point. Default is 64. More rays reduce noise but increase bake time.\n- **Min and Max Occluder Distance:** The shortest and longest distances a ray can register a hit. Defaults are 0.00001 and 0.1.\n- **Relative to Bounding Box:** When on, the distance values scale to the object's bounding box diagonal. When off, they use the scene's export units.\n- **Spread Angle:** Limits how widely the rays fan out. Default is 180 degrees.\n- **Distribution:** Sets how rays are spread across the angle, either Cosine (default) or Uniform.\n- **Ignore Backface:** Discards hits on faces that point away from the low-poly surface. It's usually best left on to avoid artifacts.\n- **Self Occlusion:** Controls which high-poly meshes the low-poly mesh is matched against. \"Always\" matches all of them (default), while \"By Mesh Name\" filters by name to exclude unwanted geometry.\n- **Automatic Normalization:** Rescales the output so the thinnest point is black and the thickest is white, using the full 0–1 range."}]},"uuid":"c2fa78a3-08c8-45e6-a27c-685672a6a1df","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:21:49.877Z","serverClassifierCon