{"parentUuid":"c13827b2-8a2c-4e81-8753-b457dc548984","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"a30145bc0fb1a513e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WvG9TddnaXAcPeV6De5pzn","type":"tool_result","content":"A filter effect in Painter is a substance that changes the contents of a layer or mask. When a layer uses the Passthrough blend mode, its filter can modify the results of everything beneath it, so you can apply a filter to the whole layer stack.\n\n**Applying a filter**\n\n- **Manually:** Select a layer's content or its mask, open the Effect menu (or right-click), and choose Add filter. In the Properties panel, pick the filter you want. To affect the layers below, set the layer to Passthrough. The page notes this is most often used on masks.\n- **Drag-and-drop:** Open the Filters section of the Shelf and drag a filter into the layer stack, placed carefully so it doesn't land in an unintended group. This method only works for whole layer stacks, not masks, and it automatically sets all channels to Passthrough.\n\n**Channels affected**\n\nThe page doesn't list individual channels. It says the drag-and-drop method sets the blend mode to Passthrough on all channels of the document, and that manually added filters may need Passthrough to affect the layers beneath them.\n\n**Other notes**\n\n- You can import new filters (SBSAR files) by dragging them onto the Assets Panel.\n- Custom filters are created in Substance 3D Designer, which offers templates for Painter.\n\n**Filter pages**\n\n*Standard*\n- Blur: /en/docs/substance-3d-painter/using/effects/filters/standard-filters/blur\n- Blur Directional: /en/docs/substance-3d-painter/using/effects/filters/standard-filters/blur-directional\n- Blur Slope: /en/docs/substance-3d-painter/using/effects/filters/standard-filters/blur-slope\n- Clamp: /en/docs/substance-3d-painter/using/effects/filters/standard-filters/clamp\n- Color Balance: /en/docs/substance