{"parentUuid":"3bdea959-dba7-4d74-94dd-9c92a9bff344","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"a30145bc0fb1a513e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LVuzeWspPBYUGfY95NmUwA","type":"tool_result","content":"The Sharpen filter adds crispness to selected channels, mainly texture layers or masks in color or grayscale. You can place it on a paint layer at the top of the stack, set it to passthrough, and apply it to all channels so everything below is sharpened. It works best with a light touch.\n\n**Parameters:**\n\n- **Sharpen Intensity:** Controls how strong the sharpening effect is. The source doesn't list a default or range."}]},"uuid":"05728888-9cdb-4e55-8f3a-b88340ac9df3","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:23:37.294Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"ce263cda-ec4e-4b1d-a326-99c113d7105f","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":null,"branch":null,"default_branch":null,"status":null,"visibility":null,"error":"pending"},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"3bdea959-dba7-4d74-94dd-9c92a9bff344","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"73b538de-de06-4ad7-a6a7-1997c5ed1935","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"a30145bc0fb1a513e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016wYAhfW9cKpkpdJtYustbV","type":"tool_result","content":"The Gradient Dynamic filter recolors a grayscale image using a gradient. Each grayscale value is matched to a point on that gradient, so darker and lighter areas take on the colors placed along it. You'd typically use it on a texture layer, or inside a mask where the output stays black and white. The page doesn't describe dynamic range or min/max behavior.\n\n**Input**\n\n- **Gradient Source (Color):** The gradient comes from a custom color map or an anchor point.\n\n**Parameters**\n\n- **Gradient Orientation:** Sets whether the gradient is sampled horizontally or vertically.\n- **Gradient Input Position:** Shifts where along the gradient the sampling occurs.\n\nThe page doesn't list default values or ranges for either parameter."}]},"uuid":"00081d7c-fe02-4b45-93c1-eda8f18f9f12","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:23:37.640Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"ce263cda-ec4e-4b1d-a326-99c113d7105f","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":null,"branch":null,"default_branch":null,"status":null,"visibility":null,"error":"pending"},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"73b538de-de06-4ad7-a6a7-1997c5ed1935","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"b40ec3cb-a6c4-4e58-9bd2-911c26744e2f","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"a30145bc0fb1a513e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Qe42vpt4KUbuJueeZevLzR","type":"tool_result","content":"The Height To Normal filter reads the height information in a texture layer and converts it into normal data, which describes the surface direction at each point. It is meant to produce accurate normals from height.\n\n**Parameters**\n\n- **Overwrite Existing Normal:** A toggle that controls whether the filter replaces any normal data already on the layer.\n- **Use World Units:** A toggle that switches the conversion between real-world measurements and a manual intensity setting.\n- **Normal Intensity:** Adjusts the strength of the generated normals. It applies only when Use World Units is off.\n- **Surface Size (cm):** Sets the size of the represented surface, using the larger of its width or height. It applies only when Use World Units is on.\n- **Height Depth (cm):** Sets the maximum height range of the surface. It applies only when Use World Units is on.\n\n**Notes**\n\n- The page gives no default values or numeric ranges for any parameter.\n- It doesn't describe normal format or orientation options, such as OpenGL versus DirectX conventions, or any blend mode.\n- The intensity and size/depth parameters are mutually exclusive, depending on the Use World Units setting."}]},"uuid":"0d844faa-5e52-458d-a3d3-f057040dadb1","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:23:38.536Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"ce263cda-ec4e-4b1d-a326-99c113d7105f","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":null,"branch":null,"default_branch":null,"status":null,"visibility":null,"error":"pending"},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"b40ec3cb-a6c4-4e58-9bd2-911c26744e2f","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"4b573d12-c994-4757-9b48-d755500a1b59","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"a30145bc0fb1a513e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JBuzUVb4cyYrRJkhwKQr8K","type":"tool_result","content":"**Blur Slope** is a filter in the Effects/Blur category that works on grayscale data. It smears or fades pixels, and the effect is most visible along sharp contrast edges between colors. You can apply it to a texture layer to blur a whole material or a specific texture, or to a mask to smear the mask. Typical uses include chipped or worn edges, dirt that bleeds past its boundaries, and smeared rust. The page doesn't explain in detail how the slope input steers the blur direction. It only states that the blur concentrates at high-contrast transitions.\n\n**Input**\n- **Custom Noise (grayscale):** Accepts a custom texture or an anchor point to use as the noise source.\n\n**Main parameters**\n- **Seed:** Randomizes the pattern while keeping your other settings the same.\n- **Intensity:** Controls the strength of the blur.\n- **Intensity Divider:** Controls how the blur intensity is distributed. The page doesn't list its options.\n- **Blending Mode:** Sets how the blur result combines with the underlying data. The page doesn't list the available modes.\n- **Quality:** Trades render quality against performance.\n\n**Source parameters**\n- **Source Type:** Chooses the noise source. The options are the default noise, the previous input, or a custom noise.\n- **Blur:** Softens the source noise or input before it's used.\n- **Position:** Sets the midpoint of the source, which works like a brightness adjustment.\n- **Contrast:** Adjusts the contrast of the source.\n- **Source Tiling:** Sets how the source repeats across the surface.\n\nThe page doesn't give default values or numeric ranges for any parameter, and it doesn't mention a height channel.\n\n**Notes for height and erosion work:** The page doesn't cover height maps directly. For erosion-style results, it suggests using Blur Slope on masks or texture layers to get worn edges, dirt spillover, and rust smearing. Adjust Source Type and Custom Noise to control the pattern, then use Intensity and Contrast to control how far the effect spreads."}]},"uuid":"339d5198-55b1-4778-9807-e2d80c4ffde7","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:23:40.907Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"ce263cda-ec4e-4b1d-a326-99c113d7105f","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":null,"branch":null,"default_branch":null,"status":null,"visibility":null,"error":"pending"},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"4b573d12-c994-4757-9b48-d755500a1b59","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"de0c2720-f5b1-40c0-beb5-a0878afd9900","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"a30145bc0fb1a513e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HygrbBfRKCt8RBZV8ZHd3z","type":"tool_result","content":"## Warp filter overview\n\nThe Warp filter distorts textures, masks, shapes, and outlines using noise. It offers regular warping, directional warping that pushes in a chosen direction, and multidirectional warping for more varied results.\n\n**Inputs:** The only input is **Custom Noise**, which lets you supply your own texture as the noise map.\n\n**Intensity:** The Intensity parameter controls how strongly the warp deforms the target. It works with the Intensity Divider, which determines how that intensity is distributed.\n\n## Parameters\n\n**Main parameters**\n- **Seed:** Generates a different random variation while keeping your other settings.\n- **Warp Mode:** Chooses the type of warp (regular, directional, or multidirectional).\n- **Intensity:** Sets the strength of the deformation.\n- **Intensity Divider:** Sets how the intensity is divided.\n- **Angle:** Sets the direction of the warp, mainly relevant to directional warping.\n- **Blending Mode:** Sets how the warp result blends with the underlying data.\n- **Directions:** Sets the number of warp directions.\n\n**Source parameters**\n- **Source Mode:** Chooses the noise source:\n  - *Default Noise* uses the built-in noise.\n  - *Previous Input* reuses the noise from the preceding input. If a fill layer uses a specific noise pattern, this lets the warp match it.\n  - *Custom Noise* uses the texture supplied through the Custom Noise input.\n- **Source Blur:** Softens the source noise.\n- **Source Balance:** Shifts the noise midpoint toward black or white, like a brightness control.\n- **Source Contrast:** Adjusts the noise contrast.\n- **Source Tilling** (spelled this way in the source): Controls how the source noise tiles.\n\nThe page does not list default values or numeric ranges for any parameter.\n\n## Notes on use for masks and height\n\n- The Warp filter works on a texture layer or inside a mask. Inside a mask, its output is black and white, so it can deform mask shapes and edges.\n- To keep a warped mask consistent with a fill layer's noise, set Source Mode to Previous Input.\n- The page does not describe using Warp directly on height data, so check the Substance 3D Painter help for height-specific guidance."}]},"uuid":"4079afca-1a45-4cd6-9715-012aa5dcc7b8","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:23:41.006Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"ce263cda-ec4e-4b1d-a326-99c113d7105f","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":null,"branch":null,"default_branch":null,"status":null,"visibility":null,"error":"pending"},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"de0c2720-f5b1-40c0-beb5-a0878afd9900","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main"}
