{"parentUuid":"e7ab1b42-9a72-4816-92da-2a941e564ffb","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"a30145bc0fb1a513e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GQbT1LvAFEPCQH7s6wYJHS","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Substance 3D Painter layer blending modes height channel Normal Replace Add Subtract Min Max Passthrough Normal map combine documentation experienceleague\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Blending modes\",\"url\":\"https://experienceleague.adobe.com/en/docs/substance-3d-painter/using/interface/layer-stack/blending-modes\"},{\"title\":\"TUTORIAL ARTICLE\",\"url\":\"https://www.adobe.com/learn/substance-3d-designer/web/mastering-blending-modes\"},{\"title\":\"blending modes\",\"url\":\"https://experienceleague.adobe.com/zh-hans/docs/substance-3d-painter/using/interface/layer-stack/blending-modes\"},{\"title\":\"blending modes\",\"url\":\"https://experienceleague.adobe.com/ja/docs/substance-3d-painter/using/interface/layer-stack/blending-modes\"},{\"title\":\"blending modes\",\"url\":\"https://experienceleague.adobe.com/zh-hant/docs/substance-3d-painter/using/interface/layer-stack/blending-modes\"},{\"title\":\"Modes de fusion\",\"url\":\"https://experienceleague.adobe.com/fr/docs/substance-3d-painter/using/interface/layer-stack/blending-modes\"},{\"title\":\"blending modes\",\"url\":\"https://helpx.adobe.com/substance-3d-painter/interface/layer-stack/blending-modes.html\"},{\"title\":\"community.adobe.com\",\"url\":\"https://community.adobe.com/t5/substance-3d-painter-discussions/tile-generator-blending-mode-stuck-on-add/m-p/12973783/highlight/true\"},{\"title\":\"community.adobe.com\",\"url\":\"https://community.adobe.com/t5/substance-3d-painter-discussions/how-to-get-normals-to-not-bleed-through-from-texture-to-another/m-p/15216233\"},{\"title\":\"community.adobe.com\",\"url\":\"https://community.adobe.com/t5/substance-3d-painter-discussions/tile-generator-blending-mode-stuck-on-add/m-p/12973966\"}]\n\nThe English Experience League page for Substance 3D Painter's blending modes covers Normal, Passthrough, Replace, Multiply, and Divide, but the snippet cuts off before Add, Subtract, Min, and Max. For those I can point to the Adobe tutorial series and a community thread rather than the official definitions.\n\n**Normal and Passthrough**\n- Normal shows the Top layer over the Bottom layer without transformation, acting as a copy mode. If the top layer has alpha, the Bottom layer shows through the transparent pixels.\n- Passthrough flattens the Bottom layer into the Top layer. It is mainly used to apply an effect to every layer below, or to smudge or clone the layers beneath. Dragging an effect into the stack creates a layer set to Passthrough on all its channels.\n\n**Replace**\n- Replace overwrites the Bottom layer. Unlike Normal, it also ignores the alpha in the Top layer, which can produce transparent pixels.\n\n**Add, Subtract, Min, and Max**\n- The English page's description of these modes is cut off in the results. The Adobe tutorial series \"Mastering Blending Modes\" has dedicated lessons on Add & Subtract and Min & Max, and focuses on masks and heightmaps. That series is aimed at Substance Designer and Painter together, so I could not confirm Painter-specific behavior from it.\n- A community thread on the Tile Generator reports that 'Add' and 'Max' gave no visible difference to one user, while 'Add Sub' did give a different result. A community expert said the blending setting affects how the generated pattern blends with the background level. This is anecdotal and concerns the Tile Generator, not general layer blending.\n\n**Normal map modes**\n- The Chinese-language version of the Experience League page lists the normal-map modes. It describes Normal map combine as a blend that preserves detail while keeping flat normals working correctly. It describes Normal map detail as a detail-oriented blend, based on Reoriented Normal Mapping, that keeps flat normal maps and the strength of both sources. Normal map inverse detail behaves the same way, except the bottom layer is transformed to fit the top layer's surface. The French page carries the same inverse-detail description.\n- The English page for these modes wasn't in the results, so I can't confirm the English wording.\n\n**Height and Normal defaults**\n- Adobe's community manager noted that when you add a new material to the layer stack, the Normal channel's blending mode is natively set to Normal map detail. He also advised checking the Height channel's blending mode, since the issue will probably be the same. A user had leather bleeding its chainmail normals and height, and the fix was switching those channels to Normal. Make sure you selected the correct channel before switching the blending mode.\n\n**Gaps:** The full English definitions for Add, Subtract, Min, and Max, and the English Normal map combine page, didn't come back in this search. The official page at experienceleague.adobe.com/en/docs/substance-3d-painter/using/interface/layer-stack/blending-modes should have them.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"90bc0a15-0d17-4440-bf26-653d5c6d3934","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:24:49.391Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"5d5ebc15-23ac-4ce9-a8fd-a21e16dee8a6","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":null,"branch":null,"default_branch":null,"status":null,"visibility":null,"error":"pending"},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"e7ab1b42-9a72-4816-92da-2a941e564ffb","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"user_permanent","reasonType":"rule"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main"}
