t, Difference, Exclusion, Normal map combine, Normal map detail, Normal map inverse detail, Value, Color, Tint, Saturation, Hue, Inverse Divide, Inverse Subtract, Linear Burn...), flagging which ones are recommended for height or normal channels, and any statement about how the height channel and normal channel combine across layers. Do not copy verbatim."}},"serverClassifierRequest":"382a656b-78c8-4c60-abec-ffe057566349","apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CfvbvJcfvYgkCyZtYNByS","requestedModel":"claude-sonnet-5-5","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","attributionSkill":"zenray-houdini","type":"assistant","uuid":"bcbf4577-d499-4648-9a13-571f10ae2da1","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:24:52.876Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"max","perTurnEffort":"max","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"bcbf4577-d499-4648-9a13-571f10ae2da1","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"a30145bc0fb1a513e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015RW4mEWNf1AtY9nfddKTbg","type":"tool_result","content":"Here are the 32 blending modes in the order the page lists them. The page doesn't include a mode called \"Hue,\" so I've left it out.\n\n1. **Normal:** Shows the top layer over the bottom layer unchanged. Transparent pixels in the top layer reveal the layers below.\n2. **Passthrough:** Flattens the layers below into the top layer, so effects, smudge, or clone can act on everything beneath it. Effects dropped into the stack use this mode automatically.\n3. **Disable:** Skips blending for that channel and shows only the layers below. This can save computation.\n4. **Replace:** Overwrites the layers below and ignores the top layer's transparency.\n5. **Multiply:** Multiplies the top layer over the bottom, always producing a darker result.\n6. **Divide:** Divides the layers below by the top layer's color. The result is usually lighter and can look blown out.\n7. **Inverse Divide:** Works like Divide with the top and bottom roles swapped.\n8. **Darken (Min):** Keeps the darker of the two values at each point.\n9. **Lighten (Max):** Keeps the brighter of the two values at each point.\n10. **Linear Dodge (Add):** Adds the top layer to the bottom and can clip unless the channel is HDR. **Height:** the page recommends it for accumulating height information.\n11. **Subtract:** Subtracts the top layer from the bottom and can clip below zero.\n12. **Inverse Subtract:** Works like Subtract with the roles swapped.\n13. **Difference:** Subtracts and takes the absolute value, so negative results become positive.\n14. **Exclusion:** Like Difference, but with lower contrast.\n15. **Signed Addition (AddSub):** Adds lighter top values to the bottom and subtracts darker ones. Mid-gray has no effect.\n16. **Overlay:** Combines Multiply and Screen. Dark top values darken the result, bright top values lighten it, and gray has no effect.\n17. **Screen:** Inverts both layers, multiplies them, and inverts the result, producing a brighter image.\n18. **Linear Burn:** Adds the two layers together and subtracts 1.\n19. **Color Burn:** Darkens the bottom layer based on the top, increasing contrast. Darker bottom areas show more of their color.\n20. **Color Dodge:** Lightens the bottom layer based on the top. Brighter top values have a stronger effect.\n21. **Soft Light:** Resembles Overlay with a gentler curve and less contrast.\n22. **Hard Light:** Resembles Overlay with the Multiply and Screen order reversed, giving less contrast.\n23. **Vivid Light:** Applies Color Dodge to light values and Color Burn to dark values, producing higher contrast. Gray is unaffected.\n24. **Linear Light:** Applies Linear Dodge to light values and Linear Burn to dark values. It's similar to Vivid Light with less contrast.\n25. **Pin Light:** Lightens or darkens based on the top layer's extremes. It can create blotchy noise and removes midtones.\n26. **Tint:** Uses the top layer's hue with the bottom layer's saturation and value. Very dark colors keep the bottom layer unchanged.\n27. **Saturation:** Uses the top layer's saturation with the bottom layer's hue and value. Dark areas become grayscale.\n28. **Color:** Uses the top layer's hue and saturation with the bottom layer's value.\n29. **Value:** Uses the top layer's value with the bottom layer's hue and saturation.\n30. **Normal Map Combine:** A Whiteout-style blend that keeps detail while flat normals still work correctly. **Normal channel only.**\n31. **Normal Map Detail:** A reoriented normal mapping blend. The top normal is reoriented to follow the bottom surface, preserving flat areas and the intensity of both sources. **Normal channel only.**\n32. **Normal Map Inverse Detail:** Works like Normal Map Detail, but the bottom normal is reoriented to fit the top surface. **Normal channel only.**\n\n**Height and normal channels across layers:** The page doesn't give separate combining rules for height. It says layers are computed from the bottom up, each top layer is mixed with the one below using its blending mode, and then its mask is applied. Blending modes are set per channel, and all blending happens internally in linear gamma space. The page also says the normal map modes are only useful for the Normal channel."}]},"uuid":"06b1d883-db35-4754-bc95-5d10ba2c354b","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:25:09.327Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"382a656b-78c8-4c60-abec-ffe057566349","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":null,"branch":null,"default_branch":null,"status":null,"visibility":null,"error":"pending"},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"bcbf4577-d499-4648-9a13-571f10ae2da1","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main"}
