{"parentUuid":"78347bc1-e40b-417f-8927-d960c91caf36","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"a30145bc0fb1a513e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SzkJ126JwCgB7KLyWqhynm","type":"tool_result","content":"**Inputs**\n- **height:** The heightfield layer that terraces are added to.\n- **mask:** An optional per-pixel mask that scales the effect of the **Mask** parameter.\n\n**Outputs**\n- **height:** The original heightfield blended with the terraces.\n- **mesa:** A mask of the flat terrace floors.\n- **cliffs:** A mask of the steep terrace walls.\n\n**Parameters**\n- **Mask:** Controls how strongly the terraces are blended into the original height.\n- **Compute Range:** Automatically sets Min Height and Max Height from the input's lowest and highest values.\n- **Min Height / Max Height:** Define the height range where terraces form. You can also pick these values from the viewport with the dropper tool.\n- **Method:** Chooses how step size is defined, either directly or by a count of terraces.\n- **Step Size:** Sets the height of each terrace step. Appears when Method is Step Size.\n- **Terraces:** Sets how many steps fit between Min Height and Max Height. Appears when Method is Number of Terraces.\n- **Offset:** Shifts the heights at which the steps occur.\n- **Random Step:** Adds random variation to each step's height. At 0, all steps are equal.\n- **Step Seed:** Sets the random pattern used by Random Step.\n- **Add Layers:** Adds extra terrace layers, each with a randomly varied offset.\n- **Layers:** Sets how many extra layers to add when Add Layers is on.\n- **Seed:** Sets the random offsets for the added layers when Add Layers is on.\n- **Global Seed:** Controls the randomness for both the steps and the layers.\n\nThe page doesn't include parameters named \"smoothness\" or \"spacing,\" so I've left them out. The closest equivalents are Step Size, Terraces, and Offset."}]},"uuid":"3ed3a80b-af54-4dd4-ac64-3d51e812fe53","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:27:46.345Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"254728c3-ca7a-4ee7-95d1-d8bf7a15bb52","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":null,"branch":null,"default_branch":null,"status":null,"visibility":null,"error":"pending"},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"78347bc1-e40b-417f-8927-d960c91caf36","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"5b70dee8-3492-458e-a4b6-44507995a612","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"a30145bc0fb1a513e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EDSJ1biuKEa8re3Wyt6pjD","type":"tool_result","content":"**Inputs**\n- **height:** The terrain layer that the debris slumps across.\n- **debris:** An optional layer that sets the starting depth of loose material. If unwired, debris is a constant value of 1.\n\n**Outputs**\n- **height:** The updated terrain, optionally with the slumped debris added back in.\n- **debris:** The debris left after slumping.\n- **flow:** A UV layer showing the direction material moved.\n\n**Parameters**\n\n*Slump behavior*\n- **Slump Mode:** Chooses the transport method: Smooth (liquid-like diffusion), Granular (chunky, randomized flow), or Rainfall (water-driven erosion and transport).\n- **Debris Scale:** Multiplies the debris layer before slumping, so a mask can be converted into a depth. Without a debris input, it sets the uniform depth.\n- **Spread Iterations:** The number of material transport steps to simulate.\n- **Repose Angle:** The steepest slope, in degrees from horizontal, that material can hold without sliding. Setting it to 0 is useful for flow fields.\n- **Spread Rate:** How quickly material moves across the terrain in each iteration.\n- **Random Seed:** Sets the randomness for the slumping process.\n\n*Rainfall mode only*\n- **Flow Force:** How strongly streams erode the debris.\n- **Rainfall Coverage:** The amount of rain simulated per frame, as a share of the terrain surface.\n- **Slope Influence:** How closely streams follow the terrain slope. Low values give straighter channels, and high values give more winding ones.\n- **Erosion Rate:** How fast a stream picks up material.\n- **Deposition Rate:** How fast a stream drops its extra sediment.\n- **Removal Rate:** How fast carried sediment breaks down. Higher values let erosion cut deeper, producing a rockier look.\n- **Evaporation Rate:** How fast a stream loses its capacity to carry sediment. Low values create long, persistent channels, and high values create smaller, soil-like depressions.\n\n*Height handling*\n- **Remove Debris from Input Height:** Subtracts the input debris from the height before slumping.\n- **Add Debris to Output Height:** Adds the slumped debris back into the height after simulation."}]},"uuid":"34a54ac2-7406-4661-b114-6b00da69f1da","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:27:46.792Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"254728c3-ca7a-4ee7-95d1-d8bf7a15bb52","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":null,"branch":null,"default_branch":null,"status":null,"visibility":null,"error":"pending"},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"5b70dee8-3492-458e-a4b6-44507995a612","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"e19fc287-ba7b-4800-b0a1-bb32fd1c50a1","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"a30145bc0fb1a513e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NVnkvYfnh6fjtA4wi1pq8N","type":"tool_result","content":"**Inputs**\n- **height**: The heightfield the mask is generated from.\n\n**Outputs**\n- **mask**: A single mask made from the features you measure. If you measure several, they are combined using the Blend Mode.\n\n**General parameters**\n- **Smooth Radius**: Blurs the heightfield before features are measured, to reduce noise.\n- **Blend Mode**: Sets how multiple feature masks are combined: Add, Multiply, Average, Minimum, or Maximum.\n- **Invert Mask**: Flips the final mask values.\n\n**Slope**\n- **Mask by Slope**: Turns slope masking on or off.\n- **Min/Max Slope Angle**: Set the range of slopes to include.\n- **Slope Ramp**: Shapes how the mask fades across that angle range.\n\n**Height**\n- **Mask by Height**: Turns height masking on or off.\n- **Compute Range**: Fills Min and Max Height from the input's actual height values.\n- **Min/Max Height**: Set the elevation range to include.\n- **Height Ramp**: Shapes how the mask fades across that elevation range.\n\n**Peaks and Valleys (curvature)**\n- **Mask by Curvature**: Turns curvature masking on or off.\n- **Max Curvature**: Limits the included absolute curvature.\n- **Curvature Ramp**: Shapes the mask transition between negative and positive curvature limits, which distinguishes peaks from valleys.\n\n**Direction**\n- **Mask by Direction**: Turns directional masking on or off.\n- **Goal Angle**: The slope direction to target, with 0 degrees along the width of the heightfield.\n- **Angle Spread**: How far from the goal angle still counts as a match.\n- **Direction Ramp**: Shapes the mask fade across that angular range.\n\n**Occlusion**\n- **Mask by Occlusion**: Turns ambient occlusion masking on or off.\n- **Compute Range**: Fills Min and Max Occlusion from the input's actual values.\n- **Min/Max Occlusion**: Set the occlusion range to include.\n- **Height Scale**: Scales height before occlusion is computed. Higher values exaggerate small height variations.\n- **Occlusion Ramp**: Shapes the mask fade across the occlusion range.\n\n*Quality (under Occlusion)*\n- **View Radius**: How far rays reach when testing occlusion. Larger values are more precise but slower.\n- **Step Scale**: Step spacing along rays. Larger steps are faster but can miss thin features.\n- **Sample Count**: Number of rays per pixel. More rays are more accurate but slower.\n- **Sample from Hemisphere**: Changes the angle normalization used in the occlusion calculation.\n\nThe page does not describe a flow feature, so I can't cover flow masking from this content."}]},"uuid":"aaf9b213-ea57-4bbe-8abf-1c37999e1d21","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:27:47.937Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"254728c3-ca7a-4ee7-95d1-d8bf7a15bb52","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":null,"branch":null,"default_branch":null,"status":null,"visibility":null,"error":"pending"},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"e19fc287-ba7b-4800-b0a1-bb32fd1c50a1","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"f8263804-6b89-4dc9-8108-1e31c15e74e9","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"a30145bc0fb1a513e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RKsA2V8La1qaLAvDhXJLck","type":"tool_result","content":"**Inputs**\n- **size_ref:** A reference layer that sets the output image size and metadata.\n- **pos:** An optional UV layer that replaces each pixel's image coordinates as the noise source.\n\n**Outputs**\n- **noise:** The computed cellular noise.\n- **border:** A Mono SDF layer giving the exact distance to the boundary between the two closest points. Useful for even-width cell edges.\n- **center:** A UV layer with the image coordinates of the nearest grid point. A UV Sample node can use it for mosaic effects.\n- **id:** An ID layer with a hash of the nearest grid point. Values can be negative.\n\n**Range**\n- **Amplitude:** Controls strength and contrast between cell patterns.\n- **Shift:** Raises or lowers overall brightness.\n\n**Pattern**\n- **Element Size:** Sets the basic cell size in image coordinates.\n- **Element Scale:** Per-axis scaling of cell size for stretched (anisotropic) patterns. Requires Per-Component Controls.\n- **Jitter:** Randomly displaces grid points before distances are measured. Values above 1 can cause artifacts.\n- **Jitter Scale:** Per-axis jitter amount. Requires Per-Component Controls.\n- **Lattice Type:** Grid gives square cells. Hexagonal gives hexagonal-style cells using a simplicial grid.\n- **Metric:** The distance formula. Euclidean gives round cells, Manhattan gives diamond shapes, and Chebyshev gives square shapes.\n- **Smooth Edge:** Adjusts how round cells are when Metric is Euclidean.\n- **Offset:** Shifts the whole noise pattern.\n- **Tile Size:** Sets the repeat period so the noise tiles seamlessly. Related values may be rounded or clamped to keep tiling valid.\n\n**Shape**\n- **Mode:** Absolute uses image-space values for shape settings. Relative uses a 0–1 range, where 0 is the cell edge and 1 is the center.\n- **Gap:** Space between cells. Larger gaps shrink cells, possibly until they disappear.\n- **Bevel:** Slopes the cell edges inward, from pointed tops to flat tops with hard edges.\n- **Bevel Ramp:** Reshapes the bevel profile from the cell border to the inset distance.\n\n**Post Processing** (applied in the listed order)\n- **Fold:** Takes the absolute value of negative values.\n- **Complement:** Inverts values (x becomes 1 − x). Best for the 0–1 range.\n- **Bias:** Pushes mid-gray values toward black or white.\n- **Gain:** Pushes mid-tones together and separates darks and lights.\n- **Gamma:** Adjusts overall brightness curve.\n- **Contrast:** Expands or compresses the tonal range around mid-gray.\n- **Clamp Minimum / Clamp Maximum:** Limits values to a lower or upper threshold.\n\nThe page does not mention F1/F2 distances or an output-type selector, so I can't describe those."}]},"uuid":"4091d550-54c1-4826-999e-9ace1e37f2d7","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:27:50.088Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"254728c3-ca7a-4ee7-95d1-d8bf7a15bb52","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":null,"branch":null,"default_branch":null,"status":null,"visibility":null,"error":"pending"},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"f8263804-6b89-4dc9-8108-1e31c15e74e9","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"2e6cbbda-12d6-4837-8823-d74a29e68ad4","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"a30145bc0fb1a513e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FyirGqKRobaeMW5CyNocq5","type":"tool_result","content":"**Inputs**\n\n- **height:** The base height layer that the strata are added to.\n- **baseheight:** An optional extra height layer that warps the strata pattern.\n- **mask:** An optional per-pixel mask, scaled by the Mask parameter.\n\n**Outputs**\n\n- **height:** The input height blended with the strata. With Output Strata Only enabled, just the strata layer.\n\n**Parameters**\n\n*General*\n- **Mask:** How much strata noise is mixed into the height.\n- **Blend Mode:** How strata combine with the height (Add, Subtract, Multiply, Difference, Maximum, Minimum).\n- **Output Strata Only:** Outputs the strata layer by itself instead of blending it.\n\n*Range*\n- **Amplitude:** Scales the strata noise strength before Center is applied.\n- **Center:** Shifts the output value range up or down.\n- **Noise Contrast:** Increases or decreases the noise's contrast before Amplitude and Center.\n\n*Pattern*\n- **Noise Type:** The base noise function (Simplex, Perlin, two Worley variants, White, Alligator, Sparse Convolution).\n- **Metric:** For Worley noise, the distance measure (Euclidean for circles, Manhattan for diamonds, Chebyshev for squares).\n- **Worley Jitter:** Randomly displaces the Worley points. Values above 1 can create artifacts.\n- **Units / Space:** The measurement system used to control the pattern.\n- **Element Size:** The size of the basic noise element. Per-Component Controls enable Element Scale for per-axis stretching.\n- **Offset:** Shifts the entire noise pattern.\n- **Strata Size:** The thickness of the layers.\n- **Strata Offset:** Shifts the layers' position.\n- **Tilt:** The layers' angle in degrees. At zero they're perpendicular to the height; higher values make them cut across it.\n- **Rotate:** Rotates the strata around the Origin.\n- **Origin:** The pivot point for rotation.\n- **Add Layers:** Adds multiple extra strata layers with varied orientations.\n- **Layers:** How many extra layers to add when Add Layers is on.\n- **Roughness:** How much each successive layer's amplitude is scaled.\n- **Tilt Variance / Rotate Variance:** The maximum random deviation in degrees for each extra layer's tilt and rotation.\n- **Seed:** The random seed that determines the extra layers' orientations.\n- **Base Height Influence:** How strongly the baseheight input distorts the strata pattern.\n\n*Fractal*\n- **Fractal Type:** How finer detail is layered on (None, Sharp, Dampened, Terrain, Hybrid Terrain, which sharpens peaks for rocky terrain).\n- **Max Octaves:** How many times the noise is layered on itself.\n- **Lacunarity:** How much the scale changes with each layer.\n- **Octave Offset:** How much the noise is offset with each layer.\n- **Roughness:** How much each layer's amplitude is scaled.\n\n*Post Processing* (applied in the listed order)\n- **Fold:** Takes the absolute value of the noise.\n- **Complement:** Inverts values (x becomes 1 − x).\n- **Bias:** Pushes mid-grey values toward 0 or 1.\n- **Gain:** Pushes mid-grey values together while spreading out the darks and lights.\n- **Gamma:** Adjusts overall brightness and darkness.\n- **Contrast:** Expands or compresses the tonal range around mid-grey.\n- **Clamp Minimum / Clamp Maximum:** Limits values to a lower and upper threshold."}]},"uuid":"f6f52a4a-6787-4eaa-ab05-b2f125beafaf","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:27:50.762Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"254728c3-ca7a-4ee7-95d1-d8bf7a15bb52","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":null,"branch":null,"default_branch":null,"status":null,"visibility":null,"error":"pending"},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"2e6cbbda-12d6-4837-8823-d74a29e68ad4","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"161e0945-fca3-40d5-82f1-ecb32442767d","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"a30145bc0fb1a513e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Cvssi6vqGKu2LhgZDWtg1S","type":"tool_result","content":"## Inputs\n\n- **height:** The terrain layer to erode.\n- **debris:** Optional. Receives updates from the simulation's thermal debris.\n- **sediment:** Optional. Receives updates from the simulation's hydro sediment.\n- **flow:** Optional. Receives updates on material flow from hydro erosion.\n- **erodability:** Optional mask that makes parts of the terrain softer or harder.\n- **rainfall:** Optional mask that varies rainfall across the terrain.\n\n## Outputs\n\n- **height:** The eroded terrain.\n- **debris:** Mono layer of material deposited by thermal erosion.\n- **sediment:** Mono layer of material deposited by hydro erosion.\n- **flow:** UV layer showing how eroded material moved during hydro erosion.\n\n## Parameters\n\n**Simulation**\n- **Simulate:** Ties the node to the Playbar so it updates over time. When off, it runs a fixed number of iterations.\n- **Iterations:** (Simulate off) Number of erosion timesteps to run.\n- **Reset Simulation:** Clears the simulation cache. Ctrl-click also returns to the start frame.\n- **Start Frame:** (Simulate on) Playbar frame where the simulation begins.\n- **Substeps:** (Simulate on) Simulation passes per frame. More passes give stronger erosion but take longer.\n\n**Caching**\n- **Cache Simulation:** Stores simulation results. Without it, every new frame recooks from the start.\n- **Cached Frames:** How many frames behind the current frame to keep.\n- **Checkpoint Rate:** Caches every Nth frame so scrubbing only recooks from the nearest checkpoint. Set to 0 to disable.\n\n**Erosion Model**\n- **Feature Size:** Width of erosion features in meters. Larger values give broad channels and slopes; smaller values give sharp detail. The minimum is 3× the input pixel size.\n- **Spread Iterations:** Number of material transport passes per frame. Together with Feature Size, this sets how far material can move in one frame.\n- **Random Seed:** Changes the random distribution of erosion features.\n- **Erodability:** Sets how easily the terrain erodes by water or weathering. The erodability input can vary this spatially.\n\n**Hydro (water erosion)**\n- **Flow Force:** Strength of water erosion. Set to 0 to disable.\n- **Bank Angle:** Steepness of channel walls. Higher values cut deeper and look more jagged.\n- **Rainfall Coverage:** Amount of rain per frame, as a share of the surface. Controls intensity. The rainfall input can vary it.\n- **Slope Influence:** How closely streams follow the terrain slope. High values make winding channels; low values make straighter ones.\n- **Erosion Rate:** How quickly streams pick up material.\n- **Deposition Rate:** How quickly streams drop sediment once they carry more than they can hold.\n- **Removal Rate:** How quickly accumulated sediment breaks down. Higher values reduce deposits and let streams cut deeper, giving a rocky look.\n- **Evaporation Rate:** How quickly a stream's carrying capacity decays. Low values create long-lasting rocky channels. High values create small, soil-like or sandy depressions.\n\n**Thermal (weathering)**\n- **Weathering Force:** Strength of thermal erosion. Set to 0 to disable.\n- **Cut Angle:** Slope angle that thermal erosion cuts down to. Lower angles give flatter results and more erosion.\n- **Repose Angle:** Steepest angle at which loose debris stays put without sliding."}]},"uuid":"0c69cae2-97c1-41c6-aa22-15793bec9d71","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:27:50.800Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"254728c3-ca7a-4ee7-95d1-d8bf7a15bb52","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":null,"branch":null,"default_branch":null,"status":null,"visibility":null,"error":"pending"},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"161e0945-fca3-40d5-82f1-ecb32442767d","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main"}
