{"parentUuid":"156b786c-33fb-4ac6-b793-c9f3b176f01b","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"a30145bc0fb1a513e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0152vjodGoaygt4S1cKWqkBy","type":"tool_result","content":"**Inputs**\n- **normal:** An RGB layer of surface normals, in either Signed or Offset format.\n\n**Outputs**\n- **height:** The reconstructed mono height layer.\n\n**Parameters**\n- **Solver (method):** Chooses how normals are integrated into a height field.\n  - *FFT* is fast and single-pass, but it treats the layer as tiling, so borders are always periodic and cannot be adjusted.\n  - *Poisson* uses an iterative multigrid solver, giving more border control, but it may need more iterations to be accurate.\n- **Iterations (Poisson only):** Sets the number of multigrid V-cycles. More iterations improve accuracy but increase processing time. This parameter has no effect with FFT.\n- **Smoothing Method (Poisson only):** Chooses the relaxation method.\n  - *Gauss-Seidel* converges better and uses less memory.\n  - *Jacobi* avoids checkerboard artifacts in the residuals but needs an extra copy of the layer.\n  - This setting is disabled when either border is set to Periodic.\n- **Normal Type:** Specifies how the input normals are stored. Signed (-1 to 1) matches geometry attributes, and Offset (0 to 1) matches normal maps. Choosing the wrong type will produce incorrect results.\n- **Height Scale:** Multiplies the reconstructed height as a post-process. Higher values make the surface steeper.\n- **Center:** Sets the mean value of the output. The solve only determines height up to an additive constant, so this parameter is needed to position the result.\n- **X and Y Borders (Poisson only):** Each side can be set to:\n  - *Open:* Height is fixed at 0 along that side.\n  - *Closed:* Height flattens toward that side.\n  - *Periodic:* The side wraps to its opposite side. When one side is periodic, the opposite side's setting is ignored."}]},"uuid":"9c9b2a44-a335-4be4-b226-1603ab02cce0","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:28:23.174Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"c825ae1a-e5cb-455e-a345-d5af67e8a476","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":null,"branch":null,"default_branch":null,"status":null,"visibility":null,"error":"pending"},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"156b786c-33fb-4ac6-b793-c9f3b176f01b","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"5019632c-4b25-4c0b-ac03-12ca8c9915c0","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"a30145bc0fb1a513e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XxkjXqtfAcN1TQwqdFPqN8","type":"tool_result","content":"**Inputs**\n- **source:** The layer or VDB that the histogram is built from.\n- **active:** An optional layer or VDB that limits which pixels count. Only pixels where this input is at least 0.5 contribute.\n\n**Outputs**\n- **histogram:** A chart with the same type and size as the source, drawn according to the Mode setting.\n- **counts:** The raw bucket data. Its width equals the number of buckets and its height is 5. The bottom four rows hold the red, green, blue, and alpha counts, and the top row stores the total number of processed pixels in its first column.\n\n**Parameters**\n- **Signature:** The layer type the source accepts.\n- **Mode:** How the histogram is drawn: Colored Bars (a horizontal color ramp), Separate Bars (grayscale bars per channel), or Graph (bar height per channel, overlapping).\n- **Buckets:** How many intervals the value range is split into. More buckets give a more detailed graph.\n- **Minimum / Maximum:** The start and end of the value range.\n- **Outside Values:** How out-of-range pixels are handled. Discard ignores them, while Clamp assigns them to the first or last bucket.\n- **Scale:** Multiplies the bar heights or intensities so small percentages are easier to read.\n- **Channel Scope:** Which of the R, G, B, and A channels the node affects.\n\n**Use of the output**\nThe histogram output is a visual chart that shows how pixel values are distributed across the range, which helps you judge brightness, contrast, and how much data falls outside your chosen window. The counts output stores the same information as raw numbers, so it can be used for analysis. The documentation doesn't describe other downstream uses for it. For changing the range of a grayscale image, the documentation points to the Remap COP instead."}]},"uuid":"0d93effb-9e31-429a-9468-657e1a999c32","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:28:24.031Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"c825ae1a-e5cb-455e-a345-d5af67e8a476","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":null,"branch":null,"default_branch":null,"status":null,"visibility":null,"error":"pending"},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"5019632c-4b25-4c0b-ac03-12ca8c9915c0","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"01e06c5d-edb4-49ef-896c-4b27ed08be55","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"a30145bc0fb1a513e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EKA4JSxkzAcvhY1GQU23Nr","type":"tool_result","content":"**Inputs**\n- **Source:** The layer or VDB to be equalized.\n- **Active:** An optional layer or VDB that defines which pixels count when computing the range. Only pixels brighter than 0.5 are considered.\n- **Mask:** An optional per-pixel map that scales how strongly the effect applies.\n\n**Output**\n- **Equalize:** The adjusted layer or VDB.\n\n**Parameters**\n\n*Mask and channels*\n- **Mask:** Sets how much of the new value blends into the original. The default of 1 uses only the new value.\n- **Toggle RGB:** Turns the Red, Green, and Blue channels on or off together.\n- **Red, Green, Blue, Alpha:** Each selects whether the effect applies to that channel, so you can equalize some channels and leave others untouched.\n\n*Equalize method (stretch or shift or scale)*\n- **Equalize By:** Chooses the overall approach:\n  - **Stretch to Black and White:** Maps the image's darkest and brightest values to the Black and White values, expanding the range. This is the stretch option.\n  - **Move Minimum to Black:** Shifts colors so the darkest value lands on Black.\n  - **Move Maximum to White:** Shifts colors so the brightest value lands on White.\n  - **Move Average to Value:** Shifts colors so the average luminance matches Target Average.\n  - **Scale to Maximum Length:** Scales colors so the brightest luminance matches White.\n- **Fit Method:** Applies only to the shift options (Move Minimum, Move Maximum, Move Average). **Shifting Values** adds the difference to every pixel. **Scaling Values** multiplies every pixel by a ratio.\n\n*Luminance*\n- **Luminance Type:** Selects how brightness is calculated from the color channels. Options include SGI, NTSC, and HDTV weightings, plus Average, Maximum Channel, Minimum Channel, Magnitude/Length, Hue, Saturation, Value, and single-channel extracts (Red, Green, Blue, Alpha).\n\n*Target values*\n- **Black:** The value that black maps to, usually 0.\n- **White:** The value that white maps to, usually 1.\n- **Target Average:** The goal average used by Move Average to Value.\n\n**Percentiles:** The page doesn't mention percentile-based range options. The range is set by the minimum, maximum, or average of the image.\n\n**Note:** The page warns that equalizing per frame can cause flickering in animated or procedural setups when frames have extreme values. A Remap COP with a fixed range is usually the safer choice."}]},"uuid":"0434b8ac-3b21-43b4-b31e-14ab7f508906","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:28:24.099Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"c825ae1a-e5cb-455e-a345-d5af67e8a476","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":null,"branch":null,"default_branch":null,"status":null,"visibility":null,"error":"pending"},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"01e06c5d-edb4-49ef-896c-4b27ed08be55","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"c072d785-662a-42ac-ab41-c76f6d6b2ebd","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"a30145bc0fb1a513e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VMLKtMsKcCJxNr27GtigBk","type":"tool_result","content":"**Parameters**\n\n- **Speed:** Sets the global rate at which values change. A reference speed of 1 produces a plain distance field.\n- **Iterations:** One pass converges for a uniform speed field. Complex speed variations may need more passes so distances can fully propagate.\n- **Tile Size:** Sets the block size used to split the work between serial and parallel processing. It shouldn't change the result once converged, and the defaults should be sufficient.\n\n**Inputs**\n\n- **initial_dist:** The starting distance layer. Values can only decrease, so sources are usually black and other pixels are filled with large values (the page's example is 60000).\n- **speed:** A per-pixel speed layer. Pixels with zero speed block propagation and act as walls, which can be used to build simple mazes.\n\n**Outputs**\n\n- **sdf:** A distance field shaped by the speed values. It is unsigned, so a further step is needed to classify pixels as inside or outside.\n\n**Texture-work uses**\n\nThe page doesn't name specific texture applications, so these are inferences from how the node behaves:\n\n- **Distance from a mask:** Set the mask area to black as sources, fill everything else with a large value, and use a uniform speed of 1. The output then gives each pixel's distance to the nearest mask edge, useful for edge falloff, dirt or wear gradients, and similar effects.\n- **Geodesic distance:** Use the speed layer to make some areas easier or harder to cross, or to block them entirely with zero. Distances then follow paths around obstacles rather than straight lines, which suits effects like grime spreading along channels or paint flowing around features.\n- **Signed results:** If you need inside/outside information, the page points to related nodes such as Mono to SDF and ID to SDF."}]},"uuid":"4fd13190-5930-4547-afbd-3daebe5bc0e0","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:28:24.950Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"c825ae1a-e5cb-455e-a345-d5af67e8a476","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":null,"branch":null,"default_branch":null,"status":null,"visibility":null,"error":"pending"},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"c072d785-662a-42ac-ab41-c76f6d6b2ebd","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"033b04a8-10ba-434d-aece-460622b99b97","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"a30145bc0fb1a513e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018WTceXMsfHiTML2mcPsQun","type":"tool_result","content":"## Inputs\n\n- **height**: The source height layer that the node uses to calculate shadows.\n\n## Outputs\n\n- **shadow**: The resulting layer with shadows applied.\n\n## Parameters\n\n**Space**\n- Sets whether light position, direction, and normal values are read in the image's local space or in world space.\n\n**Height**\n- **Height Map Scale**: Multiplies the height values before shadow occlusion is calculated. Changing it alters how tall features appear, and therefore how far they cast shadows.\n\n**Quality**\n- **Step Scale**: Sets how far apart the samples are along each ray, measured in buffer space. Larger values use fewer, more widely spaced steps, which cook faster but can miss thin features. Smaller values use denser steps for better accuracy at a higher cost.\n\n**Light Source**\n- **Type**: Chooses the light profile: Disk (an area light), Sphere (a light spread over a sphere's surface), or Directional (a light at infinity).\n- **Single Sided**: For Disk lights only, ignores light emitted from the disk's back side.\n- **Radius**: Sets the image-space size of Disk and Sphere lights. The page does not describe a separate softness control, so this is the closest parameter to one.\n- **Angular Size**: Sets the angular radius, in degrees, of a Directional light.\n\n**Disk and Sphere (position)**\n- **Use**: Chooses between Spherical coordinates and Cartesian coordinates for positioning the light.\n- **Distance**: With spherical coordinates, sets how far the light is from the origin.\n- **Altitude**: With spherical coordinates, sets the light's angle above the XY-plane, in degrees.\n- **Azimuth**: With spherical coordinates, sets the light's angle around the XY-plane from the X-axis, in degrees.\n- **Position**: With Cartesian coordinates, sets the light's 3D location.\n- **Light Normal**: Sets the direction the Disk light faces.\n\n**Directional (light direction)**\n- **Use**: Chooses between defining direction with angles (Azimuth/Altitude) or with a 3D direction vector.\n- **Azimuth**: Sets the light direction's angle around the XY-plane from the X-axis, in degrees.\n- **Altitude**: Sets the light direction's angle above the XY-plane, in degrees.\n- **Direction**: Sets the light direction as a 3D vector."}]},"uuid":"a84730e7-57f4-4749-b8f8-bf969d8d0d2d","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:28:25.884Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"c825ae1a-e5cb-455e-a345-d5af67e8a476","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":null,"branch":null,"default_branch":null,"status":null,"visibility":null,"error":"pending"},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"033b04a8-10ba-434d-aece-460622b99b97","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"7a8865e1-22ee-4d1a-9d50-2e927eae5f53","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"a30145bc0fb1a513e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01UdZUK8qPufcpkn12QWKqDu","type":"tool_result","content":"## Inputs\n\n- **size_ref:** Sets the output image size and metadata.\n- **low:** The final mesh that fires rays and receives the baked maps. Its UVs define the rasterization layout.\n- **high:** The detailed source mesh whose geometry is baked onto the low-res mesh.\n- **cage:** An optional mesh that controls ray origins and directions. Used only in Cage Mesh tracing mode.\n- **skew:** A map that applies skew correction to the outputs.\n\n## Outputs and Their Purposes\n\n- **normal:** Tangent-space normals. Used to fake surface detail like bumps and dents in game engines and renderers.\n- **edgenormal:** Rounded-edge tangent-space normals. Simulates bevels and smooth transitions without extra geometry.\n- **worldnormal:** Interpolated world-space normals (Phong shading). Used for relighting, though it can be noisy.\n- **position:** World-position values in RGB. Useful for position-driven effects like dust or weathering.\n- **occlusion:** Ambient occlusion from nearby geometry. Adds realism and darkens corners and crevices.\n- **curvature:** Convexity and concavity. Often used as masks for worn edges or damage.\n- **edge:** Edge detail from convex, concave, or both kinds of edges. Used as a mask for worn edges or colorizing edges.\n- **cavity:** Fine concave detail, such as pores. Unlike occlusion, it doesn't account for interactions between nearby parts.\n- **thickness:** Thick and thin areas, mimicking light passing through a surface (like wax or skin).\n- **height:** Displacement between the low-res and high-res meshes. Often needs remapping to be useful.\n- **alpha:** Silhouette of the object. White where geometry exists, black where it doesn't.\n- **custom:** A cable of user-specified geometry attributes rasterized into maps.\n\n## Key Parameters\n\n**Tracing and rays**\n- **Tracing Mode:** Surface Normal fires rays from the low-res normals. Cage Mesh fires rays from the cage toward the low-res surface, which is more predictable.\n- **Ray Bias (Advanced):** Offsets ray origins along the normal, which can substitute for a cage.\n- **Face Visibility Culling:** Controls whether backfaces block secondary rays, which helps floaters project without casting shadows.\n- **Sample Multiplier:** Scales all sample counts as a global quality control.\n\n**Cage**\n- **Auto Generate Cage:** Creates an internal offset copy of the low-res mesh instead of requiring a cage input.\n- **Cage Offset:** Sets how far the auto cage extends from the low-res normals.\n- **Soften Cage:** Trades projection skew against sharp-edge detail capture.\n\n**Samples and distances**\n- **Per-map Samples** (Occlusion, Curvature, Edge, Cavity, Thickness, Rounded, etc.): The number of rays per map. Higher values reduce noise.\n- **Max Distance:** How far rays search for nearby geometry. Affects occlusion, curvature, and cavity. Smaller values speed up baking and emphasize local detail.\n- **Bias, Curvature Scale, Radius, Cusp Angle, Mode:** Shape the look of curvature, edge, and rounded-normal maps.\n- **Height Space, Inner/Outer Distance:** Control how the height map is remapped to a usable range.\n\n**UVs and geometry setup**\n- **UV Attribute:** The attribute (point or vertex) used for UV unwrapping and rasterization.\n- **Normals and Tangents:** Determine whether missing attributes produce errors, warnings, or are created automatically (MikkT or texture UV tangents).\n- **Match Pieces by Name, Piece Attribute, Include/Exclude Pieces:** Bake separate components in isolation so they don't cast unwanted shadows or intersect each other.\n\n**Output finishing**\n- **Enable UV Boundary Filling:** Fills pixels beyond UV seams to reduce dark edges, especially for MIP-mapping. It's slow, so enable it mainly for final bakes.\n- **Normal Space and Normal Y Direction:** Choose signed or offset encoding and the Y orientation, which must match your target engine.\n- **Mask Out High Resolution Mesh (alpha):** Builds the alpha mask from actual high-res hits instead of the low-res UVs."}]},"uuid":"d53c0617-60e0-493e-a852-dff3f719112d","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:28:27.940Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"c825ae1a-e5cb-455e-a345-d5af67e8a476","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":null,"branch":null,"default_branch":null,"status":null,"visibility":null,"error":"pending"},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"7a8865e1-22ee-4d1a-9d50-2e927eae5f53","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main"}
