{"parentUuid":"bf9197c4-6107-420a-b591-058c4fd44a9d","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"a30145bc0fb1a513e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01E7FF6JaCHR3fDVm6CmxSuW","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Javier Perez ArtStation Substance Designer procedural material breakdown height macro meso micro\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Creating a Modular Sci-Fi Environment Using Substance Designer\",\"url\":\"https://www.artstation.com/learning/series/GD/creating-a-modular-sci-fi-environment\"},{\"title\":\"Javier Perez - Tiki Breakdown\",\"url\":\"https://www.exp-points.com/javier-prerez-tiki-material-substance-designer-qa\"},{\"title\":\"ArtStation Learning: Learn How to Create a Sci-Fi Environment in Substance Designer - ArtStation Magazine\",\"url\":\"https://magazine.artstation.com/2020/03/artstationlearning-msi-environmentsubstancedesigner/\"},{\"title\":\"Introduction to Substance with Javier Perez - ArtStation Magazine\",\"url\":\"https://magazine.artstation.com/2019/08/substance-javier/\"},{\"title\":\"Javier Perez - Substance Designer Enthusiast — Experience points\",\"url\":\"https://www.exp-points.com/substance-designer-enthusiast\"},{\"title\":\"Javier Perez - Lead Material Artist\",\"url\":\"https://www.meshmodeler.com/\"},{\"title\":\"Javier Perez - Lead Material Artist - Portfolio - ArtStation\",\"url\":\"https://meshmodeler.com/projects\"},{\"title\":\"Javier Perez - Lead Material Artist - Artstation Learning\",\"url\":\"https://www.meshmodeler.com/store/yyGe/artstation-learning-scifi-wall-substance-graph\"},{\"title\":\"Javier Perez - MeshModeler\",\"url\":\"https://www.facebook.com/MeshModeler/\"},{\"title\":\"Javier Perez - Lead Material Artist - Substance Designer\",\"url\":\"https://www.meshmodeler.com/projects/W24nVv\"}]\n\nI found Javier Perez's Substance Designer work, but no article that uses the macro/meso/micro framework by name. The closest match is his advice on height maps, which fits that layered approach.\n\n**Height map approach:** In a Tiki breakdown, he calls the height map the most important map, since it drives the other maps you build. In Substance Designer your height map is the most important map to create as it drives the rest of your maps that you create. He also recommends splitting the height map into separate reads, noting that a weak height map hurts the albedo, normal, and roughness maps built from it. If you're half-assed with your height map, your albedo, normal, and roughness will suffer from it. Always make sure you breakdown your height map into different reads.\n\n**Large-to-small ordering:** The clearest match to a macro-to-micro workflow is in a separate interview. He says he always starts with the largest forms and move on to the finer granular details. After the height map, he moves onto his albedo and finally to roughness.\n\n**Related course:** His 6-part ArtStation Learning series with MSI covers a modular sci-fi hallway. He begins by demonstrating the steps to create the height information for each of the 5 pieces that make up the environment. He will then dive into techniques and workflows to efficiently take the height information and create the remaining texture maps. The course page is at artstation.com/learning/series/GD/creating-a-modular-sci-fi-environment.\n\n**Caveat:** The search didn't surface a page that explicitly labels his process as macro, meso, and micro. The \"largest forms first, then finer details\" ordering is the nearest equivalent. For the exact terminology, check his ArtStation Learning courses or his ArtStation profile directly.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"d7b557cc-7d84-4216-b347-65a062c286c5","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:29:01.025Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"c908b4ed-c5e8-4a82-ae36-7d92b0715855","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.clau