{"parentUuid":"95381ccd-8fa1-4190-9c7f-26707553c674","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"a30145bc0fb1a513e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BmGDBFGmhnBPa4RiedFSYN","type":"tool_result","content":"The article doesn't describe the full workflow you asked about. It doesn't give a map order, edge wear or chipping steps, or the nodes you listed (Tile Sampler, Slope Blur, Warp, Histogram Scan, Flood Fill, Non-uniform blur, Height blend). It also names no techniques beyond combining shapes with blend nodes and duplicating a graph. Here is what it does cover:\n\n- **Graph structure:** The Tiki uses four graphs, one per face on the totem. Each face was built by combining multiple shapes with blend nodes. Javier laid out the first face completely, then duplicated its graph to make faces quickly by changing existing shapes and values.\n- **Supporting graphs:** Other graphs include a headband, a bottom-head albedo construction (which he calls \"extremely simple\"), a head-side undulation for more interesting silhouettes, and legs for the body. A wood grain substance was referenced in every graph, and an unused fan pattern was tried for the top head.\n- **Parameters:** He exposed only a few. Uniform colour controls for the faces and the wood grain overlay were the main ones. Other changes meant editing the graph by hand.\n- **Height map advice:** He calls the height map the most important map, since it drives the others. He suggests breaking it into reads, starting with large shapes and moving to fine detail, and avoiding grunges early because they add noise to large forms.\n\n**Other advice from the article:**\n- Put effort into renders and presentation, ideally in Marmoset Toolbag or Unreal Engine 4 rather than plain screengrabs.\n- Material artists coordinate with modellers (how much detail the material can carry) and level designers (using textures to guide the player).\n- Studios often keep shared material libraries for consistency.\n- Ask questions early, since every studio's pipeline differs.\n- Get feedback from peers, tailored to the role being targeted.\n- Material artist portfolios benefit from a range of hard and organic surfaces, and environment-art skills help.\n- Keep portfolios easy to navigate and view, and network with classmates, recruiters, and school contacts."}]},"uuid":"0b8a05ba-57a6-483f-a8c1-e1b927e81b3e","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:29:14.751Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"337b63b6-41db-4cd9-867f-4fd1bfbc0562","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","branch":"main","default_branch":"main","status":{"clean":false,"counts":{"staged":0,"modified":829,"untracked":null,"untracked_normal":5},"porcelain":null,"truncated":false},"visibility":{"origin":{"host":"github.com","remote":"matthieuhuguet/Memory","visibility":"private"},"push_remote":"origin","remotes":[{"name":"origin","host":"github.com","remote":"matthieuhuguet/Memory","visibility":"private"}],"visibility_cache":[{"host":"github.com","remote":"matthieuhuguet/Memory","visibility":"private"}]}},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"95381ccd-8fa1-4190-9c7f-26707553c674","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main"}
