 stone wall graph by Javier Perez (meshmodeler) that uses height, flood fill, gradient, slope blur, or similar nodes. The results describe his work and courses, but none cover that specific graph.\n\nWhat I did find about Javier Perez:\n- He is a senior material artist at PlayStation, and his career spans about eight years in the industry, with credits at studios such as Infinity Ward, Kojima Productions, Intrepid Studios, Sony Online, and Redemption Games.\n- He teaches on Learn Squared. One course, Substance Designer Essentials, covers basics, hard surface, full production, and presentation.\n- His ArtStation Learning series with MSI is a 6-part series on creating a sci-fi hallway in Substance Designer, starting with the height information for the hallway's five pieces.\n- His Substance Source space collection describes 15 fully procedural materials inspired by the space conquest.\n\nThe closest stone-related result is a different artist. Josh Lynch's Gnomon Workshop course, Creating Stone Wall Materials in Substance Designer, covers industry-standard workflows for believable stone walls. It also covers subgraphs and exposed parameters for organizing graphs. This may be the tutorial you're thinking of, but it isn't by Javier Perez.\n\nTo find the specific breakdown, you could check his ArtStation profile (artstation.com/meshmodeler) or Instagram (instagram.com/meshmodeler), which the Learn Squared page links to. You could also search for his ArtStation Learning videos or Learn Squared lessons on stone or masonry materials. If you can find the video or post, I can help you work through the node chain.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"fa2978a4-0cd7-4533-b46b-ec07ea1f4fdc","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:29:39.738Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"9ef9ab1b-897c-4155-9923-1e7eeaa44ed6","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1