ax","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"84ce152d-5ed3-4ad4-9ac5-02a740f23fa8","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"a30145bc0fb1a513e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VQXzqxnsgzXmwfUC2WocE7","type":"tool_result","content":"**What it does:** Slope Blur is a high-quality blur whose direction is set by a grayscale slope map. The node treats that map like a heightmap and smears the image along its slopes, so the blur follows the terrain the map describes. It builds on the Directional Warp logic internally. Because the direction comes from the map, you don't set a separate angle. For the best results, the slope map should have smooth gradients, since sharp, harsh transitions produce poor output.\n\n**Where to find it:** Filters > Blurs. Use **Slope Blur** for Color inputs and **Slope Blur Grayscale** for Grayscale inputs.\n\n**Inputs**\n- **Slope:** The grayscale map that controls blur direction.\n\n**Parameters**\n- **Samples (0–32):** Sets the number of samples. Higher values improve quality but slow processing.\n- **Intensity (0.0–16.0):** Sets the blur strength.\n- **Mode (Blur, Min, Max):** Sets how successive blur passes combine.\n  - **Blur** acts much like a standard Anisotropic Blur.\n  - **Min** erodes existing bright areas.\n  - **Max** spreads bright areas outward.\n\n**Usage notes**\n- **Erosion:** Min mode can wear away edges and details.\n- **Chipping and weathering:** Use the slope map to guide where material wears down.\n- **Edge rounding and smearing:** Blur along slopes to soften edges or drag dirt and rust in the direction the slope suggests."}]},"uuid":"e550bac4-4ab9-4911-9323-4550c625f408","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:31:09.909Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"e8ef772d-8d84-4d9d-bb63-7fcceb91d296","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","branch":"main","default_branch":"main","status":{"clean":false,"counts":{"staged":0,"modified":20,"untracked":null,"untracked_normal":0},"porcelain":null,"truncated":false},"visibility":{"origin":{"host":"github.com","remote":"matthieuhuguet/Memory","visibility":"private"},"push_remote":"origin","remotes":[{"name":"origin","host":"github.com","remote":"matthieuhuguet/Memory","visibility":"private"}],"visibility_cache":[{"host":"github.com","remote":"matthieuhuguet/Memory","visibility":"private"}]}},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"84ce152d-5ed3-4ad4-9ac5-02a740f23fa8","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"a05dfd56-82d3-441e-b0cd-143ec509832d","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"a30145bc0fb1a513e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Wz55AwYSW3sgw6PE7KY9oP","type":"tool_result","content":"**Non-Uniform Blur** is a high-quality blur whose strength varies across the image. A grayscale **Blur Map** input controls how much blur each pixel receives. Brighter areas get more blur, and black areas get none. Optional settings can make the blur directional or biased in one direction.\n\n**Parameters:**\n\n- **Blur Map (input):** A grayscale mask that sets the blur strength for each pixel.\n- **Intensity (0.0 to 50.0):** The maximum blur strength. The Blur Map scales it, so black areas are unaffected whatever this value is.\n- **Anisotropy (0.0 to 1.0):** Adds directional blur. The Angle parameter sets the direction.\n- **Asymmetry (0.0 to 1.0):** Biases the sampling toward one side. The Angle parameter sets the bias direction.\n- **Angle (0.0 to 1.0):** Sets the direction for both Anisotropy and Asymmetry.\n- **Samples (1 to 16):** Sets the number of samples per blade. Higher values improve quality.\n- **Blades (1 to 9):** Sets the number of sampling sectors. Higher values improve quality.\n\n**Usage notes:**\n\n- The doc has no separate \"quality\" setting. Quality comes from Samples and Blades, and the total sample count is Samples multiplied by Blades.\n- Anisotropy and Asymmetry only take effect with a non-zero Angle, since Angle drives both.\n- The doc's example drives the Blur Map with a gradient ramp set at 90 degrees, which shows how a smooth mask produces a gradual blur falloff."}]},"uuid":"3a403b0e-e970-42a7-850e-a1305a436331","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:31:11.059Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"e8ef772d-8d84-4d9d-bb63-7fcceb91d296","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","branch":"main","default_branch":"main","status":{"clean":false,"counts":{"staged":0,"modified":20,"untracked":null,"untracked_normal":0},"porcelain":null,"truncated":false},"visibility":{"origin":{"host":"github.com","remote":"matthieuhuguet/Memory","visibility":"private"},"push_remote":"origin","remotes":[{"name":"origin","host":"github.com","remote":"matthieuhuguet/Memory","visibility":"private"}],"visibility_cache":[{"host":"github.com","remote":"matthieuhuguet/Memory","visibility":"private"}]}},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"a05dfd56-82d3-441e-b0cd-143ec509832d","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"ef4e2e65-5527-441d-a84f-32632d9bb6f2","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"a30145bc0fb1a513e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KN3UnsariZDJ1PyEa2d3A6","type":"tool_result","content":"**Flood Fill Mapper** takes an existing pattern or texture and places a copy into each cell of a Flood Fill, unlike Flood Fill conversions that output solid values or colors. It has a Grayscale version and a Color version. The Color version adds normal map handling.\n\n**Inputs**\n- **Flood Fill Bbox:** the required cell layout from a Flood Fill.\n- **Pattern Inputs 1-8:** the images to place in cells.\n- **Pattern Distribution Map:** an ID map that assigns each cell a pattern.\n- **Scale, Rotation, and Luminance Offset Maps:** per-cell control maps.\n\n**Pattern**\n- **Pattern Input Number:** how many pattern inputs are used.\n- **Pattern Distribution Mode:** chooses patterns randomly, by shape size, or from the distribution map.\n- **Pattern Distribution Jittering:** adds slight variation to pattern assignment.\n\n**Size**\n- **Size Mode:** sets how pattern size is determined, such as relative to the texture, a shape's bounding sphere, the largest or smallest shape, or fitted to the shape's bounding box.\n- **Size:** non-uniform scaling.\n- **Scale:** global uniform scaling.\n- **Scale Map Multiplier:** how strongly the Scale Map applies.\n- **Scale Random:** random variation in scale.\n\n**Rotation**\n- **Rotation:** global rotation for all cells.\n- **Rotation Map Multiplier:** how strongly the Rotation Map applies.\n- **Rotation Random:** random rotation per cell.\n- **Rotation Autoscale:** optionally scales patterns so they still fit within a cell when rotated.\n\n**Position**\n- **Position Offset:** global offset for all cells.\n- **Position Offset Alignment:** whether the offset origin aligns to the pattern cell or the texture.\n- **Position Offset Random:** random per-cell offset.\n\n**Tiling:** Off or Horizontal+Vertical. This is only visible when Size or Scale is below 1.\n\n**Grayscale-only controls**\n- Luminance Range, Luminance Range Random, Luminance Offset, Luminance Offset Random, Luminance Offset Map Multiplier, and Background Color.\n\n**Color-only controls**\n- **Is Normal Map:** treats the pattern as a normal map and corrects tangent-space rotation.\n- **Normal Format:** DirectX or OpenGL, which flips the green channel.\n- HSL Adjustment and Random, Alpha Adjustment and Random, and Background Color.\n\nThe page does not cover angle, distance, random seed, or Gradient and Random Color output modes. It mentions Gradient and Random Grayscale only as separate Flood Fill nodes. For those details, see the Flood Fill node documentation or the linked Flood Fill conversion pages."}]},"uuid":"c8528534-1978-4fa2-afb8-c3a110fdde84","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:31:12.029Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"e8ef772d-8d84-4d9d-bb63-7fcceb91d296","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","branch":"main","default_branch":"main","status":{"clean":false,"counts":{"staged":0,"modified":20,"untracked":null,"untracked_normal":0},"porcelain":null,"truncated":false},"visibility":{"origin":{"host":"github.com","remote":"matthieuhuguet/Memory","visibility":"private"},"push_remote":"origin","remotes":[{"name":"origin","host":"github.com","remote":"matthieuhuguet/Memory","visibility":"private"}],"visibility_cache":[{"host":"github.com","remote":"matthieuhuguet/Memory","visibility":"private"}]}},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"ef4e2e65-5527-441d-a84f-32632d9bb6f2","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"320369ed-7094-4829-9fa7-c67dec7735d9","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"a30145bc0fb1a513e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011MAgCT2mds3Ljr7nDr9X9J","type":"tool_result","content":"Flood Fill is a base effect in Filters > Effects. It doesn't produce a finished texture on its own. It's a starting point that separates the detection work from the styling, so the downstream Flood Fill effects can be more flexible, efficient, and non-destructive. The page says it is \"not meant to be used by itself.\"\n\n**Input requirements:** The input should be a binary black-and-white map, with each tile separated from its neighbors by a border of pure black (0,0,0). The Tile Generator is a good source. Grayscale or sloping values can break the separation, which shows up as a lack of red values in the result and as artifact lines. To fix this, adjust the input's contrast, swap in a different input, or try other Safety/Speed settings.\n\n**Parameters:**\n- **Safety/Speed trade-off:** Sets the calculation mode for your shapes: simple or small shapes, complex or big shapes, or no failure mode. Choosing the right mode gives more accurate results.\n- **Advanced options:** Reveals additional controls.\n- **Override Safety/Speed trade-off (-1 to 100):** Visible only with Advanced options on. It's intended for building custom effects or debugging.\n\n**Outputs:** The page doesn't describe how Flood Fill detects islands or how it encodes output data such as bounding box, position, orientation, or random values. I can't give you those details from this page alone.\n\n**Mapper variants:** The page lists the sibling effects and says they build on Flood Fill's split data, but it doesn't explain how each one uses that data. Based only on their names, the \"to Random Grayscale\" and \"to Random Color\" variants likely assign random values to islands, and \"to BBox Size\" and \"to Position\" likely map island measurements, but the page doesn't confirm this.\n\n**Related pages:**\n- [Flood Fill to Gradient](/en/docs/substance-3d-designer/using/substance-graphs/nodes-reference-for-substance-graphs/node-library/filters/effects/flood-fill-to-gradient)\n- [Flood Fill to Color/Grayscale](/en/docs/substance-3d-designer/using/substance-graphs/nodes-reference-for-substance-graphs/node-library/filters/effects/flood-fill-to-grayscale-color)\n- [Flood Fill to Random Grayscale](/en/docs/substance-3d-designer/using/substance-graphs/nodes-reference-for-substance-graphs/node-library/filters/effects/flood-fill-to-random-grayscale)\n- [Flood Fill to Random Color](/en/docs/substance-3d-designer/using/substance-graphs/nodes-reference-for-substance-graphs/node-library/filters/effects/flood-fill-to-random-color)\n- [Flood Fill to BBox Size](/en/docs/substance-3d-designer/using/substance-graphs/nodes-reference-for-substance-graphs/node-library/filters/effects/flood-fill-to-bbox-size)\n- [Flood Fill to Position](/en/docs/substance-3d-designer/using/substance-graphs/nodes-reference-for-substance-graphs/node-library/filters/effects/flood-fill-to-position)\n- [Flood Fill Mapper](/en/docs/substance-3d-designer/using/substance-graphs/nodes-reference-for-substance-graphs/node-library/filters/effects/flood-fill-mapper)\n- [Flood Fill to Index](/en/docs/substance-3d-designer/using/substance-graphs/nodes-reference-for-substance-graphs/node-library/filters/effects/flood-fill-to-index)"}]},"uuid":"dd701686-fd39-4dee-a0de-6acfb9b8b31d","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:31:15.411Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"e8ef772d-8d84-4d9d-bb63-7fcceb91d296","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","branch":"main","default_branch":"main","status":{"clean":false,"counts":{"staged":0,"modified":20,"untracked":null,"untracked_normal":0},"porcelain":null,"truncated":false},"visibility":{"origin":{"host":"github.com","remote":"matthieuhuguet/Memory","v