{"parentUuid":"f4b9593d-b57a-4767-b101-62553ec2fb3a","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"a30145bc0fb1a513e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012NsVV1KMnW51KFyVaVecAA","type":"tool_result","content":"**Edge Detect** (found under Filters > Effects) analyzes a black-and-white image for areas of contrast and outputs a black-and-white mask that highlights those edges. It's useful whenever you need an edge mask. Because it relies on contrast, it works best with high-contrast inputs, so you may want to adjust contrast before feeding in an image.\n\n**Parameters:**\n\n- **Edge Width (1.0 to 16.0):** Sets how wide the highlighted band is around each detected edge.\n- **Edge Roundness (0.0 to 16.0):** Rounds, blurs, and smooths the generated mask.\n- **Tolerance (0.0 to 1.0):** A threshold factor that controls where edges are detected.\n- **Invert (False/True):** Flips the output so the edge and non-edge areas swap."}]},"uuid":"2f00b6ea-e5d0-4267-8296-1354c74d4136","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:32:20.197Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"60920d0c-2df5-4d9a-ae68-8d68e0c5ca09","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":null,"branch":null,"default_branch":null,"status":null,"visibility":null,"error":"pending"},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"f4b9593d-b57a-4767-b101-62553ec2fb3a","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"aac931f9-7fbe-4957-91db-e5ce9ce999ae","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"a30145bc0fb1a513e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01W4FzeyrQVkohq6LoiE7dLb","type":"tool_result","content":"**Multi-Directional Warp** runs Directional Warp several times in opposite directions, keeping the source texture in place. Where the standard atomic node pushes pixels in only one direction, this version spreads the warp across multiple axes, which reduces the one-sided stretching that single-direction warps often produce. Compared with Non-Uniform Directional Warp, it is simpler to use, but its direction is set only through parameters, not an input map.\n\n**Inputs**\n- **Input:** The base map to warp. It can be grayscale or color.\n- **Intensity Input:** A required grayscale mask that controls how strongly each area is warped.\n\n**Parameters**\n- **Intensity (0.0 to 20.0):** Controls how far pixels are pushed.\n- **Warp Angle (0.0 to 1.0):** Sets the base direction of the warp.\n- **Mode (Average, Max, Min, Chain):** Determines how consecutive passes are blended. It only has an effect when Directions is 2 or 4.\n- **Directions (1, 2, 4):** Sets how many axes the warp uses.\n  - **1:** The warp moves along the Warp Angle and its opposite.\n  - **2:** The warp uses the Warp Angle axis plus the perpendicular axis.\n  - **4:** The warp uses those two axes plus diagonal directions at 45-degree increments.\n\n**Usage notes**\n- Use Mode only with 2 or 4 Directions, since it has no effect with 1.\n- Because the Intensity Input is required, connect a grayscale mask to it before expecting any warp."}]},"uuid":"316c7eea-d769-477e-82e9-21695816bc72","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:32:21.093Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"60920d0c-2df5-4d9a-ae68-8d68e0c5ca09","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":null,"branch":null,"default_branch":null,"status":null,"visibility":null,"error":"pending"},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"aac931f9-7fbe-4957-91db-e5ce9ce999ae","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"366260b3-06cb-4199-9ec5-87c2d28e54cc","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"a30145bc0fb1a513e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NMR2BdBAF8D6PU77gc5uUw","type":"tool_result","content":"The **Warp** node is an Atomic node in Substance 3D Designer. It shifts the pixels of its primary Input image along the slope of a grayscale Gradient input, which deforms the image. Unlike Directional Warp, it pushes pixels uniformly away from white areas, in the direction of the gradient's slope.\n\n**Input connectors**\n- **Input** (grayscale or color, primary): the image to be warped.\n- **Gradient input** (grayscale): its slope determines the warp.\n\n**Parameters**\n- **Intensity** (float): sets the strength of the warp. The page doesn't give a numeric range.\n- **Input filtering mode** (boolean): chooses nearest or bilinear sampling for the Input. The page labels this a boolean but describes two sampling modes.\n\n**Usage notes**\n- The result depends heavily on the Gradient input, so small changes to it can produce large visual differences even at the same Intensity.\n- Adjust the Gradient's contrast, luminance, and scale, along with the Intensity, to tune the effect.\n- Conceptually, it resembles converting the Gradient to a normal map and distorting the Input along those vectors. Vector Warp can achieve a similar result, and Slope Blur offers a related effect.\n- The page's Examples section is marked \"coming soon.\""}]},"uuid":"f8a51aa2-4dc4-406d-952f-a43660584656","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:32:21.389Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"60920d0c-2df5-4d9a-ae68-8d68e0c5ca09","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":null,"branch":null,"default_branch":null,"status":null,"visibility":null,"error":"pending"},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"366260b3-06cb-4199-9ec5-87c2d28e54cc","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"62aa8736-3f50-43f0-aba3-fba0ffa1bc1c","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"a30145bc0fb1a513e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NVgEiWZntpNemBMjFUESxo","type":"tool_result","content":"**Directional Warp** displaces pixels in one chosen direction, with the amount of displacement controlled by an intensity map. This produces deformation. It works like the Warp node but is limited to a single direction. The page presents it as a simple building block for more advanced effects.\n\n**Parameters:**\n\n- **Intensity:** Sets how strong the warp is. The page gives no numeric range.\n- **Warp angle:** Sets the direction of the warp. It is measured in turns, though the page's description is cut off, so the full details are unclear. No numeric range is given.\n- **Input filtering mode:** A boolean that switches how the Input is sampled, either nearest or bilinear.\n- **Intensity map offset:** A float subtracted from the Intensity input's values, so increasing it shifts the map values downward. No numeric range is given.\n\n**Input connectors:**\n\n- **Input** (grayscale or color, primary): The image that gets warped.\n- **Intensity input** (grayscale): Defines how much warping is applied at each point of the Input.\n\n**Usage notes:**\n\n- Use the Intensity input map to control where and how much deformation occurs.\n- For more advanced control, the page points to Slope Blur and Vector Warp as related alternatives.\n- The page includes three example animations, but it doesn't describe them in text."}]},"uuid":"3be5bf3e-2e5d-4182-a6d9-8b0f5a2003f2","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:32:21.603Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"60920d0c-2df5-4d9a-ae68-8d68e0c5ca09","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":null,"branch":null,"default_branch":null,"status":null,"visibility":null,"error":"pending"},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"62aa8736-3f50-43f0-aba3-fba0ffa1bc1c","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"ac17f6cd-3231-473b-bc69-8b7af8e914e8","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"a30145bc0fb1a513e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014vierK791tBHnC26oJHZAA","type":"tool_result","content":"Tile Sampler is a procedural tile-pattern node for man-made patterns. It extends Tile Generator with extra map-driven controls for scale, position, rotation, size, color, and masking, each of which can be blended in separately. Tile Generator is usually sufficient.\n\n**Inputs**\n- **Pattern Inputs 1-6:** Custom pattern images (grayscale or color), used when the pattern type is set to image input.\n- **Scale Map:** Grayscale map that drives tile scaling.\n- **Displacement Map:** Grayscale map that drives offsetting.\n- **Rotation Map:** Grayscale map that drives per-tile rotation.\n- **Vector Map:** Color map that drives non-uniform scaling, displacement, and rotation.\n- **Color Map:** Grayscale or color map that drives per-tile tinting.\n- **Mask Map:** Grayscale map that hides selected tiles.\n- **Pattern Distribution Map:** Grayscale map that chooses among custom pattern inputs.\n- **Background:** Optional grayscale or color image.\n\n**Repetition**\n- **X Amount / Y Amount (0-64):** Horizontal and vertical repeat counts.\n- **Non Square Expansion (on/off):** Compensates for distortion on non-square layouts.\n\n**Pattern Shape and Custom Patterns**\n- **Pattern:** Chooses a built-in shape (such as square, disc, bell, brick, or capsule) or image input.\n- **Pattern Input Number (1-6):** How many custom patterns are available.\n- **Pattern Input Distribution:** Random selection, sequential looping, or driven by the distribution map.\n- **Pattern Input Filtering (Engine v4+):** Bilinear with mipmaps, bilinear, or nearest sampling.\n- **Pattern Specific (0-1)** and **Pattern Specific Random (0-1):** Adjust the shape and its per-tile variation.\n\n**Rotation**\n- **Stepped Rotation:** 0, 90, 180, or 270 degrees.\n- **Rotation (0-1):** Global free rotation.\n- **Rotation Random (0-1):** Per-tile random rotation.\n- **Rotation Map Multiplier (0-1)** and **Vector Map Multiplier (0-1):** Blend in map-driven rotation.\n\n**Symmetry**\n- **Symmetry Random (0-1):** Proportion of tiles randomly mirrored.\n- **Symmetry Random Mode:** Horizontal and vertical, horizontal only, or vertical only.\n\n**Size**\n- **Size Mode:** Normal, keep ratio, absolute, or pixel-based sizing, which differ in whether X/Y amounts affect size.\n- **Size (0-1):** Non-uniform proportions (absolute and pixel modes).\n- **Size Random (0-1):** Per-tile proportion variation.\n\n**Scale**\n- **Scale (0-10):** Global tile scale.\n- **Scale Random (0-1):** Per-tile scale variation.\n- **Scale Map Multiplier (0-1)** and **Scale Vector Map Multiplier (0-1):** Blend in map-driven scaling.\n- **Scale Parametrization Affect:** X and Y, X only, or Y only.\n\n**Position and Offset**\n- **Position Random (0-10):** Random tile placement on both axes.\n- **Offset (0-1)** with **Offset Type:** Staggered (quincunx) or global shifting, horizontal or vertical.\n- **Global Offset (0-1):** Shifts all tiles on one axis.\n- **Displacement Map Intensity (0-1)**, **Displacement Angle (0-1)**, and **Vector Map Displacement (0-1):** Control map-driven displacement and its direction.\n\n**Masking**\n- **Mask Map Threshold (0-1):** Level at which tiles start hiding.\n- **Mask Map Invert:** Reverses the mask map effect.\n- **Mask Map Sampling:** Uses the pattern center or its bounding box.\n- **Mask Random (0-1)** and **Invert Mask:** Random masking that works alongside the mask map.\n\n**Color, Luminance, and Blending**\n- **Blending Mode:** Add/subtract, max, or alpha blend, depending on the variant.\n- **Color / Background Color:** Solid tile and background colors (grayscale or color).\n- **Color/Luminance Random (0-1):** Per-tile color variation.\n- **Color Parametrization Mode:** Chooses what drives color variation, such as the color input, scale, or row/line/pattern index (color variant adds pattern center and size options).\n- **Color Parametrization Multiplier (0-1)** and **Color Parametrization Affect:** RGB and alpha, RGB only, or alpha only.\n- **Global Opacity (0-1):** Overall tile opacity (grayscale variant).\n- **Reverse Rendering Order:** Renders from back to front when enabled."}]},"uuid":"ff80f4a9-9178-45ea-aec3-c33a1ca73c4d","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:32:25.814Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"60920d0c-2df5-4d9a-ae68-8d68e0c5ca09","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":null,"branch":null,"default_branch":null,"status":null,"visibility":null,"error":"pending"},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"ac17f6cd-3231-473b-bc69-8b7af8e914e8","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main"}
