perienceleague.adobe.com/en/docs/substance-3d-designer/using/substance-graphs/nodes-reference-for-substance-graphs/atomic-nodes/directional-warp\"}]\n\nI couldn't find the dedicated Non-Uniform Directional Warp documentation page in this search. The results did include related pages that describe the node indirectly.\n\n- **Multi Directional Warp (HelpX):** This page contrasts the two nodes. Multi-Directional Warp is slightly more limited than Non Uniform Directional Warp, since its direction can only be set through parameters and not through an input map. So the Non-Uniform version takes its direction from an input map.\n- **Standard Directional Warp (Experience League):** This is the atomic node that the non-uniform variant builds on. It warps an input in a user-set direction, scaled by a user-set Intensity map. The page also lists parameters such as Intensity, Warp angle, and Input filtering mode.\n- **Community question (Adobe forums):** A user reported visible \"banding\" artifacts in the Heightmap, Normal Map and AO when using Non Uniform Dir. Warp Grayscale in version 13.1.0. A community manager replied that this seems like the expected behavior if you're pushing the intensity of the node to a high level.\n\nFor the node's own reference page, I'd try the Experience League Substance 3D Designer nodes reference, under Substance graphs > Nodes reference > Atomic nodes, where the Directional warp page sits. The Non-Uniform variant is probably listed in the same section, but I haven't confirmed that. If you want, I can run a narrower search for that page.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"f8e187ff-4c7a-4ed3-aaa6-99738d8b2818","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:32:51.341Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"72e3f164-a376-4274-9c27-23908fe07f86","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":null,"branch":null,"default_branch":null,"status":null,"visibility":null,"er