{"parentUuid":"7dea2f47-dc39-4eab-aa33-5bbe5938d0e4","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"a30145bc0fb1a513e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GqaCvSDLiXyRcHsCma1Wqw","type":"tool_result","content":"The Height Blend node merges two heightmaps according to their height values, so you can combine two detailed height inputs without needing full materials.\n\n- **Height Top and Height Bottom:** The two grayscale heightmaps to combine.\n- **Mask (optional):** A grayscale input that limits where the node's effects apply.\n- **Outputs:** A blended heightmap and a black-and-white mask that you can use in other parts of your graph.\n- **Height Offset:** Shifts the heightmaps along the height axis, which moves where the blend happens. It's the primary control.\n- **Contrast:** Sharpens or softens the transition between the two heights.\n- **Mode:** Chooses between balanced height blending and giving priority to the bottom height. The page doesn't explain how the modes differ in detail.\n- **Opacity:** Controls how strongly the top height shows, fading it in or out.\n\nFor a complete material with all its maps, use Material Height Blend instead."}]},"uuid":"08fa3f68-ed6d-4fb7-95f0-5919a49d8e0b","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:33:00.754Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"62854619-d534-455b-b847-511dc086e115","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":null,"branch":null,"default_branch":null,"status":null,"visibility":null,"error":"pending"},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"7dea2f47-dc39-4eab-aa33-5bbe5938d0e4","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"4d219596-3ef5-4d5d-a2de-8b65377d1c5e","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"a30145bc0fb1a513e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GTHUDc7SoM9rLgQj51rqCu","type":"tool_result","content":"Non-Uniform Directional Warp is an enhanced version of Directional Warp. Its warp strength and direction both come from image inputs, which gives you finer control and can produce distortion similar to Slope Blur. You'll find it under Filters > Effects.\n\nUnlike Multi Directional Warp, which sets direction only through parameters, this node reads the angle from a custom map. That makes curving and trailing effects possible that the other node can't produce.\n\n**Inputs**\n- **Input:** The grayscale base map that gets warped.\n- **Intensity Input:** A required grayscale mask that controls how strongly each area is warped.\n- **Warp Angle Input:** A required grayscale mask that controls the warp direction in each area.\n\n**Parameters**\n- **Intensity (0.0–20.0):** How far pixels are pushed.\n- **Warp Angle (0.0–1.0):** The base direction of the warp.\n- **Warp Angle Input Multiplier (0.0–1.0):** How much the Warp Angle Input map affects the angle. The map interpolates between 0 and this value.\n- **Trail Mode (Min, Max, Average):** How overlapping trails are blended.\n- **Trail Length (0.0–1.0):** How long the trails are.\n- **Trail Fade (0.0–1.0):** How quickly each trail fades out.\n- **Trail Curve (-1.0 to 1.0):** Shapes the fade falloff. It has no effect when Trail Fade is 0.\n\n**Usage notes**\n- Both mask inputs are required and must be grayscale.\n- Setting Warp Angle Input Multiplier to 0 makes the angle map have no effect, so only the Warp Angle parameter sets direction.\n- Trail Curve does nothing unless Trail Fade is above 0."}]},"uuid":"7b5a65bf-5cb6-4d5a-903c-9f76edb03002","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:33:02.383Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"62854619-d534-455b-b847-511d