{"parentUuid":"56e706b9-b04f-4288-a9e9-f54cd8ca4980","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"a30145bc0fb1a513e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FgqTE1WnpwgJfpcZkh2wzj","type":"tool_result","content":"**Workshop overview:** This 4-hour beginner course by Vincent Dérozier teaches how to build a realistic cliff material in Substance Designer. It focuses on keeping highly detailed surfaces easy to read. Realism comes from how large forms, mid-sized shapes, and fine details relate to one another, not from adding more information. Students build a production-ready cliff material, then extend its graph into a set of related variants for games, cinematics, and real-time scenes. The course uses only standard Substance Designer tools. Its topics include large-scale forms, secondary breakup, erosion, fine surface detail, color, roughness, texture balance, and graph optimization.\n\n**Shape hierarchy:** The material is built in layers. Large masses come first, then mid-sized forms and surface logic, then small detail. Rhythm and shape hierarchy determine what the viewer notices first, second, and third.\n\n**Lessons (9 total, 4h 17m):**\n\n1. **Introduction (free), 3m 35s:** The instructor's background, the workshop goals, and reference and render previews that set the visual direction.\n2. **Main Shapes, 30m 36s:** Base shapes, controlled breakups and deformations, and a clear hierarchical structure.\n3. **Mid Shapes & Surfacing, 37m 40s:** Secondary volumes, merging elements, stepping effects, chiseling, mineral character, cracks, and porosity.\n4. **Color, Part 1, 38m 35s:** Starting from a neutral base, using custom noises, gradient maps, temperature variation, mineral deposits, weathering, and patina.\n5. **Color, Part 2, 43m 8s:** Layering frequencies, pigments, leaks, and edge detail driven by height data, with shape hierarchy and rhythm guiding the result.\n6. **Roughness, 15m 51s:** Sediment flow patterns, thickness-driven variation, and embedded stone details.\n7. **Optimization, 30m 33s:** Simplifying node structures and identifying which elements can be cut without losing visual quality.\n8. **Reusability, 41m 18s:** Adapting the base graph to generate alternate heightmaps and surface variations.\n9. **Wrap-Up, 16m 11s:** Final refinements, render analysis, and deterministic workflows in production pipelines.\n\nThe page doesn't name specific Substance Designer nodes. It describes techniques and methods only, such as custom noises, gradient maps, and height-driven edge detail. Lessons 2 through 9 require a subscription."}]},"uuid":"0b385076-e963-4cb0-823b-2709d94b9538","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:33:35.650Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"5c089392-ade0-4c95-89c0-e52e9e8fa96d","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":null,"branch":null,"default_branch":null,"status":null,"visibility":null,"error":"pending"},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"56e706b9-b04f-4288-a9e9-f54cd8ca4980","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main"}
