{"parentUuid":"6bc592be-ccf6-453e-ab4d-0dc84800f53e","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"a30145bc0fb1a513e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EfZsKgNsXxHBTvdeeWRYF6","type":"tool_result","content":"**Artist:** Albert Soto (3D Artist & Material Specialist), interviewed by Kirill Tokarev.\n\nThe article doesn't cover flood fill, histogram scan, edge detect, or erosion nodes, so I've left those out. It also gives few exact parameter values, so the settings below are mostly qualitative.\n\n**Rock material workflow in Substance Designer**\n\n1. **Base shape:** Combine a polygon node with a linear gradient using a minimum-darken blend mode. Feed the result into a random tile node (a tile sampler or splatter can substitute) to generate varied landscape shapes.\n2. **Height detail:** Apply a slope blur, then use a blend node set to subtract, with noise, to carve surface variation. Duplicate this chain to get both soft and rough detail.\n3. **Offset shapes:** Duplicate the shape height, randomize positions, and resize the shapes. For more variation, warp with Perlin noise and add another slope blur.\n4. **Porous surface:** Feed directional noise into a multi-directional warp, adjusting strength and angle so the lines follow the slope of the height. Add a light HQ blur, then subtract the result from the cliff face to create pitted holes.\n5. **Color variation:** Warp the height using micro detail from the directional noise and macro height in the gradient input. Sample colors from a gradient node, then refine with HSL adjustments and masks.\n6. **Pebble overlay:** Scatter pebbles with a tile sampler, using an inverted grayscale mask to control where they gather (high or low points). Use two level nodes, an HQ blur, and two blend nodes. The second blend node uses an add (linear dodge) mode with a dark level, so the pebbles appear to sit on the surface.\n7. **Pebble color:** Clamp a level node on the pebble height to create a mask. Duplicate the tile sampler, plug the mask into its pattern input, adjust its color random setting, and sample a gradient for diffuse variation."}]},"uuid":"1051d54b-2dfa-4875-bf9b-61dab7e65dd9","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:34:08.999Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"0f76b46e-885b-406b-8e4e-05a808631d17","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":null,"branch":null,"default_branch":null,"status":null,"visibility":null,"error":"pending"},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"6bc592be-ccf6-453e-ab4d-0dc84800f53e","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main"}
