{"parentUuid":"619f0a9d-1217-45cc-a90b-1f88adb21e3a","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"a30145bc0fb1a513e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JtL2PbmQsChmr9YiAd71NM","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Substance Designer ceramic tiles glazed height breakdown bevel edge chips crazing 80.lv\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"building a medieval encaustic floor tile material using substance 3d ue5\",\"url\":\"https://80.lv/articles/building-a-medieval-encaustic-floor-tile-material-using-substance-3d-ue5/\"},{\"title\":\"Week 5: Substance Designer\",\"url\":\"https://georgecozensfmp.myblog.arts.ac.uk/?p=120\"},{\"title\":\"Old Stone Paver\",\"url\":\"https://gamesartist.co.uk/old-stone-paver/\"},{\"title\":\"Azulejos Tiles\",\"url\":\"https://gamesartist.co.uk/azulejos-tiles/\"},{\"title\":\"material study in substance designer moroccan tiles\",\"url\":\"https://origin.80.lv/articles/material-study-in-substance-designer-moroccan-tiles\"},{\"title\":\"gamesartist.co.uk\",\"url\":\"https://gamesartist.co.uk/?p=5447\"},{\"title\":\"studying complex materials in substance designer\",\"url\":\"https://80.lv/articles/studying-complex-materials-in-substance-designer\"},{\"title\":\"substance designer free roof tile breakdown\",\"url\":\"https://80.lv/articles/substance-designer-free-roof-tile-breakdown\"},{\"title\":\"Painted Ceramic Tiles\",\"url\":\"https://gamesartist.co.uk/painted-ceramic-tiles/\"}]\n\nI didn't find an 80.lv article that matches your query exactly, meaning a glazed ceramic tile breakdown covering height, bevel edges, chips, and crazing. The closest results cover related parts of the workflow.\n\n**Closest 80.lv match: medieval encaustic tiles.** Alisa Prakapenka's breakdown uses modular subgraphs to represent clean, broken, and damaged tile states and show historical wear and aging. For height, she begins with a Tile Generator to block out the grid, then adds an Edge Detect and a Bevel node to give the tiles some initial shape, bevel out the edges, and create distinct separation lines between them. Her later steps warp the tiles with noise so they look less uniform.\n\n**Other 80.lv result: Moroccan tiles.** Massimo Caggese's study is a procedural material made in Substance Designer and is less focused on the height-edge workflow you asked about.\n\n**Related results outside 80.lv:**\n- **Ceramic tiles by Ludovic Petiot (Adobe):** This is the most relevant for glazed ceramics. He crafted these tiles using Substance Designer, incorporating patterns, and the write-up includes a Moving Tiles node that was introduced to break continuity between tiles.\n- **Azulejos tiles (gamesartist.co.uk):** For edge damage, the author smoothed the tiles a bit and used a slope blur to break the very straight edge. For height range, they clamped the height range from [0;1] to [0;0.8] to leave room for surface details. Their color pass includes various brushing layers and vary the size of the details such as spots, color bleeding, or crazing.\n- **Georgе Cozens' student blog:** For chips, the notes describe Edge_Chip_Macro for bigger chips and Edge_Chip_Micro for smaller ones, with a General_Lighting (Levels Node) controlling how flat or deep the tiles are.\n\nFor a glazed ceramic-specific breakdown, the Azulejos and Painted Ceramic write-ups are probably your best starting points. If you want, I can search for a more targeted query, such as \"glaze crazing Substance Designer tutorial.\"\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"fb2888d7-4245-4724-841a-2af94b0c9f3f","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:34:43.323Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"448362a9-fa0f-4b3a-892f-5a7472281f94","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":null,"branch":null,"default_branch":null,"status":null,"visibility":null,"error":"pending"},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray