Displacement Map, Metallic Map (Metals Only) and Ambient Occlusion Map.\n- **Worn painted metal:** A painted sheet with peeling paint is described as having its paint colour, rust colour, paint blister amount, rust amount, dripping rust length, and scratch amount controlled through the Substance Designer SBSAR file.\n- **Dents and erosion:** A thick steel plate listing mentions a lot of erosion and darken holes and dents, which points to how dent and erosion inputs are usually exposed.\n- **Worn surfaces:** A worn cast iron texture is described as having a lot of chipped peel, which is the kind of edge-wear effect a height-based breakdown would drive.\n\nThe listings only describe what the SBSAR exposes. They don't show the graph, height-map construction, or breakdown method. For that, I'd need a tutorial source. I can run another search aimed at 80.lv articles on Substance Designer metal wear, dents, or height-based breakdowns if you'd like.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"5eeb23c4-2ae8-46fa-9647-6c60b50ab55c","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:34:43.794Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"448362a9-fa0f-4b3a-892f-5a7472281f94","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":null,"branch":null,"default_branch":null,"status":null,"visibility":null,"error":"pending"},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"6fc538f1-8c6e-4c3b-a15d-4bcfc46a2ab1","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"user_permanent","reasonType":"rule"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"5aad4d55-b185-47fd-a385-f2bfb4562a50","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"a30145bc0fb1a513e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QLFQpkpcQ284edWJFCNQRb","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Substance Designer chipped edges technique edge detect noise slope blur min histogram scan stone tile chipping breakdown\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Tips and Storytelling in Substance Designer\",\"url\":\"https://80.lv/articles/001agt-002mrs-tips-and-storytelling-in-substance-designer\"},{\"title\":\"Substance designer - Brick wall feedback — polycount\",\"url\":\"https://polycount.com/discussion/202087/substance-designer-brick-wall-feedback\"},{\"title\":\"Blur Slope\",\"url\":\"https://experienceleague.adobe.com/en/docs/substance-3d-painter/using/effects/filters/standard-filters/blur-slope\"},{\"title\":\"Rosen Kazlachev - Substance Designer - Pixel Edge Detect\",\"url\":\"https://rosko.artstation.com/blog/9rOQ/substance-designer-pixel-edge-detect\"},{\"title\":\"Slope Blur\",\"url\":\"https://helpx.adobe.com/substance-3d-designer/substance-compositing-graphs/nodes-reference-for-substance-compositing-graphs/node-library/filters/blurs/slope-blur.html\"},{\"title\":\"The Edge Loop: Simple but amazing rock chip detail in Substance Designer\",\"url\":\"http://theedgeloop.blogspot.com/2016/12/simple-but-amazing-rock-chip-detail-in.html\"},{\"title\":\"Edge Detect\",\"url\":\"https://helpx.adobe.com/substance-3d-designer/substance-compositing-graphs/nodes-reference-for-substance-compositing-graphs/node-library/filters/effects/edge-detect.html\"},{\"title\":\"Substance Designer Quick Tips\",\"url\":\"https://digitalcolony3d.wordpress.com/2019/07/03/substance-designer-quick-tips/\"},{\"title\":\"Substance Designer\",\"url\":\"https://digitalcolony3d.wordpress.com/category/substance-designer/\"},{\"title\":\"stwshop.gumroad.com\",\"url\":\"https://stwshop.gumroad.com/l/BFNtI\"}]\n\nThe most common approach is to drive a Slope Blur with noise, then subtract or mask the result against your height. Several sources describe variations of this.\n\n**1. Build the chip shape with a noise-driven Slope Blur**\nA Slope Blur set to Min, driven by a noise pattern, is the core of the trick. One tutorial uses a slope blur which is driven by a noise pattern (clouds 2 works very well, but anything would work). The slope blur's Samples attribute is set to 32 and the mode is set to Min. Min mode will \"eat away\" existing areas, which is what carves the chips. Adobe's documentation notes that the node is useful for chipping and weathering edges or smearing and leaking dirt or rust.\n\n**2. Choose the noise carefully**\nOne Polycount user built chips by running a Slope Blur on a \"perlin noise zoom\" node, then amplified the black values with Levels to get deeper cuts, and then masked it with a random tile mask so only some bricks were chipped. Another commercial node offers Mode 1 based on Perlin noise, Mode 2 on Gaussian Noise, Mode 3 on a Tile Sampler, and an option to use the inverted height input.\n\n**3. Keep the slope input smooth**\nAdobe notes the slope input should ideally contain sloping gradients; harsh, sharp transitions will not work well! If your noise has hard steps, blur it first.\n\n**4. Apply the result to the height**\nThe same Polycount user noted that a common approach is to run Slope Blur on some type of noise to help get the right shape, and then Subtract that from your base Height. Then add surface damage on top.\n\n**5. Clean up harsh transitions**\nA Blur followed by a Histogram Scan can soften the sharp edge where a carved shape meets the surface. One breakdown describes blurring the edge and using a Histogram Scan to clean up the harsh edge.\n\n**6. Use Edge Detect for thin edge lines**\nFor a crisp rim or crack line, Edge Detect is the built-in tool. Its parameters are Edge Width, Edge Roundness, and Tolerance. Edge Detect gives every edge the same thickness, so one approach uses a varied pattern input with blur, slope blur, and histogram scan to control the thickness of the edges for a more organic feel.\n\n**7. Soften the damage where it matters**\nIf the chips look too strong, one tip is to blend a slope blur over whatever shape you're blurring, you can decrease the intensity of the edge damage.\n\n**Pitfalls**\nA Slope Blur with plain clouds alone may not give convincing large chunks. One user reported that Slopeblur node with clouds does not chip away big chunks of brick realistically. The fix was the noise-plus-masking approach above.\n\n**Ready-made option for bricks and stones**\nA paid node, STW Edge Wear 2, is made for this job and will help you to create detailed edge wear and surface effects for things like bricks, stones, wood, cracks etc. Its companion Dilation/Erosion graph can help you ti tighten the gaps between bricks and stones after using the edge wear. It was built for Designer 2020.1.2, so check compatibility with your version.\n\n**Note on sources:** The Slope Blur and Edge Detect reference pages on Adobe's site are current. The tutorials and forum posts are several years old, and the Adobe Substance 3D Painter page covers the Painter version of the filter, which has different parameters.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"3fe8894e-508c-403a-a2c8-c43b68cad232","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:34:48.085Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"448362a9-fa0f-4b3a-892f-5a7472281f94","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":null,"branch":null,"default_branch":null,"status":null,"visibility":null,"error":"pending"},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"5aad4d55-b185-47fd-a385-f2bfb4562a50","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"user_permanent","reasonType":"rule"},"userType":"external","entryp