{"parentUuid":"31dc0baa-ebed-4117-805f-19bc71173a66","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"a30145bc0fb1a513e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PfzkyA1fmBZ28CncT7p5ow","type":"tool_result","content":"The post describes only two nodes:\n\n1. **Noise pattern:** Create a noise pattern. The author recommends Clouds 2, though the post says any noise would work.\n2. **Slope blur:** Feed the noise into a slope blur node, which uses it as the driving input. Set Samples to 32 and the mode to Min.\n\nThe page doesn't mention subtracting from height or a histogram scan cleanup step, so I can't paraphrase those. It also doesn't describe the visual result in words. The only illustration is a screenshot, so I can't tell you how the final rock chips look."}]},"uuid":"b4d73f77-59d8-46c3-bdf4-30e40baa8b90","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:35:01.844Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"7ca139c0-6466-424b-b352-504ef5431f5a","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":null,"branch":null,"default_branch":null,"status":null,"visibility":null,"error":"pending"},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"31dc0baa-ebed-4117-805f-19bc71173a66","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"3780520f-ce05-41e0-a591-0b0c72f4ec59","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"a30145bc0fb1a513e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MMkrypqSNfkif4EgCk2uQf","type":"tool_result","content":"**Height map node workflow (paraphrased):**\n\n1. **Base grid:** Start with a **Tile Generator** to block out the tile layout. Add **Edge Detect** and **Bevel** to shape the tiles, soften their edges, and separate them with distinct seam lines.\n2. **Organic warp:** Route **Perlin** and directional noise into **Directional Warp** and **Anisotropic Blur** to bend the tile edges and flatten the surface unevenly, so it looks less computer-generated.\n3. **Masking:** Use **Threshold**, **Non-Uniform Blur**, and gradient generators to isolate areas for micro-detail, surface wear, and subtle elevation changes.\n4. **Recombining:** Layer the warped details, noise, and edge damage back onto the tile structure with **Blend** nodes, adjusting blend modes to get the final base height.\n5. **Subgraph variants:** Feed the finished base height into three separate subgraphs for the clean, broken, and damaged states.\n\n**Note:** The article doesn't describe dedicated chipping or crack nodes, or give specific parameter values for the height graph. It mentions cracks starting at the inlay edge and chipping as conceptual aging effects. The only numeric value given is the texel density, set at 2K per 2 meters."}]},"uuid":"e6dc273e-660e-405f-b1b2-cfcb5fbb4f7d","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:35:04.797Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"7ca139c0-6466-424b-b352-504ef5431f5a","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":null,"branch":null,"default_branch":null,"status":null,"visibility":null,"error":"pending"},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"3780520f-ce05-41e0-a591-0b0c72f4ec59","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"dde4cd0d-e6c3-45ae-b10d-c1029503c877","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"a30145bc0fb1a513e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Tk77ZHW5DCqcJQXmQ2U5Uu","type":"tool_result","content":"The article's height workflow runs in this order:\n\n1. **Base shapes:** A 7×7 paver pattern is built with Tile Generator Grayscale, with slight offset and scale randomness. Two more patterns, 5×9 and 5×5, are added for variety.\n2. **Shape deformation:** Edge Detect bevels the corners, and a Distance node clears small inner bubbles. A Transformation 2D node temporarily doubles resolution during the bevel, then scales back. Slope Blur Grayscale with low-frequency noise adds large-scale edge damage.\n3. **Edge damage:** Two-frequency Clouds2 and a mid-frequency Perlin Noise mask distribute two edge damage patterns.\n4. **Height variation:** Flood Fill to Gradient with Angle Variation tilts tiles, and Flood Fill to Random Grayscale controls elevation and missing tiles.\n5. **Cracks:** The crack graph generates base shapes, then deforms, warps, and chips the edges. A crack mask and a crack-plus-gap mask are prepared for later sections.\n6. **Pebbles and dirt:** Height Blend integrates pebbles, which sink into the dirt and are partly covered by grout. A Levels node can raise some back.\n\nHistogram Scan doesn't appear in the height workflow. The article uses it in the Base Color section to isolate dark values. Also, the article doesn't name a separate warp node for height, so \"warp\" refers to the crack step."}]},"uuid":"8e909150-d9d3-48d0-8697-d7ba5bd6ce02","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:35:05.928Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"7ca139c0-6466-424b-b352-504ef5431f5a","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":null,"branch":null,"default_branch":null,"status":null,"visibility":null,"error":"pending"},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"dde4cd0d-e6c3-45ae-b10d-c1029503c877","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"76d7b0ca-e444-4f74-b8e6-9f15c5a49f65","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"a30145bc0fb1a513e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BY17FGSU7VxAnHPDQFF2U1","type":"tool_result","content":"**Tile height node workflow (paraphrased):**\n\n1. **Tile shapes:** Start with a Guided Scatter node, whose Flood Fill output keeps execution cheap.\n2. **Smoothing:** Soften the tile shapes slightly.\n3. **Edge breakup:** Run a Slope Blur to roughen the perfectly straight tile edges. Grout hides most of this, but it gives later slope work more randomness to build on.\n4. **Height range clamp:** A Levels node compresses the height from 0 to 1 down to 0 to 0.8. This leaves the 0.8 to 1 band free for surface details and slope variation, so later height edits don't break the base.\n5. **Macro detail:** Add large surface variations so each tile looks different.\n6. **Damage:** Use mostly Slope Blur nodes for chipped edges. A Quantize node with a low count adds shard-like steps. Directional nodes at low strength with a direction count of 4 break up the typical slope blur look.\n7. **Crazing:** An nPass graph builds glaze cracks in repeating passes. Each pass cuts cells into smaller ones, using the Pass Index as a seed and rotating the gradient 90° on odd passes. Perlin noise and Quantize add organic variation, and the Density parameter adds more. A Flood Fill to BBox removes tiny fragments, a Distance node closes gaps, and each result feeds the next pass through a Buffer.\n8. **Applying cracks:** The crack output wears the tile pattern, adding fragility to the surface."}]},"uuid":"c4e88869-6f70-4938-8584-fffc86c4f23c","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:35:06.302Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"7ca139c0-6466-424b-b352-504ef5431f5a","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":null,"branch":null,"default_branch":null,"status":null,"visibility":null,"error":"pending"},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"76d7b0ca-e444-4f74-b8e6-9f15c5a49f65","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"6dd72f24-2c5b-4131-91eb-4c48219216bf","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"a30145bc0fb1a513e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01D8kYApRTfS8P5EzF9rGLZV","type":"tool_result","content":"The article doesn't describe a height-specific workflow, so this is a reconstruction from the parts it does cover.\n\n- **Plank layout:** The article doesn't mention a Tile Generator. It uses Flood Fill Mapper nodes (color and grayscale) to lay out three wood types across three plank sizes, then blends the patterns with random masks.\n- **Knots:** A Tile Sampler scatters knot shapes driven by a Bell-shaped input. The base pattern is warped, and a mask blends layered knot detail into the points of deformation. That detail is a Bell shape blended with a Gradient Dynamic, then passed through Slope Blur and Warp. Cracks in the knots use the same Tile Sampler setup, with a crack pattern as the input shape.\n- **Grain:** Grain is built in layers. Perlin noise sets the overall shape, a directional warp adds vertical flow, fine-noise micro-warping adds fibers, and anisotropic noise breaks up the pattern. Thin longitudinal grooves are added separately.\n- **Chipped edges:** Damage is handled in its own graph. Three chip shapes are scattered along splines that follow the plank borders, with one template per plank size. These combine into a unified damage mask, where the size of each crack group can be controlled. The article describes this as a mask, not a height input.\n- **Height blend:** The article doesn't describe how these elements are combined into a height output.\n\nThe article gives no numeric values for any of these nodes."}]},"uuid":"bfb97589-f511-41c3-9fa3-94d22ed0b7ce","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:35:06.796Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"7ca139c0-6466-424b-b352-504ef5431f5a","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":null,"branch":null,"default_branch":null,"status":null,"visibility":null,"error":"pending"},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"6dd72f24-2c5b-4131-91eb-4c48219216bf","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"06ffcb1e-c9ce-41dd-92fd-e218868b9264","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"a30145bc0fb1a513e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HgpxQ21wMcJMc8eKKdAtZ9","type":"tool_result","content":"**Wood workflow:**\n- Start the grain with a lightly blurred Anisotropic Noise, then blend in a softened noise at very low opacity (about 0.06) to add slight wobble. Raising that opacity breaks up the straight lines.\n- Scatter Thorn shapes with Tile Random to create knots. Their falloff adds more rings near each knot's center. Make knot masks with a Highpass and a Histogram Scan.\n- Build growth rings with a Tile Sampler that places randomly mirrored gradients with varied width and brightness. Blur lightly, apply a Directional Warp over the wood base so the rings stretch and squash around the knots, then invert if the result is too dark.\n- For fibers, use Directional Noise 2 through a Non Uniform Blur Grayscale. This keeps more fiber detail than a Directional Blur, which over-smooths. Directional Warp the rings slightly along the grain, and overlay Directional Noise 4 for mid-scale variation.\n- Colorize with Gradient (Dynamic) nodes fed by a palette made from blended Uniform Color nodes. Process the grain and ring colors separately, blend them afterward to reduce noise, and darken knot centers with a multiply using the knot mask.\n\n**Height, edge, and slope blur tips:**\n- **Drips with Non Uniform Blur:** Blur a mask using a Directional Scratches map adjusted with Levels to vary drip length and density. Blend the original mask back with Max (Lighten) to restore the top edge. An optional second blur softens the drips.\n- **Slope-based leaks:** Start with Gaussian Spots 2, isolate a band with a Histogram Scan, and run two Slope Blur Grayscale passes in Max mode, driven by a linear gradient. Two passes lengthen the leaks while limiting sampling artifacts. To hide tiling seams, duplicate the mask offset by 50% vertically, blur it, shift it back, and blend it in while masking the edges. Finish with Levels to sharpen the silhouette.\n- **Moss edges:** Use a very thin Edge Detect, then a Distance node to create a gradient that gives the moss depth. Blur the mask, break up its outline with noise, and adjust contrast with Levels.\n- **Moss blobs:** Apply Slope Blur Grayscale to both inputs of the height map to inflate blobs so they read as separate plants. Then lower the brightest peaks slightly with Levels.\n- **Softening moss height:** Lightly blur the height and blend it back over itself. Apply a Non Uniform Blur to flatten shapes and reduce small bright spots, then merge with the base using Height Blend and Levels."}]},"uuid":"49fca07c-8b9b-4cf1-bc45-df1efe3f7b5f","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:35:07.162Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"7ca139c0-6466-424b-b352-504ef5431f5a","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":null,"branch":null,"default_branch":null,"status":null,"visibility":null,"error":"pending"},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"06ffcb1e-c9ce-41dd-92fd-e218868b9264","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main"}
