{"parentUuid":"38b513fc-9e63-46f3-b390-a9742b5475cf","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"a30145bc0fb1a513e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MqfQ67WQUkAcymBJwqpgeu","type":"tool_result","content":"**Author:** Zahar Scherbov, interviewed by Arti Sergeev (17 September 2019).\n\n**Road height map:** The road runs only on straight sections, mirroring real serpentine roads. A Spacing node (by Alexander Prokopchuk) helped lay out the segments, and a Non-Uniform Blur node improved his workflow.\n\n**Vegetation:** A Tile Sampler node, driven by a mask of the road direction, keeps trees on the slopes between road segments. Grass and other vegetation are separated by height and color so they read as different heights.\n\n**Color and detail:** Shadows added to the Base Color map create extra depth on trees. Road markings are intermittent and appear only on straight sections. Ground, grass, and tree details are also painted into Base Color, and the cars were reworked two or three times.\n\n**Not covered:** The article doesn't mention layering, flood fill, slope blur, edge detect, height blend, or histogram scan nodes, and it gives no specific numeric values."}]},"uuid":"cf4b21e1-ec4a-494d-8b78-5857f61a4b61","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:40:51.267Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"cd026a56-a15a-4d4a-951f-73fb1d9e7cd3","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","branch":"main","default_branch":"main","status":{"clean":false,"counts":{"staged":0,"modified":28,"untracked":null,"untracked_normal":0},"porcelain":null,"truncated":false},"visibility":{"origin":{"host":"github.com","remote":"matthieuhuguet/Memory","visibility":"private"},"push_remote":"origin","remotes":[{"name":"origin","host":"github.com","remote":"matthieuhuguet/Memory","visibility":"private"}],"visibility_cache":[{"host":"github.com","remote":"matthieuhuguet/Memory","visibility":"private"}]}},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"38b513fc-9e63-46f3-b390-a9742b5475cf","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"d08bfdad-831d-4f7a-9509-0001255bf252","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"a30145bc0fb1a513e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01YVC71LhvRVXV1wXSse7jpP","type":"tool_result","content":"According to Francesco Furneri, the rock workflow in Substance Designer runs as follows. The article gives no numeric values for any node, and it doesn't cover cracks or chips.\n\n1. **Base shape:** Start with a simple square and apply a **Slope Blur** to make the edges irregular.\n2. **Shaping:** Apply a **Directional Warp** to push the shape toward a more vertical form. Chain several **2D Transformation** nodes to adjust the shape in the 2D view.\n3. **Edge erosion:** Use blending nodes such as **Subtract/Add**, **Sub**, and **Overlay** to erode the edges.\n4. **Erosion-style pattern:** Start from a **cloud map**, apply a **Directional Warp** to it, and blend the result onto the rock.\n5. **Surface detail:** Apply a **Fractal Sum** noise to add high-frequency detail.\n6. **Histogram Scan:** The article uses this node mainly for masks and for setting brightness and contrast on noise maps, in the cigarette butt graph rather than the rock base.\n7. **Height Blend:** Merge the terrain with the rock elements using a single slider. Furneri applied it lightly so soil partially covers a square rock, which looks natural.\n\nAuthor: Francesco Furneri."}]},"uuid":"9721e285-de01-407c-a412-1dbf8c69db15","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:40:52.372Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"cd026a56-a15a-4d4a-951f-73fb1d9e7cd3","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","branch":"main","default_branch":"main","status":{"clean":false,"counts":{"staged":0,"modified":28,"untracked":null,"untracked_normal":0},"porcelain":null,"truncated":false},"visibility":{"origin":{"host":"github.com","remote":"matthieuhuguet/Memory","visibility":"private"},"push_remote":"origin","remotes":[{"name":"origin","host":"github.com","remote":"matthieuhuguet/Memory","visibility":"private"}],"visibility_cache":[{"host":"github.com","remote":"matthieuhuguet/Memory","visibility":"private"}]}},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"d08bfdad-831d-4f7a-9509-0001255bf252","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"539f3f1f-749f-4b76-8ad4-8cff695c96b4","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"a30145bc0fb1a513e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01UvfHZBeyK3xwDuKo6U6NH5","type":"tool_result","content":"**Height map tips from Charlie Foreman's Substance Designer article**\n\n**Hierarchy of shapes and order of work**\n- Treat height like a 3D blockout. Block the macro shapes first, then add micro detail. Foreman calls height \"the brain of your material.\"\n- Work on Height, Normal, and AO together at the start. The normal and AO help you judge how shapes will read before you have lighting.\n- Plan how shapes will blend before you build them, and keep node counts low so later changes don't force rework down the chain.\n\n**Values and range**\n- Keep a wide range of values. A sloppy or narrow height map becomes hard to work with later.\n- Avoid pure black and pure white, which cause peaking. Use Auto Levels to restore the full histogram when the map looks too grey.\n- Use Max Lighten instead of Add when blending heights, so white values don't stack past their maximum.\n\n**Node usage**\n- **Histogram Scan and Levels:** clamp or adjust value ranges. Because the chain is non-destructive, an early change can shift everything downstream.\n- **Height Blend:** stacks height layers, generates masks, and lets you adjust each layer's height separately.\n- **Tile Sampler:** distributes objects by value. Foreman also uses it to build cell-like patterns with more control than the Cells node.\n- **Edge Detect:** creates grooves and edge patterns, and can combine with Tile Sampler to shape cell patterns.\n- **Directional Warp:** breaks up grunge and cell-based cracks so they don't run in straight, unnatural lines.\n- **Flood Fill:** not covered in this article.\n\n**Chips and edge damage**\n- Blend a slope blur over a shape to control edge damage intensity. This adds only one blend node.\n- Use a bevel to make dust or buildup collect along slopes and edges.\n- Use Histogram Select to create variation, such as differing damage across bricks.\n\n**Other guidance**\n- Textures are cheaper than geometry in engines, so detail should come through the normal map rather than rely on height or tessellation.\n- Avoid overusing Cells and Slope Blur, which can produce inorganic results.\n\n**Artists named in the article**\n- **Charlie Foreman:** Environment Texture Artist and author.\n- **Kirill Tokarev:** interviewer for 80 Level.\n- **Daniel Thiger, Josh Lynch, and Phil Liu:** other artists referenced for talks, pipeline insights, or work."}]},"uuid":"52080ae7-29a5-4bbe-9bfd-cc23363b4c81","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:40:57.156Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"cd026a56-a15a-4d4a-951f-73fb1d9e7cd3","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","branch":"main","default_branch":"main","status":{"clean":false,"counts":{"staged":0,"modified":28,"untracked":null,"untracked_normal":0},"porcelain":null,"truncated":false},"visibility":{"origin":{"host":"github.com","remote":"matthieuhuguet/Memory","visibility":"private"},"push_remote":"origin","remotes":[{"name":"origin","host":"github.com","remote":"matthieuhuguet/Memory","visibility":"private"}],"visibility_cache":[{"host":"github.com","remote":"matthieuhuguet/Memory","visibility":"private"}]}},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"539f3f1f-749f-4b76-8ad4-8cff695c96b4","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main"}
