{"parentUuid":"88b8030c-d74a-4ec8-982e-00af25fcb541","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"a30145bc0fb1a513e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Db4FhPxLwbUKpr77oY4SGX","type":"tool_result","content":"The article doesn't name the nodes you listed (distort, streak blur, tile pattern, scatter shapes, heightfield erode, cellular noise). It describes the workflow in general terms, so I can only paraphrase those parts.\n\n**Workflow (SideFX tutorial series, guided by VFX artist Moeen Sayed):**\n\n- **Rock texture:** Builds faceted shapes with Worley-style noise, adds fine detail with fractal noise, and layers detail passes. It then uses ambient occlusion to drive a color ramp and previews the resulting texture maps as a material on geometry, either in the COP network or on custom geometry.\n- **Dirt texture:** Uses several COP nodes to create tileable, seamless point scatters, which are rasterized into usable textures. Scattered stones are added, introducing instancing concepts.\n- **Grass:** Connects SOPs and COPs so values pass between the two contexts. It covers tiling at the SOP level, adjusting instancing attributes, and using SDF shapes as stamps copied onto points.\n\n**Height and organic detail:** Height and organic qualities come from layering noise (Worley for faceting, fractals for detail), using ambient occlusion for coloring, and scattering and rasterizing points for surface features. The article doesn't describe the heightfield math.\n\n**Parameter hints:** None are given beyond the general concepts above.\n\n**Author:** Amber Rutherford, Senior Editor at 80 Level, published 17 July 2024."}]},"uuid":"cac4dd13-3891-4a3e-a292-eb7cc1ee3e37","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:44:05.107Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"6ceb81cc-1fb7-4e27-8d9e-3389c78b70d5","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","branch":"main","default_branch":"main","status":{"clean":false,"counts":{"staged":0,"modified":20,"untracked":null,"untracked_normal":0},"porcelain":null,"truncated":false},"visibility":{"origin":{"host":"github.com","remote":"matthieuhuguet/Memory","visibility":"private"},"push_remote":"origin","remotes":[{"name":"origin","host":"github.com","remote":"matthieuhuguet/Memory","visibility":"private"}],"visibility_cache":[{"host":"github.com","remote":"matthieuhuguet/Memory","visibility":"private"}]}},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"88b8030c-d74a-4ec8-982e-00af25fcb541","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"3160fadb-f0b6-4591-a11c-a3bfcd605728","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"a30145bc0fb1a513e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KEiiQz4sqKE77po2XdXFvY","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Houdini Copernicus height map procedural material tutorial slope blur \"Distort\" \"Slope Direction\" Substance Designer equivalent erosion stone\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"HOUDINI FOUNDATIONS TERRAIN GENERATION\",\"url\":\"https://media.sidefx.com/files/tutorials/foundations-terrain/hfoundations19_5_06_terrain.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"What’s new Copernicus\",\"url\":\"https://www.sidefx.com/docs/houdini/news/22/copernicus.html\"},{\"title\":\"H22 World Building\",\"url\":\"https://www.sidefx.com/products/whats-new-in-h22/world-building/\"},{\"title\":\"Terrain\",\"url\":\"https://www.sidefx.com/products/houdini/world-building/terrain/\"},{\"title\":\"HeightField Erode 3.0 geometry node\",\"url\":\"https://www.sidefx.com/docs/houdini/nodes/sop/heightfield_erode.html\"},{\"title\":\"Tutorial 1: Make a Volcano with Houdini Heightfields\",\"url\":\"https://processofmotion.com/courses/1657997/lectures/37749133\"},{\"title\":\"Houdini Stone Tiles - Copernicus Tutorial\",\"url\":\"https://voxyde.com/houdini-nuke-tutorials/houdini-stone-tiles-copernicus/\"},{\"title\":\"Copernicus nodes\",\"url\":\"https://www.sidefx.com/docs/houdini/nodes/cop/index.html\"},{\"title\":\"Copernicus heightfields and terrains\",\"url\":\"https://www.sidefx.com/docs/houdini/heightfields_cop/index.html\"},{\"title\":\"Heightfields and terrains\",\"url\":\"https://www.sidefx.com/docs/houdini/heightfields/index.html\"}]\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Slump\",\"url\":\"https://www.sidefx.com/docs/houdini/heightfields_cop/slump.html\"},{\"title\":\"Slope Direction Copernicus node\",\"url\":\"https://www.sidefx.com/docs/houdini/nodes/cop/slopedir.html\"},{\"title\":\"Distort Copernicus node\",\"url\":\"https://www.sidefx.com/docs/houdini/nodes/cop/distort.html\"},{\"title\":\"Height to Normal Copernicus node\",\"url\":\"https://www.sidefx.com/docs/houdini/nodes/cop/heighttonormal.html\"},{\"title\":\"Height to Ambient Occlusion Copernicus node\",\"url\":\"https://www.sidefx.com/docs/houdini/nodes/cop/heighttoambientocclusion.html\"},{\"title\":\"Project Skylark\",\"url\":\"https://vimeo.com/1094515342\"},{\"title\":\"Masking\",\"url\":\"https://www.sidefx.com/docs/houdini/heightfields_cop/masking.html\"},{\"title\":\"Copernicus nodes\",\"url\":\"https://www.sidefx.com/docs/houdini/nodes/cop/index.html\"},{\"title\":\"What’s new Copernicus\",\"url\":\"https://www.sidefx.com/docs/houdini/news/22/copernicus.html\"},{\"title\":\"Cops - Houdini and CG tips\",\"url\":\"https://www.tokeru.com/cgwiki/HoudiniCops.html\"}]\n\nI couldn't find a tutorial that matches your exact workflow, but the Copernicus docs cover the pieces you'd need to build a stone-erosion setup that mirrors a Substance Designer slope blur.\n\n**The core building blocks**\n\n- **Slope Direction:** Converts a height layer into a direction layer. It outputs a UV layer storing the direction of the slope of the height input, with larger values for steeper slopes. Its Angle parameter can rotate that direction; the docs note that setting it to 90 gives directions that avoid going up slope and instead circumnavigate hills.\n- **Distort:** This node can perform the distortion in a fixed direction or by tracing through vectors of an input UV layer. Feeding it the Slope Direction output is the Houdini analogue of a slope-driven blur. The docs also include a Distort By Slope recipe, which puts down a Distort COP and a Slope Direction COP that plugs into the dir input of the Distort COP.\n- **Distort Streak mode:** The Streak option sets how samples combine along the distortion. Blur averages the samples together, which smears the image along the distortion direction. Min accepts the smallest value along the trajectory, which is useful when eroding an input shape. For stone edge wear, Min is worth testing.\n- **Scale and sign:** Distort's Scale controls overall strength, and negative values follow the directions backwards when tracing. Flipping the sign is how you change whether the effect pushes up or down the slope.\n\n**Erosion and debris**\n\n- **HeightField Erode** simulates hydraulic and thermal erosion at a specific scale to create more realistic terrain.\n- **HeightField Slump** simulates loose stones and rocks sliding down a short distance and forming deposits in lower areas. Its Rainfall mode produces flow maps, and the docs show using a flow map from the rainfall to drive distortion. That is probably the closest match to a stone-debris look.\n- **Masks:** The Mask by Feature COP measures the terrain's steepness in degrees for slope, and you can use that to limit where the distortion applies.\n\n**Suggested approach (my synthesis, not from a source)**\n\n1. Start with a height layer, add noise, then terrace or strata for the stone structure.\n2. Build a slope-based mask with Mask by Feature.\n3. Run Slump in Rainfall mode and feed its flow output into Distort as a direction, which gives a stone-debris smear.\n4. Use Slope Direction plus Distort with Streak set to Blur for the slope-blur look, or Min for edge erosion.\n5. Derive normals with Height to Normal and ambient occlusion with Height to Ambient Occlusion for the material.\n\n**Caveats**\n\n- The Copernicus docs say that Copernicus-based heightfields are *not* a replacement for Houdini's traditional SOP-based approach, and the Copernicus version still lacks some nodes and optimizations for processing huge images.\n- Houdini's Slope Direction and Distort nodes don't map one-to-one onto Substance Designer's Slope Blur parameters, so expect to tune strength and direction by eye.\n- For a tutorial closer to your request, search the SideFX content library or the Copernicus terrain recipes. A SideFX \"Learn how to create a heightfield in Copernicus\" tutorial exists, covering terrain layers, heightfield noise, terracing, erosion, and strata details.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"c11e4841-5fa1-4631-9737-2c9d8157b0fd","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:44:08.864Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"6ceb81cc-1fb7-4e27-8d9e-3389c78b70d5","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","branch":"main","default_branch":"main","status":{"clean":false,"counts":{"staged":0,"modified":20,"untracked":null,"untracked_normal":0},"porcelain":null,"truncated":false},"visibility":{"origin":{"host":"github.com","remote":"matthieuhuguet/Memory","visibility":"private"},"push_remote":"origin","remotes":[{"name":"origin","host":"github.com","remote":"matthieuhuguet/Memory","visibility":"private"}],"visibility_cache":[{"host":"github.com","remote":"matthieuhuguet/Memory","visibility":"private"}]}},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"3160fadb-f0b6-4591-a11c-a3bfcd605728","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"user_permanent","reasonType":"rule"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main"}
