{"parentUuid":"b46a2ad6-97a6-419c-8be0-808aabad0b99","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a30145bc0fb1a513e","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CfvdSTJ18Y11DbnNH3jTb","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_011X9xdWQmRjkEWBXgcL2UVk","name":"WebFetch","input":{"url":"https://www.sidefx.com/docs/houdini/heightfields_cop/masking.html","prompt":"Paraphrase the masking page: how HeightField Mask by Feature creates slope/height/curvature/occlusion masks, how to combine masks, and usage tips."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3989,"cache_read_input_tokens":582932,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3989,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_011X9xdWQmRjkEWBXgcL2UVk":{"url":"https://www.sidefx.com/docs/houdini/heightfields_cop/masking.html","prompt":"Paraphrase the masking page: how HeightField Mask by Feature creates slope/height/curvature/occlusion masks, how to combine masks, and usage tips."}},"serverClassifierRequest":"2c88b0c5-3a9f-4d90-9500-d8aad4cd0304","apiBlockIndex":3,"requestId":"req_011CfvdSSti44P2aZ4ara9iZ","requestedModel":"claude-sonnet-5-5","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","attributionSkill":"zenray-houdini","type":"assistant","uuid":"0d8086a5-f86d-4dbd-b138-307575737ebf","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:44:50.279Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"max","perTurnEffort":"max","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"b46a2ad6-97a6-419c-8be0-808aabad0b99","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"a30145bc0fb1a513e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VMPWnMtyZvC9Y51nQ8Pssu","type":"tool_result","content":"The page describes the base terrain setup and noise parameters, but it doesn't mention a **Mono to HeightField** node or workflow, so I can't cover that part.\n\n**Base terrain setup**\n- Create a COP Network. The easiest route is the HeightField COP Network tool on the SOP level, which adds a preconfigured COP Network SOP. You can also use a COP Network at the OBJ, img, or Solaris stage level.\n- Layers default to the XY plane, but terrains are usually built in the horizontal ZX plane. The default 2×2 canvas is too small for large terrains, so the uniform scale is raised (the example uses 500 for a 1 km edge).\n- Work at low resolution while developing, then increase it for the final result. Power-of-two values such as 512 or 2048 are recommended.\n- Add a HeightField node, which is a preconfigured Layer COP with Type Info set to Height. Connect a HeightField Noise COP to it through the `size_ref` input to get a rocky, hilly starting terrain. You can also skip the layer and start with the noise node directly.\n\n**Noise types**\n- Perlin, sparse, and simplex types add fine detail.\n- Cellular types and Alligator suit defining the main landform structure, often refined afterward with detail noise or erosion.\n- White noise gives a blocky, more regular look.\n\n**Key parameters**\n- **Amplitude:** Scales the maximum terrain height. It isn't in meters, so the default of 500 won't produce realistic hills.\n- **Element Size:** Sets the scale of the noise pattern. Small values create spiky surfaces, and large values create smoother ones. It is the inverse of frequency.\n- **Center:** Controls the terrain's vertical position."}]},"uuid":"ddffbec4-3cc3-4c33-8a2a-043a58ac2f67","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:44:55.691Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"2c88b0c5-3a9f-4d90-9500-d8aad4cd0304","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":null,"branch":null,"default_branch":null,"status":null,"visibility":null,"error":"pending"},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"b46a2ad6-97a6-419c-8be0-808aabad0b99","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"0d8086a5-f86d-4dbd-b138-307575737ebf","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"a30145bc0fb1a513e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011X9xdWQmRjkEWBXgcL2UVk","type":"tool_result","content":"The **HeightField Mask by Feature COP** turns terrain properties into mono masks. Connect an existing terrain to its `height` input. Each property has a ramp that remaps its values and controls how soft the mask edges are.\n\n- **Slope:** Measures steepness in degrees. You set a minimum and maximum slope angle to define the mask range.\n- **Height:** Masks terrain between a minimum and maximum elevation. If you don't know the actual values, use \"Compute Range.\"\n- **Curvature:** Identifies peaks and valleys, which you can think of as how round or sharp the terrain is. A max curvature setting controls this.\n- **Direction:** Masks areas facing a chosen horizontal direction. A goal angle sets the direction, and an angle spread widens or narrows the selection.\n- **Occlusion:** Masks nearby terrain that is blocked from view. Its parameters scale the output, and the Quality section improves its appearance.\n\n**Combining masks:** You can enable and disable each mode freely to mix them. For more complex results, chain Blend COPs with different modes such as add, multiply, or soft light. A Mask input on the blend controls the strength of the effect.\n\n**Usage tips:**\n- Masks must be mono. Convert RGB or RGBA images with the RGB to Mono COP.\n- Blur masks to get smooth transitions, and use Invert to flip them.\n- Adjust Contrast to change the balance of light and dark, and use Equalize when the input range is unknown.\n- Combine feature masks with noise or grunge maps for more natural variation.\n- Use Mono to RGB with color ramps to visualize masks, such as separating rock types or showing snow."}]},"uuid":"41c01bee-ed7f-4d62-89ef-604163a5ebc2","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:44:56.223Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"2c88b0c5-3a9f-4d90-9500-d8aad4cd0304","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":null,"branch":null,"default_branch":null,"status":null,"visibility":null,"error":"pending"},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"0d8086a5-f86d-4dbd-b138-307575737ebf","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"45c0b24b-3716-4726-970c-45fa02d672e3","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"a30145bc0fb1a513e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012HfPWM7hWKK4eibBka2bD2","type":"tool_result","content":"**Inputs**\n- **size_ref:** Sets the output image size and supplies metadata.\n- **source:** The layer that gets the fractal effect.\n- **octaves, lacunarity, roughness:** Optional inputs that scale the matching node parameters.\n\n**Output**\n- **fractal:** The resulting fractal layer.\n\n**Core parameters**\n- **Maintain Tileable:** Rounds or clamps some values (lacunarity to integers, angle to 90-degree steps) so the result keeps tiling. The source must already tile.\n- **Max Octaves:** How many layers of the pattern are combined.\n- **Lacunarity:** The frequency multiplier applied at each step, which sets how much smaller each successive copy is.\n- **Roughness:** The amplitude multiplier for each successive octave, which controls how strongly the smaller copies contribute.\n- **Bias:** Sets the midpoint subtracted from each sample, keeping results centered near zero.\n- **Offset with Bias:** Shifts the final output range by the bias value, which helps in Maximum mode.\n- **Blend Mode:** How octaves combine:\n  - *Add* stacks each octave onto the next.\n  - *Subtract* subtracts each octave from the next.\n  - *Maximum* keeps the higher of the two neighboring octaves.\n  - *Over* layers one octave over the next (RGBA input only).\n- **Border:** How sampling behaves beyond the input's edges.\n- **Filter:** The sampling filter. Area filters can reduce noise when the distortion shrinks large regions.\n\n**Randomization parameters**\n- **Seed:** Controls the random values for jitter, angle, and flips. Changing it produces a new pattern.\n- **Jitter:** Random positional offset added to each octave, in image space.\n- **Angle:** Rotation applied to the octaves. Its mode is either *Per Octave* (each octave steps through increments of the angle) or *Random* (each octave gets a random rotation within ±the angle).\n- **Random Flip X / Y:** Randomly mirrors each octave along the X or Y axis.\n\n**Post-processing parameters** (applied in the order listed)\n- **Fold:** Takes the absolute value of negative values.\n- **Complement:** Inverts values (x becomes 1 − x), mainly meaningful for the 0–1 range.\n- **Bias:** Pushes mid-gray values toward black or white.\n- **Gain:** Pulls mid-tones together and pushes darks and lights apart.\n- **Gamma:** Adjusts the overall brightness curve of the noise.\n- **Contrast:** Expands or compresses the tonal range around mid-gray.\n- **Clamp Minimum / Clamp Maximum:** Limits values to a lower and upper threshold.\n\n**How the gaps get filled**\nThe node samples the same source at increasing frequencies. Each octave is a smaller-scale copy of the pattern, and lacunarity controls how quickly the scale shrinks. Roughness sets how much each copy contributes. Blend Mode decides how these copies merge, so the smaller copies fill the spaces between the larger ones and produce a continuous, self-similar pattern. Jitter, angle, and flips make the copies less regular, which gives more organic results. (The page describes this behavior generally and doesn't explain the gap-filling process in detail.)"}]},"uuid":"3d36f4f2-8d61-4689-a18f-7c6cc11f0d27","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:44:58.206Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"2c88b0c5-3a9f-4d90-9500-d8aad4cd0304","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":null,"branch":null,"default_branch":null,"status":null,"visibility":null,"error":"pending"},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"45c0b24b-3716-4726-970c-45fa02d672e3","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9