{"parentUuid":"055a596d-f6d0-4554-b2df-5c9f50435c4c","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"a30145bc0fb1a513e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NA5hRBHfBDTrdYP8qWVjBJ","type":"tool_result","content":"**Inputs**\n- **position:** Position layer from the geometry, which sets where each projection lands.\n- **world normal:** Normal vector field from the geometry, used to blend the three projections.\n- **texture:** A single texture used for all three projections (for Uniform Texture mode).\n- **x_texture, y_texture, z_texture:** Separate textures for the X, Y, and Z projections (for Texture Per Axis mode).\n\n**Output**\n- **triplanar:** A cable carrying both the color and the normal vectors.\n\n**Parameters**\n- **Normal Type:** Tells the node how the input normal is encoded. Signed (-1 to 1) suits geometry normals, and Offset (0 to 1) suits normal maps.\n- **Texture Source:** Chooses between one shared texture (Uniform Texture) or separate textures per axis (Texture Per Axis).\n- **Scale:** Sets the texture scale for all three projections. Smaller values tile the texture more.\n- **Rotate:** Rotates the texture in each projection direction.\n- **Offset:** Shifts the texture in each projection direction.\n- **Per-axis Controls:** Separate Scale, Rotate, and Offset settings for the X, Y, and Z projections.\n- **Blend:** Controls how the X, Y, and Z projections mix. Poorly interpolated ramps can leave visible seams.\n- **X-Z Blend:** Controls the mix between the X and Z projections.\n- **Y Blend:** Controls the mix between the Y projection and the other two.\n- **Filter Scale:** Sets the anti-aliasing filter amount. It defaults to the voxel size and can be adjusted for the UV map.\n- **Filter:** Chooses the sampling method used to read the image.\n\nThe page doesn't list any blend contrast or sharpness parameter. The closest controls are the Blend ramps, which shape the transition between projections."}]},"uuid":"4a846307-3442-4959-beae-9e703b75f026","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:45:14.508Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"a5817914-2a9f-4302-9683-96c1679289cc","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":null,"branch":null,"default_branch":null,"status":null,"visibility":null,"error":"pending"},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"055a596d-f6d0-4554-b2df-5c9f50435c4c","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"195cc9ef-4109-4994-b266-bc54f643401e","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"a30145bc0fb1a513e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AFAuhvDFP4xoKWHB1jZjQX","type":"tool_result","content":"**Inputs**\n- **bg:** The base normal map, the background layer that gets blended onto.\n- **fg:** The detail normal map, the top layer that is blended into the base.\n- **mask:** An optional per-pixel mask that is scaled by the Mask parameter.\n\n**Outputs**\n- **blend:** The result of blending the bg and fg normal maps.\n\n**Parameters**\n- **Mask:** Controls how much of the new value is mixed in. The default of 1 uses only the new value, and lower values mix in less.\n- **Normal Type:** Sets whether the inputs are Signed (-1 to 1, such as geometry normals) or Offset (0 to 1, such as typical normal map files). The output matches this type.\n- **Combine Method:** Sets the blending algorithm, described below.\n\n**Blend methods on this page**\n- **Whiteout:** Adds the x and y components together and multiplies the z components. It is the most thorough of the three methods.\n- **UDN:** Adds the x and y components together but keeps the z component from the base map. It is faster than Whiteout but can lose detail on flatter normals.\n- **Reoriented:** Rotates the detail map onto the base map, preserving the strength of both. It often gives more accurate results but costs more performance.\n\n**Notes**\n- The page does not list \"Detail,\" \"Linear,\" or \"RNM\" as methods. Reoriented is the closest match to what RNM usually refers to, but the page doesn't say they're the same.\n- Both normal maps must be in the same space and the same type (both Signed or both Offset). Use the Convert Normal COP to change types."}]},"uuid":"e28f5746-7ae9-4c36-abde-ba3ac916b0e6","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:45:15.252Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"a5817914-2a9f-4302-9683-96c1679289cc","context":{"git_s