{"parentUuid":"53e1ed31-56aa-495d-945b-9475d5727967","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"a30145bc0fb1a513e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01R1vtN7dV4YQCsQPjFqkQqN","type":"tool_result","content":"**Workflow for the Viking axe (per the 80.lv interview)**\n\n- **Faking geometry:** The axe's 3D look comes from a height map rendered through tessellation in the engine, which Javier says can \"fake geometry with a defined height map.\" He also points to Substance Designer's newer shape extrude as a way to fake geometry. He chose a symmetrical weapon so it would read as extruded when applied to a double-sided plane.\n- **Graph structure:** The piece was split into four graphs: Axe (main), Viking, Swirls, and Shield. Viking and Swirls were separated for neatness and performance. The shield was added last, for composition.\n- **Order of steps:**\n  1. Build the big forms first, starting with the wooden handle, using shape, transform, and directional warp nodes.\n  2. Layer the other components on top of the handle.\n  3. Draw the swirls with a curve-drawing node by Ilya Kuzmichev, which works like a pen tool.\n  4. Build the Viking with height blends, layering face, nose, beard, and hair, using the new Quad Transform node.\n  5. Layer the shield over the Viking at the end.\n- **Height values:** The hardest part was layering elements with correct height values so Marmoset wouldn't over-extrude them. He adjusted the tessellation slider and the levels in Substance Designer until it worked.\n\n**Not covered in the article:** It doesn't mention edge wear. It also doesn't state a \"height first\" rule or a \"macro to micro\" tip by name. The closest match is the approach of blocking out large shapes before adding detail, and the emphasis on getting height values right."}]},"uuid":"1c30e4c0-80b3-481d-b1dc-fb7c19fe17e8","timestamp":"2026-10-11T15:01:03.067Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"df