ud, Sand, Bark, Lava, Volcanic Rock); his ArtStation project pages answered HTTP 403, the NVIDIA tutorial pages hold only titles, and the Gnomon instructor page returned 404 to the fetch tool')\nB('SNIPPET: a Gnomon course \"Texturing and Shading for Games 2\" is credited to him (10 weeks, Substance Designer and Unreal Engine) in search results for https://www.gnomon.edu/academics/individual-courses/course-list/texturing-and-shading-for-games-2 ; the page text itself came back empty')\nB('LOCAL claims to treat with care: Copernicus1.md lines 883 to 1010 attribute to Perez a macro/meso/micro triad, a 512-node reference graph with a top-15 node list, five chain \"signatures\" and a nine-node MainShape recipe, and lines 1202 to 1246 turn them into a 53-node rock recipe; UNVERIFIED as Perez attributions, because none of the web pages read uses the words macro, meso or micro for him, none gives those node counts, and the public README of https://github.com/matthieuhuguet/substance-designer-mcp (read 11 October 2026) says only that its \"pro\" recipes follow professional graph patterns, without naming an artist')\nB('The techniques themselves stay valid: each one is documented independently in the Adobe node pages and in other artists breakdowns read for this part (see B3 and B4), so the local recipes can be used as starting graphs but not quoted as his work')\nS()\n\nH(3, 'B1 Javier Perez: documented height methodology')\nS()\nB('Source: https://www.exp-points.com/substance-designer-enthusiast . SOURCE: he builds the height map first, starting with the largest forms and then the finer granular detail; albedo follows the height, roughness comes last; he argues the height map drives most other maps; he re-tests new Designer nodes after each release because several routes give the same result; he gathers reference in PureRef with varied angles, close-ups for small surface detail and the same material under different lighting')\nB('Source: https://www.exp-points.com/javier-prerez-tiki-material-substance-designer-qa . SOURCE: the height map is the most important map; break it into separate reads; start with large shapes then fine detail; avoid grunges early because they add noise to large forms; the Tiki used four graphs (one per face), one face built fully then duplicated and edited, with only a few exposed parameters (uniform face colors, wood grain overlay)')\nB('Source: https://www.adobe.com/products/substance3d/magazine/javier-perez-takes-you-to-space.html . SOURCE: for the space collection he gathered NASA reference, made a base shape that tiles while keeping early detail light so the result stays unique after tiling, relied on tile generators and tile samplers for repeats while spending most effort on the main hard-surface shape, added detail both before and after the tile sampler, hand-placed unique pieces with Transform 2D, built fabric folds with the Crystals node warped by noise, rounded pipes with Non-Uniform Blur, used a random seed for per-tile fold variation, exposed weathering (dust and dirt in crevices, scratches, element visibility) and pushed height harder on exterior panels because tessellation would be applied')\nB('Source: https://80.lv/articles/forging-viking-axes-in-substance-designer . SOURCE: the axe is a height map rendered with tessellation to fake geometry; four graphs (Axe, Viking, Swirls, Shield); big forms first (handle) with shape, transform and directional warp nodes; components layered on top; the Viking built with height blends layering face, nose, beard and hair; the hardest part was giving layers correct relative heights so the viewer would not over-extrude them, adjusted with the tessellation slider and Levels')\nB('Source: https://www.learnsquared.com/courses/substance-designer-essentials (lesson pages /1?video=2714, /2?video=2740, /3?video=2766). SOURCE lesson order: Lesson 1 teaches Height Values, then node families (Shapes and Tiles, Blends, Transformations, Noises, Flood Fills), then a brick (Creating a Brick, Tiling Our Brick, Adding More Variation, Alternate Options), the Tile Sampler (Tile Sampler, Pattern Examples, Creating Outputs) and Damages (Blend Grout, Extra Grout Options, Cracks); Lesson 2 builds a hard-surface panel (Insets, Tiling Patterns, Changing the Tiling, Creating Cutlines, Referencing Graphs, Damaging Height, Exposing Parameters, Gradient Nodes); Lesson 3 starts from a reference then builds a cobblestone height (Cobblestone, Damaging Cobblestone, Cobblestone Grout, Floor Variations) before sidewalk, cracks, gutter and grating, and only then albedo, roughness and graph optimization')\nB('Source: https://www.meshmodeler.com/pages/nvidia . SOURCE: a 12-part NVIDIA Creator Series on Substance Designer; nine titles are listed (Create a Muddy Dirt Road parts 1 and 2, Vertex Painting in Unreal Engine 4, Creating Mossy Wall Texture, Creating Material Cracks, Create Detailed Materials From Scratch and Height Maps, Creating Tile Trim Textures, Using the Flood Fill Node, Create Stunning Tile Patterns) with no description text')\nB('Source: https://80.lv/articles/creating-cobblestones-and-clover-in-substance-designer . SOURCE (credit by another artist): following Perez River Bed tutorial each moss patch is built separately, the patches are collected into a composition, that composition feeds a Tile Sampler to make a dense carpet, a base adds form and relief, and the moss is blended into the stones like the ground')\nB('Source: https://www.meshmodeler.com/projects/W24nVv . SOURCE: the sci-fi hallway was built entirely in Designer on a single plane mesh with six graphs and rendered in Unreal Engine 4; a 6-part ArtStation Learning series with MSI starts with the height of the five hallway pieces and then derives the other maps (SNIPPET from https://magazine.artstation.com/2020/03/artstationlearning-msi-environmentsubstancedesigner/ , page returned HTTP 403)')\nB('Source: https://www.viewconference.it/article/501/creating-realistic-terrain-in-substance-designer . SOURCE: a VIEW Conference 2026 workshop titled \"Rocks & Forest: Creating Realistic Terrain in Substance Designer\" is attributed to him; the page gives no content, so no technique is claimed from it')\nS()\n\nH(3, 'B2 Macro, meso, micro hierarchy: what the sources actually say')\nS()\nB('Perez (B1): largest forms first, then finer granular detail; height broken into reads; grunge late (SOURCE, exp-points pages above)')\nB('Vincent Derozier, Gnomon cliff workshop (https://mrx.thegnomonworkshop.com/workshops/creating-realistic-cliff-materials-using-substance-designer): SOURCE realism comes from the relation between large forms, mid-sized forms and fine detail rather than from more information; the material is built as large masses first, then mid-sized forms and surface logic, then small detail; rhythm and shape hierarchy decide what the viewer notices first, second and third; lessons run Main Shapes (base shapes, controlled breakups and deformations, hierarchy), Mid Shapes and Surfacing (secondary volumes, merging, stepping, chiseling, mineral character, cracks, porosity), Color, Roughness, Optimization, Reusability (alternate heightmaps)')\nB('Charlie Foreman, 80 Level (https://80.lv/articles/001agt-002mrs-tips-and-storytelling-in-substance-designer): SOURCE treat height like a 3D blockout, macro shapes first then micro detail; call height the brain of the material; work Height, Normal and AO together from the start; keep a wide value range and avoid pure black or white; use Auto Levels to restore the histogram; use Max Lighten instead of Add when blending heights; keep node count low; avoid overusing Cells and Slope Blur')\nB('Karen Stanley, Ubisoft Massive (https://80.lv/articles/studying-complex-materials-in-substance-designer): SOURCE roof tiles start with the main tile shape in the height map, then a profile curve by gradients, tiling and variation, grunge surface detail and edge-detect chipping; her lava goes macro to micro with large base forms, then rolls and cracks, with warp nodes for flow')\nB('Pete Sekula (https://80.lv/articles/modern-ways-to-create-assets-materials-for-games/): SOURCE for rough natural surfaces (bark, cliff, cobblestone) he starts with the height map, for artificial ones (mosaic tile) he starts with the individual tile shapes; he studies the reference, sets noisy detail aside and builds from big forms to small refinements')\nB('Rogelio Olguin (https://80.lv/articles/the-basics-of-substance-designer-with-rogelio-olguin/): SOURCE his Gnomon course sets up the height information first, then designs a cobblestone texture from start to finish')\nB('The exact triad words macro, meso, micro appear only in the local note Copernicus1.md lines 883 to 906 (UNVERIFIED as an external convention); the three-level idea itself is documented above by several artists')\nB('Operational reading for Copernicus, UNVERIFIED: macro = silhouette and spacing blocks (`tilepattern`, `cellpattern`, `sdfshape`, low `fractalnoise`), meso = breakup, tilt, chips, cracks (`distort`, flood-fill chains, `shapescatter`), micro = pores and tooth at low amplitude (`fractalnoise` high frequency, `sharpen` Gain 0); amplitudes must shrink with scale so that the micro read never changes the macro silhouette; the local note suggests 0.05 to 0.15 for the micro blend (Copernicus1.md line 963, LOCAL)')\nS()\nopen(OUT, 'a', encoding='utf-8').write('\\n'.join(L) + '\\n')\nprint(len(L), 'lines appended')\nPY\nwc -l /Users/zenray/.claude/tmp/tmp-zenray-height/dataset/parts/06_painter_perez_pro_techniques.md","description":"Append Part B sections B0 to B2"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":16932,"cache_read_input_tokens":686783,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":16932,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"