-designer/ . SOURCE: tutorial section lists only; Desert Bedrock covers Base Shapes, Erosion and Layering, Cracks, Sand Shape, Dune and Pebbles, then diffuse, sparkles, normals and roughness; Jungle Mud covers macro and micro Shapes, twigs, grass, mud, AO, diffuse, roughness and water; both include a non-directional warp node credited to Evan Liaw and Bradford Smith; the node chains are behind the paid tutorials and are not documented here')\nS()\nH(4, 'B3.2 Josh Lynch')\nB('Source: https://80.lv/articles/substance-day-at-gdc-2018 . SOURCE: GDC 2018 talk \"The Substance of Shadow of War\" while at Red Storm Entertainment, on keeping Middle-Earth environments cohesive with Designer')\nB('Source: search listings of the Gnomon Workshop page https://bmc.thegnomonworkshop.com/workshops/creating-stone-wall-materials-in-substance-designer (page text empty to the fetch tool). SNIPPET: \"Creating Stone Wall Materials in Substance Designer\", intermediate level, 7 lessons, about 2 h 50 min; covers picking reference photographs and a plan of attack, an overview of the stone wall material, out-of-the-box nodes for believable results, and subgraphs with exposed parameters for organized, reusable graphs; no node chain is listed')\nB('SNIPPET (Polycount thread https://polycount.com/discussion/comment/2385140): in a mud sketchbook he updated cracks so they follow the shapes of the raised areas')\nS()\nH(4, 'B3.3 Pete (Peter) Sekula')\nB('Source: https://80.lv/articles/modern-ways-to-create-assets-materials-for-games/ . SOURCE: height first for natural surfaces, tile shapes first for artificial ones; limit the palette (only a few colors do the work); pack reused node chains into single nodes to build a personal toolset; a custom grunge generator blends noise inputs into a unique grunge map; AutoHotkey shortcuts bind nodes to keys')\nB('Source: https://80.lv/articles/creating-cypress-bark-in-substance-designer . SOURCE: custom nodes Linear Grain (directional noise warped by a large and a small Perlin noise to get a wobbly grain), Get Slope (slope map from the height: flat dark, steep bright, to control where detail appears) and Height Selector (isolates pixels of a height by value, to texture only tops or bottoms); the article shows the graph only as images, so the full chain is not documented')\nS()\nH(4, 'B3.4 Bradford Smith')\nB('Source: https://80.lv/articles/tutorial-jungle-mud-in-substance-designer/ . SOURCE: credited together with Evan Liaw as the inspiration of the non-directional warp node used in Thiger tutorials')\nB('Gap: a \"Red Rock Cliff Face | Bradfolio\" page at https://3dreference.notion.site/Red-Rock-Cliffs-6df8172f7767426cbe88b607a8469b3d returned only the word Notion to the fetch tool, so no node chain of his is documented in this file')\nS()\nH(4, 'B3.5 Vincent Derozier')\nB('Source: https://mrx.thegnomonworkshop.com/workshops/creating-realistic-cliff-materials-using-substance-designer . SOURCE: 4 h 17 min, 9 lessons (Introduction, Main Shapes, Mid Shapes and Surfacing, Color Part 1, Color Part 2, Roughness, Optimization, Reusability, Wrap-Up); standard Designer tools only; color is driven partly by height-derived edge detail; Reusability adapts the base graph to alternate heightmaps')\nB('Source: https://80.lv/articles/tutorial-mosaic-creation-techniques-with-substance-designer . SOURCE: a 3 hour Levelup.Digital tutorial on mosaic tile flooring with sections Reference Breakdown, SBS Setup and SVG, Heightmap and Color, Additional Tile Material; the SVG route is for architectural patterns')\nB('Source: https://www.adobe.com/learn/substance-3d-designer/web/vincent-d-rozier-s-procedural-bust-breakdown . SNIPPET: his bust was split into one sub-graph per element to stay focused, with the risk of losing the big picture')\nS()\nH(4, 'B3.6 Rogelio Olguin')\nB('Source: https://80.lv/articles/the-basics-of-substance-designer-with-rogelio-olguin/ . SOURCE: Gnomon Workshop course of about 6 hours; builds height information first, designs a cobblestone texture from start to finish, builds simple tools that combine into one texture with many procedural options, and discusses visual interest; no nodes or chapters are named on the page')\nS()\nH(4, 'B3.7 Charlie Foreman and Mark Foreman (two different artists)')\nB('Source: https://80.lv/articles/001agt-002mrs-tips-and-storytelling-in-substance-designer . SOURCE (Charlie Foreman): Histogram Scan and Levels clamp ranges; Height Blend stacks height layers, generates masks and lets each layer height be adjusted; Tile Sampler distributes objects by value and can build cell-like patterns with more control than Cells; Edge Detect makes grooves and edge patterns; Directional Warp breaks up grunge and cell cracks so they do not run straight; blend a slope blur over a shape to set edge damage intensity; a bevel makes dust collect along slopes and edges; Histogram Select varies damage across bricks; detail should come through the normal rather than relying on height or tessellation')\nB('Source: https://www.adobe.com/learn/substance-3d-designer/web/mark-foreman-s-substance-3d-designer-tips-and-tricks . SOURCE (Mark Foreman, Adobe Learn): the wood, leak, drip and moss chains reused in T08, T10 and T13: Anisotropic Noise lightly blurred for grain, Tile Sampler growth rings, Directional Warp around knots, Directional Noise 2 through Non Uniform Blur Grayscale for fibers, drips by blurring a mask with a Directional Scratches map, slope-based leaks with two Slope Blur Grayscale passes in Max mode, moss edges with thin Edge Detect plus Distance, moss blobs inflated by Slope Blur Grayscale on both height inputs, height softened by Non Uniform Blur then merged with Height Blend')\nS()\nH(4, 'B3.8 Cairo Goodbrand (80 Level, 3 February 2022)')\nB('Source: https://80.lv/articles/designing-nature-inspired-materials-in-substance-3d-designer/ . SOURCE: the Red salt-rock height is built first on a flat gray base: Cells 4 warped for irregular edges, Non-Uniform Edge Detect (a community node by Maxime Guyard-Morin, standard Edge Detect also works) to make a binary mask of pieces with low randomness so segments stay packed, Flood Fill then Flood Fill to Random Gradient with angle variety so pieces tilt inward as if struck, a second Flood Fill to a random gray mask, Histogram Scan to choose which pieces crack further (Balance exposed as a parameter to control secondary versus tertiary cracks), the same setup repeated at each smaller size changing only cell scale with larger breaks more likely, and an optional Vector Warp Greyscale that offsets grunge with a gradient map from the cracks so detail stays inside pieces')\nB('SOURCE: the Blue underwater rocks use a parameterized rock generator blended with Max Lighten; the Round material scatters dot-shaped brush strokes through a Tile Sampler fed by masks')\nS()\nH(4, 'B3.9 Albert Soto, Francesco Furneri, Massimo Caggese, Alisa Prakapenka')\nB('Source: https://80.lv/articles/rocky-environment-in-substance-designer-zbrush/ . SOURCE (Albert Soto): rock base from a polygon blended with a linear gradient in minimum-darken then fed to a random tile node; Slope Blur then Subtract with noise carves variation, the chain duplicated for soft and rough detail; shape heights duplicated, randomly repositioned, resized, warped with Perlin noise and slope-blurred again; porous surface from directional noise through a multi-directional warp whose strength and angle follow the height slope, a light HQ blur, then subtracted from the cliff face; pebbles scattered with a Tile Sampler driven by an inverted grayscale mask, with a second blend in Linear Dodge (Add) at a dark level so pebbles appear seated')\nB('Source: https://blog.turbosquid.com/2019/05/30/designing-details-procedural-rock-details-with-substance-designer/ . SOURCE (Francesco Furneri): a square with Slope Blur for irregular edges, Directional Warp toward a vertical form, chained 2D Transformation nodes, erosion of edges with Subtract/Add/Overlay blends, an erosion-style pattern from a cloud map warped by Directional Warp, Fractal Sum noise for high-frequency detail, and Height Blend applied lightly so soil partly covers the rock')\nB('Source: https://80.lv/articles/material-study-in-substance-designer-moroccan-tiles . SOURCE (Massimo Caggese): primitives combined by add and subtract blends, blurred, tuned with histogram scan, warped or mirrored; a curved ceramic piece with a community CurveTool; neighbouring parts subtracted to set gap thickness; light chipping on the stone parts only; a TileRandomizer node varies color and roughness per tile; global dirt layers last')\nB('Source: https://80.lv/articles/building-a-medieval-encaustic-floor-tile-material-using-substance-3d-ue5/ . SOURCE (Alisa Prakapenka): Tile Generator for the grid, Edge Detect and Bevel for shape and seam lines, Perlin and directional noise through Directional Warp and Anisotropic Blur to bend edges, Threshold, Non-Uniform Blur and gradient generators to isolate micro-detail areas, Blend nodes to recombine, then subgraphs for clean, broken and damaged states')\nS()\nH(4, 'B3.10 Shray Khanna, Vasilina Sirotina, Karen Stanley and the Games Artist breakdowns')\nB('Source: https://80.lv/articles/building-riverstones-bed-in-substance-designer . SOURCE (Shray Khanna): Distance into a randomized Tile Sampler for large boulders, Edge Detect to separate them, Flood Fill with gradient and random brightness for varied angles and heights, Blur HQ to soften toward the ground; a second Tile Sampler places warped, non-uniformly blurred round stones in the gaps; Slope Blur breaks repetition; Height Blend combines layers; a faint Multi-directional Warp adds grain; Quantize Grayscale with Slope Blur adds layering; mud from two cell patterns, slope-blurred Gaussian noise and BnW Spots 2, with an inverted stone mask warped by non-directional warps so mud creeps up; pebbles by Tile Sampler tied to the mud by Height Blend; then moss, leaves, plants, twigs and puddles')\nB('Source: https://80.lv/articles/creating-cobblestones-and-clover-in-substance-designer . SOURCE (Vasilina Sirotina per search listing): stones from Tile Random with Distance, refined by Slope Blur and Mosaic Grayscale plus two mixed noises, a Flood Fill adds height and incline variation, Height Blend joins ground and stones, moss by Perez method, clover leaves placed by Tile Sampler with Luminance Random for overlapping heights, Mask Random to thin them and an inverted stone mask as Mask Map so clover grows only on the ground')\nB('Source: https://gamesartist.co.uk/old-stone-paver/ , https://gamesartist.co.uk/azulejos-tiles/ , https://gamesartist.co.uk/wooden-floor/ , https://gamesartist.co.uk/river-pebbles/ . SOURCE (site author not named on the pages read): paver = Tile Generator 7x7 with offset and scale randomness, Edge Detect bevel, Distance to clear bubbles, Transformation 2D doubling resolution during the bevel, Slope Blur with low-frequency noise for large edge damage, Flood Fill to Gradient with Angle Variation and Flood Fill to Random Grayscale for elevation and missing tiles, Height Blend for pebbles; azulejos = Guided Scatter flood-fill output, Slope Blur on straight edges, Levels compressing 0 to 1 into 0 to 0.8, Quantize low count for shards, directional nodes with 4 directions, an nPass crazing graph; wooden floor = Flood Fill Mapper plank layout, Tile Sampler knots from a Bell input with Gradient Dynamic, Slope Blur and Warp, chips scattered along splines; river pebbles = six Tile Samplers large to small, each masking the already placed stones, merged by Height Blend')\nB('Source: https://80.lv/articles/studying-complex-materials-in-substance-designer . SOURCE (Karen Stanley): the full node graphs are in paid PDFs; the article gives only the height-first order recorded in B2')\nS()\nopen(OUT, 'a', encoding='utf-8').write('\\n'.join(L) + '\\n')\nprint(len(L), 'lines appended')\nPY\nwc -l /Users/zenray/.claude/tmp/tmp-zenray-height/dataset/p