s-1629471 (11 October 2026)\n488\t- SOURCE: an anchor point exposes a layer or mask element in the stack so other layers can reference it, each reference with its own adjustments; it is added from the Effects menu on a layer or on a mask (11 October 2026)\n489\t- SOURCE: references are made from a Fill layer, a Fill effect, or the input of a substance filter of type Effect, Procedural or Generator (for example the Custom Noise, Micro Height and Micro Normal inputs listed in A3 and A4); the referencing item then uses the content of the anchor (11 October 2026)\n490\t- SOURCE: only anchors below the referencing layer can be used, moving an anchor above it breaks the reference (undo restores it), references cannot cross texture sets, the anchor properties list who references it and a referencing item can jump to its anchor (11 October 2026)\n491\t- SOURCE (API page): a reference points to an anchor point effect node; in a multi-channel context `channel_mapping` says which anchor channel feeds each destination channel (the example maps Roughness to Height), in a single-channel context `referenced_channel` picks one channel and can be set only when the reference is single-channel and the anchor is multi-channel; `alpha_matte` keeps the alpha, extracts it, or mattes it against a default background; levels can be read and written per reference (11 October 2026)\n492\t- Propagation, UNVERIFIED reading: because a reference reads the anchor content, an edit to the anchored layer, mask or its effects below should reach every reference; the pages do not state this sentence explicitly (11 October 2026)\n493\t- SNIPPET: community advice puts anchors at the bottom of the layer stack; a June 2026 community report says that after 12.0.3 to 12.1.0 an anchor created from a mask could be reused only inside another mask, while anchors on Fill or Paint layers still worked in masks and material channels (11 October 2026)\n494\t- COP, OFFICIAL-MAP: Dot (Portal) node = `null` plus `fetch`; 