{"parentUuid":"2de73179-0ab9-4373-9cca-520b39e32788","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"a30145bc0fb1a513e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BTTwBSett5H3gSwk4rFPv5","type":"tool_result","content":"616\t\n617\t#### B3.2 Josh Lynch (11 October 2026)\n618\t- Source: https://80.lv/articles/substance-day-at-gdc-2018 . SOURCE: GDC 2018 talk \"The Substance of Shadow of War\" while at Red Storm Entertainment, on keeping Middle-Earth environments cohesive with Designer (11 October 2026)\n619\t- Source: search listings of the Gnomon Workshop page https://bmc.thegnomonworkshop.com/workshops/creating-stone-wall-materials-in-substance-designer (page text empty to the fetch tool). SNIPPET: \"Creating Stone Wall Materials in Substance Designer\", intermediate level, 7 lessons, about 2 h 50 min; covers picking reference photographs and a plan of attack, an overview of the stone wall material, out-of-the-box nodes for believable results, and subgraphs with exposed parameters for organized, reusable graphs; no node chain is listed (11 October 2026)\n620\t- SNIPPET (Polycount thread https://polycount.com/discussion/comment/2385140): in a mud sketchbook he updated cracks so they follow the shapes of the raised areas (11 October 2026)\n621\t\n622\t#### B3.3 Pete (Peter) Sekula (11 October 2026)\n623\t- Source: https://80.lv/articles/modern-ways-to-create-assets-materials-for-games/ . SOURCE: height first for natural surfaces, tile shapes first for artificial ones; limit the palette (only a few colors do the work); pack reused node chains into single nodes to build a personal toolset; a custom grunge generator blends noise inputs into a unique grunge map; AutoHotkey shortcuts bind nodes to keys (11 October 2026)\n624\t- Source: https://80.lv/articles/creating-cypress-bark-in-substance-designer . SOURCE: custom nodes Linear Grain (directional noise warped by a large and a small Perlin noise to get a wobbly grain), Get Slope (slope map from the height: flat dark, steep bright, to control where detail appears) and Height Selector (isolates pixels of a height by value, to texture only tops or bottoms); the article shows the graph only as images, so the full chain is not documented (11 October 2026)\n625\t\n626\t#### B3.4 Bradford Smith (11 October 2026)\n627\t- Source: https://80.lv/articles/tutorial-jungle-mud-in-substance-designer/ . SOURCE: credited together with Evan Liaw as the inspiration of the non-directional warp node used in Thiger tutorials (11 October 2026)\n628\t- Gap: a \"Red Rock Cliff Face | Bradfolio\" page at https://3dreference.notion.site/Red-Rock-Cliffs-6df8172f7767426cbe88b607a8469b3d returned only the word Notion to the fetch tool, so no node chain of his is documented in this file (11 October 2026)\n629\t\n630\t#### B3.5 Vincent Derozier (11 October 2026)\n631\t- Source: https://mrx.thegnomonworkshop.com/workshops/creating-realistic-cliff-materials-using-substance-designer . SOURCE: 4 h 17 min, 9 lessons (Introduction, Main Shapes, Mid Shapes and Surfacing, Color Part 1, Color Part 2, Roughness, Optimization, Reusability, Wrap-Up); standard Designer tools only; color is driven partly by height-derived edge detail; Reusability adapts the base graph to alternate heightmaps (11 October 2026)\n632\t- Source: https://80.lv/articles/tutorial-mosaic-creation-techniques-with-substance-designer . SOURCE: a 3 hour Levelup.Digital tutorial on mosaic tile flooring with sections Reference Breakdown, SBS Setup and SVG, Heightmap and Color, Additional Tile Material; the SVG route is for architectural patterns (11 October 2026)\n633\t- Source: https://www.adobe.com/learn/substance-3d-designer/web/vincent-d-rozier-s-procedural-bust-breakdown . SNIPPET: his bust was split into one sub-graph per element to stay focused, with the risk of losing the big picture (11 October 2026)\n634\t\n635\t#### B3.6 Rogelio Olguin (11 October 2026)\n636\t- Source: https://80.lv/articles/the-basics-of-substance-designer-with-rogelio-olguin/ . SOURCE: Gnomon Workshop course of about 6 hours; builds height information first, designs a cobblestone texture from start to finish, builds simple tools that combine into one texture with many procedural options, and discusses visual interest; no nodes or chapters are named on the page (11 October 2026)\n637\t\n638\t#### B3.7 Charlie Foreman and Mark Foreman (two different artists) (11 October 2026)\n639\t- Source: https://80.lv/articles/001agt-002mrs-tips-and-storytelling-in-substance-designer . SOURCE (Charlie Foreman): Histogram Scan and Levels clamp ranges; Height Blend stacks height layers, generates masks and lets each layer height be adjusted; Tile Sampler distributes objects by value and can build cell-like patterns with more control than Cells; Edge Detect makes grooves and edge patterns; Directional Warp breaks up grunge and cell cracks so they do not run straight; blend a slope blur over a shape to set edge damage intensity; a bevel makes dust collect along slopes and edges; Histogram Select varies damage across bricks; detail should come through the normal rather than relying on height or tessellation (11 October 2026)\n640\t- Source: https://www.adobe.com/learn/substance-3d-designer/web/mark-foreman-s-substance-3d-designer-tips-and-tricks . SOURCE (Mark Foreman, Adobe Learn): the wood, leak, drip and moss chains reused in T08, T10 and T13: Anisotropic Noise lightly blurred for grain, Tile Sampler growth rings, Directional Warp around knots, Directional Noise 2 through Non Uniform Blur Grayscale for fibers, drips by blurring a mask with a Directional Scratches map, slope-based leaks with two Slope Blur Grayscale passes in Max mode, moss edges with thin Edge Detect plus Distance, moss blobs inflated by Slope Blur Grayscale on both height inputs, height softened by Non Uniform Blur then merged with Height Blend (11 October 2026)\n641\t\n642\t#### B3.8 Cairo Goodbrand (80 Level, 3 February 2022) (11 October 2026)\n643\t- Source: https://80.lv/articles/designing-nature-inspired-materials-in-substance-3d-designer/ . SOURCE: the Red salt-rock height is built first on a flat gray base: Cells 4 warped for irregular edges, Non-Uniform Edge Detect (a community node by Maxime Guyard-Morin, standard Edge Detect also works) to make a binary mask of pieces with low randomness so segments stay packed, Flood Fill then Flood Fill to Random Gradient with angle variety so pieces tilt inward as if struck, a second Flood Fill to a random gray mask, Histogram Scan to choose which pieces crack further (Balance exposed as a parameter to control secondary versus tertiary cracks), the same setup repeated at each smaller size changing only cell scale with larger breaks more likely, and an optional Vector Warp Greyscale that offsets grunge with a gradient map from the cracks so detail stays inside pieces (11 October 2026)\n644\t- SOURCE: the Blue underwater rocks use a parameterized rock generator blended with Max Lighten; the Round material scatters dot-shaped brush strokes through a Tile Sampler fed by masks (11 October 2026)\n645\t\n646\t#### B3.9 Albert Soto, Francesco Furneri, Massimo Caggese, Alisa Prakapenka (11 October 2026)\n647\t- Source: https://80.lv/articles/rocky-environment-in-substance-designer-zbrush/ . SOURCE (Albert Soto): rock base from a polygon blended with a linear gradient in minimum-darken then fed to a random tile node; Slope Blur then Subtract with noise carves variation, the chain duplicated for soft and rough detail; shape heights duplicated, randomly repositioned, resized, warped with Perlin noise and slope-blurred again; porous surface from directional noise through a multi-directional warp whose strength and angle follow the height slope, a light HQ blur, then subtracted from the cliff face; pebbles scattered with a Tile Sampler driven by an inverted grayscale mask, with a second blend in Linear Dodge (Add) at a dark level so pebbles appear seated (11 October 2026)\n648\t- Source: https://blog.turbosquid.com/2019/05/30/designing-details-procedural-rock-details-with-substance-designer/ . SOURCE (Francesco Furneri): a square with Slope Blur for irregular edges, Directional Warp toward a vertical form, chained 2D Transformation nodes, erosion of edges with Subtract/Add/Overlay blends, an erosion-style pattern from a cloud map warped by Directional Warp, Fractal Sum noise for high-frequency detail, and Height Blend applied lightly so soil partly covers the rock (11 October 2026)\n649\t- Source: https://80.lv/articles/material-study-in-substance-designer-moroccan-tiles . SOURCE (Massimo Caggese): primitives combined by add and subtract blends, blurred, tuned with histogram scan, warped or mirrored; a curved ceramic piece with a community CurveTool; neighbouring parts subtracted to set gap thickness; light chipping on the stone parts only; a TileRandomizer node varies color and roughness per tile; global dirt layers last (11 October 2026)\n650\t- Source: https://80.lv/articles/building-a-medieval-encaustic-floor-tile-material-using-substance-3d-ue5/ . SOURCE (Alisa Prakapenka): Tile Generator for the grid, Edge Detect and Bevel for shape and seam lines, Perlin and directional noise through Directional Warp and Anisotropic Blur to bend edges, Threshold, Non-Uniform Blur and gradient generators to isolate micro-detail areas, Blend nodes to recombine, then subgraphs for clean, broken and damaged states (11 October 2026)\n651\t\n652\t#### B3.10 Shray Khanna, Vasilina Sirotina, Karen Stanley and the Games Artist breakdowns (11 October 2026)\n653\t- Source: https://80.lv/articles/building-riverstones-bed-in-substance-designer . SOURCE (Shray Khanna): Distance into a randomized Tile Sampler for large boulders, Edge Detect to separate them, Flood Fill with gradient and random brightness for varied angles and heights, Blur HQ to soften toward the ground; a second Tile Sampler places warped, non-uniformly blurred round stones in the gaps; Slope Blur breaks repetition; Height Blend combines layers; a faint Multi-directional Warp adds grain; Quantize Grayscale with Slope Blur adds layering; mud from two cell patterns, slope-blurred Gaussian noise and BnW Spots 2, with an inverted stone mask warped by non-directional warps so mud creeps up; pebbles by Tile Sampler tied to the mud by Height Blend; then moss, leaves, plants, twigs and puddles (11 October 2026)\n654\t- Source: https://80.lv/articles/creating-cobblestones-and-clover-in-substance-designer . SOURCE (Vasilina Sirotina per search listing): stones from Tile Random with Distance, refined by Slope Blur and Mosaic Grayscale plus two mixed noises, a Flood Fill adds height and incline variation, Height Blend joins ground and stones, moss by Perez method, clover leaves placed by Tile Sampler with Luminance Random for overlapping heights, Mask Random to thin them and an inverted stone mask as Mask Map so clover grows only on the ground (11 October 2026)\n655\t- Source: https://gamesartist.co.uk/old-stone-paver/ , https://gamesartist.co.uk/azulejos-tiles/ , https://gamesartist.co.uk/wooden-floor/ , https://gamesartist.co.uk/river-pebbles/ . SOURCE (site author not named on the pages read): paver = Tile Generator 7x7 with offset and scale randomness, Edge Detect bevel, Distance to clear bubbles, Transformation 2D doubling resolution during the bevel, Slope Blur with low-frequency noise for large edge damage, Flood Fill to Gradient with Angle Variation and Flood Fill to Random Grayscale for elevation and missing tiles, Height Blend for pebbles; azulejos = Guided Scatter flood-fill output, Slope Blur on straight edges, Levels compressing 0 to 1 into 0 to 0.8, Quantize low count for shards, directional nodes with 4 directions, an nPass crazing graph; wooden floor = Flood Fill Mapper plank layout, Tile Sampler knots from a Bell input with Gradient Dynamic, Slope Blur and Warp, chips scattered along splines; river pebbles = six Tile Samplers large to small, each masking the already placed stones, merged by Height Blend (11 October 2026)\n656\t- Source: https://80.lv/articles/studying-complex-materials-in-substance-designer . SOURCE (Karen Stanley): the full node graphs are in paid PDFs; the article gives only the height-first order recorded in B2 (11 October 2026)\n657\t\n658\t### B4 Technique cards: problem, Designer chain, Copernicus chain, parameter hints, visual signature (11 October 2026)\n659\t\n660\tCard format: Problem solved; Designer chain and documented uses (SOURCE with URL); Copernicus chain (OFFICIAL-MAP where SideFX states it, otherwise UNVERIFIED built from verified node pages); parameter hints; visual signature (UNVERIFIED); local notes. Official mapping page: https://www.sidefx.com/docs/houdini/copernicus/substance_designer.html (11 October 2026)\n661\t\n662\t#### T01 Height first, separate reads, value-range hygiene (11 October 2026)\n663\t- Problem solved: detail built before the large forms makes the silhouette unreadable and forces rework; a narrow or clipped value range makes later blends peak or flatten (11 October 2026)\n664\t- Designer chain (SOURCE): reference first; large shapes, then mid breakups, then micro detail; judge height together with Normal and AO from the first nodes; keep a wide range without pure black or white and use Auto Levels to restore it; stack heights with Max Lighten; compress the base into 0 to 0.8 to leave 0.8 to 1 for surface detail (azulejos); keep grunge out of the early stage (Perez); keep node count low (Charlie Foreman); expose few parameters (Perez, Lynch) (11 October 2026)\n665\t- Copernicus chain: one named block per read (macro, meso, micro) each ending in a `null`, merged with `blend` (Maximum for raised unions, Add for zero-mean sculpting deltas, Mask = amplitude); monitors kept beside the chain: `heighttonormal`, `heighttoambientocclusion`, `curvature`; range tools: Auto Levels node = `equalize` (OFFICIAL-MAP), Levels node = `remap` (OFFICIAL-MAP); the headroom compression is `remap` Output Min 0 and Output Max 0.8 (UNVERIFIED mapping) (11 October 2026)\n666\t- Parameter hints: the `equalize` page warns that equalizing per frame can flicker for procedural changes and recommends a `remap` with a fixed range; `remap` Compute Range fills Input Min and Max from the layer; `statistics` outputs average, minimum and maximum (SOURCE: https://www.sidefx.com/docs/houdini/nodes/cop/equalize.html and https://www.sidefx.com/docs/houdini/nodes/cop/statistics.html) (11 October 2026)\n667\t- Visual signature (UNVERIFIED): the reference shows readable large masses at thumbnail size, mid-size steps at half size and tooth only at full size; if tooth is the only readable scale the surface is micro-only (fabric, sandpaper) and the macro block can stay flat (11 October 2026)\n668\t- Local notes: Copernicus1.md line 1537 imposes node names of the form letter, number, subject, role (for example `C03_TL__distance_to_corner`) and line 1627 requires every detail layer to have a zero mean; line 1509 records that an earlier fiber-wood attempt failed partly because the macro form came after the fibers (11 October 2026)\n669\t\n670\t#### T02 Shape blockout, bevel profiles and distance fields (11 October 2026)\n671\t- Problem solved: give each tile, stone or cell a believable footprint and cross-section (flat top, bevel, dish, pillow) before any damage (11 October 2026)\n672\t- Designer chain (SOURCE): Tile Generator for grids; polygon plus linear gradient in minimum-darken into Tile Random (Soto); primitives combined by add and subtract, blurred, histogram-scanned and warped (Caggese); Edge Detect plus Bevel for tile seams (Prakapenka); Edge Detect to bevel corners, Distance to clear small bubbles and Transformation 2D doubling resolution during the bevel (paver); warped Cells 4 for irregular facets (Goodbrand); the Distance node grows masks more predictably than blur and bevels with a sharp linear falloff, with Euclidean, Manhattan or Chebyshev metrics (https://experienceleague.adobe.com/en/docs/substance-3d-designer/using/substance-graphs/nodes-reference-for-substance-graphs/atomic-nodes/distance) (11 October 2026)\n673\t- Copernicus chain: `tilepattern` (outputs tiles, id, uv; Pattern Types Stack, Header, Stretcher, Flemish and other bonds, Windmill, Basketwave, Herringbone, Custom; Inset; Round Corners; Draw As Filled, Distance, Gradient or Outline) -> `feather` for the bevel; `cellpattern` (Gap, Bevel, Bevel Ramp, Random Height, Orient, Steepness, outputs noise, border, center, id) for cobble blocks; `sdfshape` -> `sdftomono` for single shapes; `planarinflate` for pillow profiles (Height Scale, Pre Scale, Wrinkles, Mounds, Blur, Iterations); `monotosdf` -> `sdfadjust` (Iso Offset) to grow or shrink; `eikonal` for obstacle-aware distance (11 October 2026)\n674\t- OFFICIAL-MAP rows used: Shape = `sdfshape` + `sdftomono`; Bevel = `feather`; Distance = `feather` and `extrapolateboundaries`; Tile Generator = `tilepattern`; Make It Tile = `tilepattern` and `feather` (11 October 2026)\n675\t- Parameter hints (SOURCE: Feather page and OFFICIAL-MAP text): Feather Direction Circle gives smoother results, Feather from High to Low expands outer edges into the background, Distance for Unit Change sets the bevel reach, Include Border on for tiling; Extrapolate Boundaries needs `fillarea` and `mask` inputs and Edge Padding to bleed values outward (11 October 2026)\n676\t- Visual signature (UNVERIFIED): crisp polygon footprints with constant gap width and a uniform slope band (tile or brick), irregular rounded cells (cobble), pillowy convex bodies (quilting, inflated shapes) (11 October 2026)\n677\t- Local notes: Copernicus1.md line 1516 says Tile Pattern in Distance mode gave a plateau and recommends the uv output (u and v in [-0.5, 0.5]) with |u| over the half width; line 1541 states uv runs from -1 to +1 per tile; the two notes conflict, so UNVERIFIED which range holds in 22.0.368 and a probe export is needed; line 1519 says the `noise` output of `cellularnoise` gives a network of lines and the `id` output plus `randommono` gives filled plates (11 October 2026)\n678\t\n679\t#### T03 Slope blur: edge breakup, rounding, chips, erosion, cascades (11 October 2026)\n680\t- Problem solved: straight borders and uniform plateaus look synthetic; the cure is to drag, erode or spread values along a flow derived from a smooth control map (11 October 2026)\n681\t- Designer chain (SOURCE https://experienceleague.adobe.com/en/docs/substance-3d-designer/using/substance-graphs/nodes-reference-for-substance-graphs/node-library/filters/blurs/slope-blur): Slope Blur takes a grayscale Slope input steering the blur (the input must have smooth gradients), with Samples 0 to 32, Intensity 0 to 16 and Mode Blur (like an anisotropic blur), Min (erodes bright areas) or Max (spreads bright areas); uses named by Adobe are chipping, weathering, edge rounding and smearing dirt or rust (11 October 2026)\n682\t- Documented uses: noise (Clouds 2) as slope with Samples 32 and Min to chip rock (http://theedgeloop.blogspot.com/2016/12/simple-but-amazing-rock-chip-detail-in.html); low-frequency noise slope for large edge damage on pavers and slope blur on straight tile edges to give later steps more randomness (gamesartist pages); two Max passes under a linear gradient to lengthen leaks while limiting sampling artifacts (Mark Foreman); a duplicated slope-blur and subtract chain for soft and rough detail (Soto); slope blur on both height inputs to inflate moss blobs (Mark Foreman); a cascade is the repeated pair with different control noise; Charlie Foreman warns against overusing it (11 October 2026)\n683\t- Copernicus chain, OFFICIAL-MAP: Slope Blur node = the \"Distort by Slope\" recipe, a `slopedir` wired to the `dir` input of a `distort`, which pushes the source away from higher control values and toward lower ones; chain: control noise (`fractalnoise`) -> `blur` -> `slopedir` -> `distort`.`dir`, shape or height -> `distort`.`source`, Streak Min for chips and erosion, Streak Max to spread, Streak Blur to smear (11 October 2026)\n684\t- Parameter hints (SOURCE: https://www.sidefx.com/docs/houdini/nodes/cop/distort.html and https://www.sidefx.com/docs/houdini/nodes/cop/slopedir.html): `slopedir` Kernel Size widens the derivative distance to remove stepping, Angle 90 makes the directions go around hills instead of up or down them, Scale sets magnitude, Read Pixels outside Image for wrapped tiles; `distort` Scale is the strength (negative traces backward), `scale` input is a per-pixel multiplier, Pixels per Step on with Maximum Steps raised traces curved paths more accurately (the nearest thing to Designer Samples, UNVERIFIED), Use Streak Weight Ramp tapers the streak; mapping of Designer Intensity 0 to 16 onto `distort` Scale is UNVERIFIED and must be tuned by eye (11 October 2026)\n685\t- Visual signature (UNVERIFIED): edges that look dragged along one drift direction, notches whose shape follows an underlying soft noise instead of the edge normal, highlights smeared along slopes, rims worn only where the control noise is steep (11 October 2026)\n686\t- Local notes: Copernicus1.md lines 1211 and 1213 list a Perez-labelled pair, samples 16 intensity 0.45 mode Max then samples 8 intensity 0.25 mode Min (LOCAL, attribution UNVERIFIED); the name `slopeblur` there is not a COP (11 October 2026)\n687\t\n688\t#### T04 Warps: regular, directional, multi-directional, non-uniform, vector (11 October 2026)\n689\t- Problem solved: bend straight lines and cracks into organic ones without changing the overall layout, and avoid the one-sided stretching of a single-direction warp (11 October 2026)\n690\t- Designer chain (SOURCE https://experienceleague.adobe.com/en/docs/substance-3d-designer/using/substance-graphs/nodes-reference-for-substance-graphs/atomic-nodes/warp and /directional-warp): Warp shifts the input along the slope of a gradient input (Intensity, input filtering); Directional Warp displaces in one direction (Warp angle in turns, Intensity input map, intensity map offset); Multi Directional Warp (https://experienceleague.adobe.com/en/docs/substance-3d-designer/using/substance-graphs/nodes-reference-for-substance-graphs/node-library/filters/effects/multi-directional-warp) runs the directional warp several times in opposite directions with Intensity 0 to 20, Warp Angle 0 to 1, Mode Average, Max, Min or Chain and Directions 1, 2 or 4; Non-Uniform Directional Warp (same folder, non-uniform-directional-warp) reads direction and intensity from maps and adds Trail Mode, Trail Length, Trail Fade, Trail Curve; Vector Warp (same folder, vector-warp) moves pixels by a color vector map with Intensity 0 to 1 and a DirectX or OpenGL Vector Format (11 October 2026)\n691\t- Documented uses: Cells 4 warped before edge detection (Goodbrand); Directional Warp over the wood base so rings stretch around knots (Mark Foreman); Directional Warp to stop cell cracks running straight (Charlie Foreman); Slope Blur then Directional Warp on a square for a vertical rock (Furneri); directional noise into a multi-directional warp whose angle follows the slope for pits (Soto); a faint multi-directional warp for grain (Khanna); the DETE node keeps shapes anchored (Thiger) (11 October 2026)\n692\t- Copernicus chain, OFFICIAL-MAP: Warp node = `distort`; structures (UNVERIFIED): Warp = `slopedir` of the gradient -> `distort`.`dir` with Streak Off; Directional Warp = `distort` Direction Type Angle (degrees = turns times 360) with the intensity map on the `scale` input and Streak Off; Multi-Directional = two or four `distort` at Angle a, a+90 (and the 45 degree diagonals), merged by `average` Operation Average, Max or Min, or wired in series for Chain; Non-Uniform = `distort`.`dir` fed by a UV layer built from the angle map, `scale` = intensity map, Streak Min, Max or Blur with a Streak Weight ramp as the trail fade; Vector Warp = the vector map directly on `distort`.`dir` (11 October 2026)\n693\t- Parameter hints: keep Scale small and let the map do the work (LOCAL examples: `distort` Scale 0.008 on planks at Copernicus1.md line 1353 and 0.02 on fibers at line 1398); `distort` Border should follow the layer border so tiling survives; mask input limits the warp region (11 October 2026)\n694\t- Visual signature (UNVERIFIED): rectilinear joints and cracks turned gently wavy, grain bending around round knots, elongated pores, a shape that slides sideways without losing its anchor (11 October 2026)\n695\t\n696\t#### T05 Flood fill family: per-piece tilt, per-piece level, per-piece pattern (11 October 2026)\n697\t- Problem solved: tiles, planks and stones that are individually flat still need individual height, tilt, size-based darkening or a different pattern each; a mask alone cannot give that (11 October 2026)\n698\t- Designer chain (SOURCE https://experienceleague.adobe.com/en/docs/substance-3d-designer/using/substance-graphs/nodes-reference-for-substance-graphs/node-library/filters/effects/flood-fill and its sibling pages): Flood Fill is a base effect that needs a black-and-white input with pure black borders between pieces and is not used alone; Flood Fill to Gradient makes each piece a linear gradient with Angle, Angle Variation (the most powerful control), Multiply by Bounding Box Size, Angle and Slope input maps and Flat Slope Color; Flood Fill to Random Grayscale and Random Color give each piece a random value; further mappers are to BBox Size, to Position, to Index, and the Flood Fill Mapper that places a pattern in every cell with scale, rotation and luminance maps (11 October 2026)\n699\t- Documented uses: random gradients tilt rock pieces inward, a second random-gray fill plus Histogram Scan chooses which pieces crack further, repeated per scale (Goodbrand); Flood Fill to Gradient with Angle Variation tilts tiles and Flood Fill to Random Grayscale sets elevation and missing tiles (paver); three randomized gradients combined by minimum-darken (river pebbles); Flood Fill adds height and incline to cobblestones (Sirotina); Perez dedicated an NVIDIA episode to the Flood Fill node and a Learn Squared video to Flood Fills (titles only) (11 October 2026)\n700\t- Copernicus chain, OFFICIAL-MAP: Flood Fill = `segmentbyconnectivity` and `uvmapbyid`; Flood Fill to Random Grayscale and Color = `randommono` and `randomrgb`; chain (UNVERIFIED structure): piece mask -> `segmentbyconnectivity` (Connectivity Above Threshold, Threshold 0.5) -> `id` -> `randommono` with the id wired into its `seed` input (random level per piece) -> `blend` Add to the base; tilt: `uvmapbyid` (Mode UV Map, UV Space Texture, so each piece gets 0 to 1 coordinates) plus a `wrangle` that outputs dot(uv minus 0.5, (cos a, sin a)) with a from a second `randommono` of the same id; bounding-box size: `uvmapbyid` Mode Size or `statisticsbyid` area; position: `uvmapbyid` Mode Center, Minimum or Maximum; pattern per cell (Flood Fill Mapper): `uvmapbyid`.`uv` -> `sample` (UV Sample, UV Space Texture) of the pattern, with `uvscale` available for filtering (11 October 2026)\n701\t- Parameter hints (SOURCE: segmentbyconnectivity, uvmapbyid, randommono, idtomono, fillconnected pages): `segmentbyconnectivity` writes -1 where the rule fails and non-negative integers elsewhere, Collapse IDs renumbers from zero but ids can still change with resolution, so random per-piece values may shift when the resolution changes; `randommono` Range Values Minimum and Maximum, Seed, Zero Negative IDs sends the background to 0; `idtomono` converts ids by Cast, Safe Pack or Bitwise; `fillconnected` floods only one connected region from a Seed Location, so it does not replace the per-island fill (11 October 2026)\n702\t- Visual signature (UNVERIFIED): every tile or stone sits at its own level or leans its own way while its own surface stays planar; a few pieces missing or sunken; darker small pieces when size drives brightness (11 October 2026)\n703\t- Local notes: Copernicus1.md line 1518 records that `randommono` returns one constant unless the id goes into its seed input (index 4); lines 1220 and 1242 are the Perez-labelled flood-fill and histogram steps (LOCAL) (11 October 2026)\n704\t\n705\t#### T06 Histogram scan: turning soft noise into controllable masks (11 October 2026)\n706\t- Problem solved: noise has no edges; every chip, plateau, crack and wear mask needs a threshold whose size and hardness can be set and exposed (11 October 2026)\n707\t- Designer chain (SOURCE https://experienceleague.adobe.com/en/docs/substance-3d-designer/using/substance-graphs/nodes-reference-for-substance-graphs/node-library/filters/adjustments/histogram-scan): Position 0 to 1 acts like brightness and widens or narrows a transition (default 0 gives all black, so start near 0.5), Contrast 0 to 1 sets edge hardness, Invert Position flips the result; named as the way to expand or shrink masks in a controlled manner (11 October 2026)\n708\t- Documented uses: Balance exposed to control secondary versus tertiary cracks (Goodbrand); knot masks from Highpass plus Histogram Scan and a Gaussian Spots 2 band for leaks (Mark Foreman); Histogram Select for varied damage across bricks (Charlie Foreman); blur then Histogram Scan to clean the harsh edge of a carved shape (Polycount snippet https://polycount.com/discussion/202087/substance-designer-brick-wall-feedback) (11 October 2026)\n709\t- Copernicus chain, OFFICIAL-MAP: Histogram Scan = `remap` Operation Threshold (Threshold When Greater, Threshold, Width; Width 0 is a hard cutoff); a band needs two thresholds (Greater and Less) multiplied with `blend` Multiply (UNVERIFIED); `compare` or `compareblend` with Tolerance and Falloff Linear or Elendt give soft edges; expose Threshold as a promoted HDA parameter (11 October 2026)\n710\t- Parameter hints: `remap` Remap operation has Compute Range, Outside Range Clamp at End, Repeat or Extend; LOCAL line 1542 warns that a remap ramp must cover the whole input range; the mapping from Designer Contrast to Width is UNVERIFIED (11 October 2026)\n711\t- Visual signature (UNVERIFIED): clean blobs and bands with a stable ratio of masked to unmasked area (the local peeling-paint recipe measured about 65 percent paint to 35 percent bare wood, Copernicus1.md line 1360) (11 October 2026)\n712\t\n713\t#### T07 Tile generator, tile sampler, splatter and fractal fill (11 October 2026)\n714\t- Problem solved: place many copies of a shape with controlled count, size classes, rotation, level and non-overlap, then fill the gaps with smaller copies (11 October 2026)\n715\t- Designer chain (SOURCE https://experienceleague.adobe.com/en/docs/substance-3d-designer/using/substance-graphs/nodes-reference-for-substance-graphs/node-library/texture-generators/patterns/tile-sampler and /splatter): Tile Sampler extends Tile Generator with map inputs (Scale Map, Displacement Map, Rotation Map, Vector Map, Color Map, Mask Map, Pattern Distribution Map, up to six pattern inputs), X and Y Amount 0 to 64, Scale and Scale Random, Rotation Random, Position Random, Offset, Mask Map Threshold, Mask Random, Color or Luminance Random and a Blending Mode of add/subtract, max or alpha; Splatter has Pattern Size Width and Height, Rotation Variation, Disorder, Disorder Angle, Size Variation, Gain, Grid Number, and its Zoom breaks tiling (11 October 2026)\n716\t- Documented uses: six Tile Samplers from large stones to small, each a duplicate with another seed and X/Y amounts, each placed stone set masked into the next sampler through its mask input and joined by Height Blend (river pebbles); Perez moss carpet from patches and the Perez space collection with a tileable base shape plus samplers (B1); Soto pebbles driven by an inverted gray mask; Sirotina clover with Luminance Random and Mask Random; Khanna boulders, stones, pebbles, leaves and twigs; Foreman Tile Sampler to distribute objects by value (11 October 2026)\n717\t- Copernicus chain, OFFICIAL-MAP: Tile Sampler = `shapescatter`; Splatter = `shapescatter` and `curvescatter`; Tile Generator = `tilepattern`; `shapescatter` inputs size_ref, stamps (up to 10, default star), density (rejection sampling), jitter, direction, angle, scale, stretch, color, alpha, sort, select; outputs result, id, uv; Blend Method includes Maximum, Minimum, Add, Subtract, Multiply, Depth-Sorted Over; Do Tiled keeps stamps from clipping at the image edge; Mode Cycle, Random, Layer Select or Limit Selection chooses stamps; `curvescatter` scatters stamps along a curve input with Distribution Distance, Scatter or By Count (11 October 2026)\n718\t- Gap filling: the SideFX Terrain Cobblestone recipe uses a modified `cellpattern`, a `fractalsample` and several `blend` nodes, where `fractalsample` (Max Octaves, Lacunarity, Roughness, Blend Mode Add, Subtract, Maximum or Over, Jitter, Angle, Random Flip X and Y) samples the pattern at rising frequencies so smaller copies fill the gaps, Maximum keeping tiles from overlapping (SOURCE: https://www.sidefx.com/docs/houdini/heightfields_cop/recipes.html and https://www.sidefx.com/docs/houdini/nodes/cop/fractalsample.html) (11 October 2026)\n719\t- Mapping of Designer inputs, UNVERIFIED: Mask Map -> `density`, Pattern Distribution Map -> `select` with Mode Layer Select, Rotation Map -> `angle`, Scale Map -> `scale`, Vector Map -> `direction` and `stretch`, Luminance Random -> Stamp Color Variance on Value, Mask Random -> `density` below 1, Reverse Rendering Order -> `sort` (11 October 2026)\n720\t- Parameter hints: Blend Method Maximum for raised stamps and Subtract or Minimum for pits; one `shapescatter` per size class with its own Scatter Seed; Stamps X and Y are the grid counts; Treat Mono as Alpha changes how mono stamps expand; build stamps with `sdfshape` -> `sdftomono` or `planarinflate` (11 October 2026)\n721\t- Visual signature (UNVERIFIED): several discrete size classes of the same few shapes, random rotation, little overlap, a hierarchy where the biggest pieces were placed first and the smallest fill what remains (11 October 2026)\n722\t- Local notes: Copernicus1.md line 1517 says a column offset on alternate columns needs a Custom Pattern multiparm (ncolumns, docoloffset, coloffset) in `tilepattern` (11 October 2026)\n723\t\n724\t#### T08 Non-uniform blur: softness that varies across the image (11 October 2026)\n725\t- Problem solved: a uniform blur destroys crisp features; many surfaces need crisp tops with soft flanks, rounded tubes beside sharp bolts, fibers that keep fine detail, or drips of graduated length (11 October 2026)\n726\t- Designer chain (SOURCE https://experienceleague.adobe.com/en/docs/substance-3d-designer/using/substance-graphs/nodes-reference-for-substance-graphs/node-library/filters/blurs/non-uniform-blur): a grayscale Blur Map sets the blur per pixel (white more, black none); Intensity 0 to 50 is the maximum, Anisotropy 0 to 1 and Asymmetry 0 to 1 add direction and bias, Angle 0 to 1 drives both, Samples 1 to 16 per blade and Blades 1 to 9 set quality (total samples = Samples times Blades) (11 October 2026)\n727\t- Documented uses: Perez rounds pipes with it (B1); Mark Foreman feeds Directional Noise 2 through Non Uniform Blur Grayscale because a Directional Blur over-smooths fibers, blurs a mask by a Directional Scratches map shaped with Levels to vary drip length and blends the original back with Max Lighten, and softens a height with it to flatten shapes and remove bright specks before Height Blend; Khanna blurs warped round stones non-uniformly; Prakapenka uses it in masks (11 October 2026)\n728\t- Copernicus chain: isotropic = `blur` with its `size` input (a mono or float input that scales the radius per pixel), Filter Gaussian, Units Pixels or Image; anisotropic = `streakblur` Mode Blur with the `scalelength` input multiplying Length, Angle or the `direction` UV input; Designer Asymmetry maps loosely to `streakblur` Bias (-1 ends the streak at the pixel, +1 starts it, UNVERIFIED); Blades and Samples have no counterpart because the quality is fixed (11 October 2026)\n729\t- Parameter hints: a black pixel in the `size` map means no blur; the Size parameter is the maximum radius; for drips use Angle 90 or 270 with a random scratch map on `scalelength` (UNVERIFIED) (11 October 2026)\n730\t- Visual signature (UNVERIFIED): softness that depends on position (feathered flanks under sharp ridges, soft tubes next to hard hardware, fibers with intact micro detail, streaks that fade out at different lengths) (11 October 2026)\n731\t\n732\t#### T09 Height blend: merging two heights by their own values (11 October 2026)\n733\t- Problem solved: stacking objects on a base with a painted mask gives the wrong contact line; the correct look is that the higher surface wins locally and the contact follows where the heights cross (11 October 2026)\n734\t- Designer chain (SOURCE https://experienceleague.adobe.com/en/docs/substance-3d-designer/using/substance-graphs/nodes-reference-for-substance-graphs/node-library/material-filters/effects-material/height-blend): inputs Height Top, Height Bottom and an optional Mask; outputs the blended height and a black-and-white mask; Height Offset 0 to 1 is the primary control and moves where the blend happens, Contrast 0 to 1 sharpens or softens the transition, Mode is Balanced height or Bottom height priority, Opacity 0 to 1 fades the top; Material Height Blend is the full-material version (11 October 2026)\n735\t- Documented uses: ground plus stones (Sirotina); pebbles sinking into dirt and partly covered by grout (paver); moss blended like the ground; Perez Viking layers face, nose, beard and hair (B1); terrain plus rock with a single slider applied lightly so soil partly covers a rock (Furneri); mud and pebbles tied by undulation (Khanna); Charlie Foreman: stack height layers and adjust each layer height separately (11 October 2026)\n736\t- Copernicus chain: the 376-name list has no height-blend node, so build it from verified pieces (UNVERIFIED structure): `compareblend` with A = top height shifted by `function` Shift (the Height Offset), B = bottom height, True If A greater than B, input `true` = top, input `false` = bottom, Tolerance and Falloff Linear or Elendt as the contrast, Bias to favour one side; the Designer mask output = `compare` with the same operands and tolerance (output `mask`); Opacity = the Mask parameter of the final `blend`; Bottom priority = a positive Bias toward B (11 October 2026)\n737\t- Cheaper cases: a pure union of raised shapes is `blend` Maximum or `average` Operation Max; Charlie Foreman prefers Max Lighten over Add so whites do not stack past one (11 October 2026)\n738\t- Visual signature (UNVERIFIED): partly buried objects, contact lines that wander with the surface heights, overlaps where the higher object always shows (11 October 2026)\n739\t\n740\t#### T10 Edge chipping (11 October 2026)\n741\t- Problem solved: crisp rims look new; chips must sit only at the rims of raised shapes, with irregular notches whose depth fades inward (11 October 2026)\n742\t- Designer chains (SOURCE): (1) noise as slope input of a Slope Blur with Samples 32 and Mode Min, then subtract from the base height (http://theedgeloop.blogspot.com/2016/12/simple-but-amazing-rock-chip-detail-in.html and a Polycount thread https://polycount.com/discussion/202087/substance-designer-brick-wall-feedback); (2) Edge Detect (Edge Width 1 to 16, Edge Roundness 0 to 16, Tolerance 0 to 1, Invert; https://experienceleague.adobe.com/en/docs/substance-3d-designer/using/substance-graphs/nodes-reference-for-substance-graphs/node-library/filters/effects/edge-detect) multiplied by noise and thresholded by Histogram Scan; (3) a slope blur blended over a shape to set edge damage intensity (Charlie Foreman); (4) Slope Blur nodes for chips, a low-count Quantize for shard-like steps, and directional nodes at low strength with 4 directions to break the slope-blur look (azulejos); (5) thr