he grain (11 October 2026)\n771\t\n772\t#### T14 Metal relief: brushed, hammered, dented, worn (11 October 2026)\n773\t- Problem solved: metal height is mostly a directional micro-relief plus a few dents and edge chips; it is easy to overdo (11 October 2026)\n774\t- Documented parameter semantics (SOURCE: Painter MatFinish pages in A4.25): Brushed Linear has Brushing Intensity, Length and Rotation, Hammered has Details Intensity and Rotation, Rough has Brushing Intensity and Brushing Warp, Perforated Circles has Holes Size; none of the Painter pages states which channels they write (11 October 2026)\n775\t- Designer chains (SOURCE): non-uniform rounding of pipes (Perez), height pushed harder on exterior panels because of tessellation (Perez), height-driven damage plus dirt in hard-surface panels (Learn Squared Damaging Height); no readable Designer breakdown of hammered metal was found (11 October 2026)\n776\t- Copernicus chain (UNVERIFIED structure): brushed = stretched `fractalnoise` -> `streakblur` Mode Blur (Angle = Rotation, Length) -> `remap`; hammered = `shapescatter` with dish stamps (`sdfshape` Circle -> `planarinflate`) and Blend Method Subtract or Minimum, or `cellularnoise` Euclidean with Bevel; scratches = `streakblur` of sparse random lines; edge wear = A3.2 chain; check with `heighttonormal` and `heighttoambientocclusion` (11 October 2026)\n777\t- LOCAL: Copernicus1.md lines 1248 to 1272 describe a 45-node brushed metal recipe (anisotropic blur samples 16, intensity 25, anisotropy 0.95 and angle 0 on a Perlin noise of scale 24; clipped levels 0.35 to 0.70; wear zones by edge detect and flood fill); attribution to Perez is UNVERIFIED and `non_uniform_blur_grayscale` there maps to `streakblur` or `blur` (11 October 2026)\n778\t- Visual signature (UNVERIFIED): long parallel hairlines with slight waviness, overlapping dished dents with soft rims, chips at panel edges (11 October 2026)\n779\t\n780\t#### T15 Ceramic and glazed tile relief (11 October 2026)\