on, but they disagree on its release date and its place in the series.\n\n**Contents:** The collection has 15 materials that fall into three distinct, and yet connected categories: \"Living Quarters,\" \"Space Station\" and \"Space Shuttle.\" Each material is fully tweakable. The Living Quarters set covers an astronaut's habitat, with reinforced walls and built-in storage meant to handle zero gravity. The Space Station set has softer edges and mixes in cloth and high-tech polymers like those on the ISS. Perez said he aimed for something that could represent a spaceship or rocket exterior when applied. The materials are based on real-world photographic reference of materials used on the International Space Station, the old Space Shuttles, and NASA living quarters.\n\n**Release date and order:** One source places the release in early 2018. CGChannel reports that Allegorithmic released a collection of 15 spacecraft materials created by PlayStation senior material artist Javier Perez, with the update dated 20 March 2018. Another source, a March 18, 2019 press release, calls it the first Signature Series collection of 2019. The 2018 date is consistent with the \"second release\" framing: Daniel Thiger's inaugural set came first. Another CGChannel note also says Allegorithmic has released its third Signature Series, created by CD PROJEKT RED senior environment artist Mark Foreman in December 2018. I could not resolve the conflict from these results. The 2019 date may be a press-release date for the same set, or a separate release, so check the Substance Source library page for the official date.\n\n**Series context:** The program began in January 2018. The inaugural Signature Series release comes from game designer Daniel Thiger, lead environment artist for Bungie. Perez's set is the one most often described as the space-themed release in the series.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"8b226