{"parentUuid":"e459c2dc-20eb-4783-82e4-b6f79cdc36b9","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"a30145bc0fb1a513e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01J4PKCyJPM716jBu8rCoB5K","type":"tool_result","content":"**What this page covers:** The Filter List window lets you quickly add effects to a scene without building a full COP network. You can add, edit, and reorder the nodes in the window, and Houdini applies them in the order listed. The page describes three ways to use filter lists: in slap comp, in Solaris with the Image Filter LOP, and in the deprecated USD Render ROP workflow.\n\n**Categories and nodes by function:** The page doesn't include this. It says only that **Add Filter** lets you choose from a list of nodes, without naming them or grouping them. It also links to other pages for more detail: Custom filters, the Image Filter LOP, and the COP Image Filter List node."}]},"uuid":"593a410c-6fa3-4092-ad9c-3929c801fd1b","timestamp":"2026-10-11T15:15:51.564Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"9feb0a2c-9e95-40d4-b3fb-877caca16ce6","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":null,"branch":null,"default_branch":null,"status":null,"visibility":null,"error":"pending"},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"e459c2dc-20eb-4783-82e4-b6f79cdc36b9","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main","slug":"deep-stirring-lark"}
{"parentUuid":"15f7a1de-001f-47dc-a14f-9b1691a9e807","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"a30145bc0fb1a513e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Xp3ZLAMfoGcAVpLjApjeTd","type":"tool_result","content":"The Copernicus network uses several coordinate spaces, each with its own range.\n\n- **Buffer:** The raw samples of the image, matching how data is laid out in memory. The page calls a buffer element the pixel-equivalent for buffer space.\n- **Pixel:** A canonical pixel-sized area whose origin is its lower-left corner. The display window sets where the image sits in this pixel array, and the data window sets where the buffer sits.\n- **Texture:** Runs from 0 to 1 across the data window and buffer. Because it always spans that full range, it stretches the texture to fit.\n- **Image:** Runs from -1 to 1 across the display window. It scales the image to fit while preserving the pixel aspect ratio, so non-square images leave borders in the viewport.\n- **Local:** Runs from -(w/2, h/2) to (w/2, h/2) across the display window, where w and h are its width and height in world units. It also preserves the pixel aspect ratio.\n- **World:** A 3D location in modelling space. The viewport uses it, and by default it matches image space.\n\nCopernicus uses image space rather than texture space because it works linearly and can hold negative values. The page says values stay in their natural range, so HDR and vector data aren't clamped or normalized.\n\n**Crop behavior differs by space:**\n- **Image space** crops are absolute. Two identical crops don't stack.\n- **Texture space** crops are relative to the incoming data window. Two identical crops stack, so their effects multiply.\n- **Pixel space** crops use the exact pixel values you enter.\n\n**What the page doesn't cover:** It doesn't say how parameters such as blur size or distort scale are measured in each space, so I can't confirm their units. It also doesn't discuss resolution independence or tiling directly. The normalized 0–1 texture range and the aspect-preserving image and local spaces are the closest relevant details, but the page doesn't draw explicit conclusions about either topic."}]},"uuid":"be9420a6-7b07-4958-b544-f836c4d551a7","timestamp":"2026-10-11T15:15:53.125Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"9feb0a2c-9e95-40d4-b3fb-877caca16ce6","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":null,"branch":null,"default_branch":null,"status":null,"visibility":null,"error":"pending"},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"15f7a1de-001f-47dc-a14f-9b1691a9e807","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main","slug":"deep-stirring-lark"}
{"parentUuid":"d603a7b4-965e-47e3-8312-9f0b29eee04b","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"a30145bc0fb1a513e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QbzDtDCZhM6TjXuycJpF22","type":"tool_result","content":"Copernicus uses two normal conventions. The page describes signed and offset normals in detail but doesn't cover the other topics you listed.\n\n**Signed vs. offset normals**\n- **Signed normals** match what Karma and SOPs use. Each RGB channel holds the normal's direction directly, so values can be negative. The page notes this can be a problem for many game engines. Signed normals have an origin at (0, 0, 0) and a length of 1. They appear as the `N` attribute on geometry and as Karma's `N` output.\n- **Offset normals** are signed normals remapped into the 0 to 1 range, as the page puts it, \"scaled and offset to fit in the `0` to `1` range.\" This is the form shaders usually expect for normal maps, which gives them the familiar blue look. Offset normals are centered near (0.5, 0.5, 0.5) and have a length of 0.5.\n\n**Controlling the convention**\nYou choose which convention a node reads or writes through:\n- The **Type Info** parameter on nodes such as Layer Properties COP\n- The **Conversion** parameter on nodes such as Convert Normal COP\n- The **Normal Type** parameter on Height to Normal COP\n\n**Not covered on this page**\n- OpenGL vs. DirectX Y-axis direction\n- Tangent space vs. world space\n- Specific guidance on how Height to Normal output should be used or exported beyond selecting its Normal Type\n\nFor those topics, check the Height to Normal COP node documentation or Karma's normal map documentation."}]},"uuid":"cb2f0f48-4d32-445d-83c6-33f059f77639","timestamp":"2026-10-11T15:15:53.499Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"9feb0a2c-9e95-40d4-b3fb-877caca16ce6","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":null,"branch":null,"default_branch":null,"sta