{"parentUuid":"3ca8d793-8428-4279-a2ce-d5ebcb47e55c","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"a30145bc0fb1a513e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MMZmELzMRJ1YxttB1pypSN","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"80.lv Substance Designer metal material breakdown dents scratches rust height map nodes \"Substance Designer\" worn metal plate\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"creating tarnished metal with ornament in substance designer\",\"url\":\"https://80.lv/articles/creating-tarnished-metal-with-ornament-in-substance-designer/\"},{\"title\":\"studying complex materials in substance designer\",\"url\":\"https://80.lv/articles/studying-complex-materials-in-substance-designer\"},{\"title\":\"001agt learning substance designer heightmap basecolor masks\",\"url\":\"https://80.lv/articles/001agt-learning-substance-designer-heightmap-basecolor-masks\"},{\"title\":\"I am very new to Substance, but I'm quickly realizing how powerful it is. I am trying to achieve a look similar to this:\",\"url\":\"https://polycount.com/discussion/comment/2335437/\"},{\"title\":\"a collection of useful nodes for sd\",\"url\":\"https://80.lv/articles/a-collection-of-useful-nodes-for-sd\"},{\"title\":\"therookies.co\",\"url\":\"https://www.therookies.co/projects/25247\"},{\"title\":\"Heightmaps from Substance Designer\",\"url\":\"https://digitalproduction.com/2018/04/09/heightmaps-aus-substance-designer/\"},{\"title\":\"From the course: Substance Designer for Architectural Visualization\",\"url\":\"https://origin-www.lynda.com/3D-Animation-tutorials/Using-height-map-add-normal/802829/2918292-4.html\"},{\"title\":\"Website powered by\",\"url\":\"https://joshuapelican.artstation.com/projects/eaa5G3\"},{\"title\":\"polycount.com\",\"url\":\"https://polycount.com/discussion/comment/2656011\"}]\n\nI didn't find an 80.lv breakdown that matches your exact description (dents, scratches, rust on a worn metal plate). The closest 80.lv result is a tarnished metal breakdown, which covers some of the same height-map techniques.\n\n**Closest match: tarnished metal on 80.lv**\nStan Brown, an Environment Material Artist at Grinding Gear Games, wrote a step-by-step guide to a tarnished metal material. It covers height data, grime and dirt layers, Albedo, Roughness, and more. His height setup starts with divider strips. He makes them from square shape nodes, bevels them, and runs them through a curve node to add a dip. A histogram select turns that dip into a mask for a tile sampler. He then blends the result into the strips with max lighten. The article is useful if you want a metal plate with panel seams and recessed areas, though it's about tarnish and ornament rather than damage.\n\n**Related 80.lv articles**\n- Karen Stanley's guides cover roof tiles and lava. Her roof guide includes creating damage using the edge detect node, which is the same kind of edge-based wear you'd use for scratches and chipped edges.\n- A bark breakdown on 80.lv uses a subtract the clean shape from the slightly blurred shape and contrast with a Level technique, which is also an edge-detect approach.\n\n**Off-site worn metal reference**\nA worn metal material on ArtStation covers a cut-up metal that was worn, but it isn't on 80.lv and doesn't show a full node breakdown in the results.\n\nTo find the exact article, try searching 80.lv directly for \"scratched metal,\" \"rusted metal,\" or \"damaged metal Substance Designer.\"\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"8977213e-ba4c-4aec-8c8c-22190f6d8487","timestamp":"2026-10-11T15:16:30.264Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"8a339f0b-5b14-4718-891d-45fef7da7ef4","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","branch":"main","default_branch":"main","status":{"clean":false,"counts":{"staged":0,"modified":35,"untracked":nu