{"parentUuid":"25adf28d-d0c4-4ea4-ae82-08814a4bee0b","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"a30145bc0fb1a513e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014oy97Vex4mTaFoRg1pksB1","type":"tool_result","content":"**Source:** Margot Casiro, 3D Environment Artist at Ubisoft Montpellier, interviewed by Kirill Tokarev for 80 Level (November 2019).\n\n**Height map first**\nCasiro builds the height map before anything else because it drives the rest of the material. She sometimes sketches color alongside it to catch problems early, and she keeps roughness mostly separate, using fresh noises rather than reusing existing chains.\n\n**Shape hierarchy**\nShe works from the top down:\n- Start with simple shapes on a flat plane, viewed from above.\n- Deform the primary forms with transform nodes.\n- Carve the main form by adding or subtracting secondary shapes.\n- Add volume with NonUniformBlur, refined by a Level or CurveNode.\n- Stack each shape using the same mask-and-blend method.\n\n**Nodes mentioned**\n- **Tile Sampler:** Builds the bark pattern from stretched paraboloids. For a nine-layer flower material, she used nine Tile Samplers to get one mask per layer.\n- **Slope Blur:** Combined with a large-scale Moisture Noise to roughen bark edges.\n- **Edge Detect:** She doesn't name a node. Instead, she subtracts a sharp shape from a blurred one and boosts the contrast with a Level, which works as a kind of edge detect.\n- **Flood Fill:** She considers it essential for clean masks and uses it in nearly every graph. It also drives gradient-map color variation.\n- **Histogram Scan:** Her main tool for extracting masks, and for thresholding height values to create holes.\n- **Height Blend:** Supplies the masks she needs when testing color on blended heights. It can also merge layers without clamping, though she recommends understanding range control first.\n\n**Parameter tips**\n- **Blur intensity:** Tie the NonUniformBlur intensity to the pattern count so it drops as patterns increase. She set the curve so the first division equals 1, with 4.12 as the target for her iteration.\n- **Layer ranges:** Each element starts at the full range, so narrow the ranges between layers to make a background stand out. If the ranges are set correctly, layers combine without clamping.\n- **Histogram checks:** Use the 2D view's Histogram tool to read height values.\n- **Messy masks:** If blended heights produce unclear masks, build an ambient occlusion map with a low radius, sharpen it with Levels, and blend it into the mask to recover outlines for Flood Fill.\n- **Finishing:** Run AutoLevel to fill the full height range, then use Ben Wilson's Inflate node to give the bark a convex, voluminous look."}]},"uuid":"b825ac86-f182-4756-bbfe-05782450bf01","timestamp":"2026-10-11T15:16:54.859Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"d114e947-70bf-4091-9e92-e5eb5f53ac3b","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":null,"branch":null,"default_branch":null,"status":null,"visibility":null,"error":"pending"},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"25adf28d-d0c4-4ea4-ae82-08814a4bee0b","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main","slug":"deep-stirring-lark"}
{"parentUuid":"e476c0a2-9c76-4d9f-8d5d-aa19c94ba399","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"a30145bc0fb1a513e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015aTgejVoGQdAUs5s3N7iNH","type":"tool_result","content":"The article doesn't cover several requested topics: hierarchy of shapes, slope blur, flood fill, histogram scan, warp, tile sampler, height blend, and performance. It's an interview with hard surface artist James Ritossa about making a brass material in Substance Painter and Designer, so the height map guidance is limited.\n\n**Artists**\n- **James Ritossa** is the only artist giving tips. Sharlene Lin's Substance Days presentation and Wes, a developer, are mentioned, but no tips are attributed to them.\n\n**Tips from James Ritossa**\n- **Dirt and dust:** Add a thin layer of dust to nearly everything, using a Dust Soft 2 node. For dirt, he uses a Mortar Wall base with height turned off.\n- **Repurposing materials:** Don't limit a material to its name. A rust material can drive dirt or color variation.\n- **Accuracy:** Vary roughness and base color instead of matching physical values exactly, but stay close to the reference and avoid overly glossy finishes.\n- **Imperfections:** Small details like fingerprints, grease, and water spots make materials look real.\n- **Layering:** Build layered materials, such as paint chipping over metal, by thinking about how the layers wear in real life. Study real wear where possible.\n\n**Node and mask usage**\n- Dust Soft 2 and Dust Occlusion masks add dust and collect dirt in crevices.\n- A Rust Fine material adds fine dirt.\n- A Levels node adjusts grunge until it looks right.\n- An Mg Mask editor, with contrast, balance, AO, and grunge inputs, drives the peeling effect.\n- A MatFinish Rough filter adds subtle roughness variation.\n\n**Edge wear and chipping**\n- An edge rust smart mask controls wear, with edge wear balance at 0.69 and contrast at 0.\n- Grunge in the roughness channel removes gloss from edges.\n- An edge scratches mask is softened by a Dust Soft 2 mask.\n- An Edges Blur mask on a brass base fades color on the most exposed surfaces.\n- For peeling paint, use a rough base so the paint appears to wrap around grime and imperfections.\n\n**Shape complexity and performance**\n- He uses Substance Painter for metals without complex shapes and Substance Designer for grass, roof tiles, and paneling.\n- He says the workflow is fast, with the brass material taking about 15 minutes, and prefers Boolean plus Dynamesh over Sub-D modeling for speed."}]},"uuid":"f2198646-6b42-4df6-8fcd-8780247f1526","timestamp":"2026-10-11T15:16:55.672Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"d114e947-70bf-4091-9e92-e5eb5f53ac3b","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":null,"branch":null,"default_branch":null,"status":null,"visibility":null,"error":"pending"},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"e476c0a2-9c76-4d9f-8d5d-aa19c94ba399","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main","slug":"deep-stirring-lark"}
{"parentUuid":"06d0a3a1-73e1-48f6-bfac-bc4199173d93","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"a30145bc0fb1a513e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JqcPdFxnNEQfruNdzFQGtP","type":"tool_result","content":"**Artist:** Stan Brown, an Environment Material Artist at Grinding Gear Games in New Zealand, working on Path of Exile and Path of Exile 2. Arti Sergeev interviewed him.\n\n**Height workflow (in order)**\n\nThe article doesn't use the labels \"panel seams\" or \"dents,\" and grime, scratches, and tarnish are handled in the albedo and roughness sections, not the height chain. The steps below cover what the article does describe.\n\n1. **Divider strips:** Two square shapes are combined by subtracting the smaller from the larger, which sets strip width and spacing. The strips are beveled and run through a curve node to add a center dip. A histogram select isolates each dip and uses it as the mask for a tile sampler set to paraboloids. The Y value is raised to make them oval, and they're rotated about 40°. Blur and curve nodes shape the result, which is blended in with max lighten. A levels node sets the blend height, then a blur and range node compress it. It is max-lightened onto a uniform color base.\n2. **Base value:** The uniform color is set to 0.5 in absolute 16-bit. The higher bit depth prevents stepping, and the neutral midpoint lets values be added or subtracted easily.\n3. **Top/bottom divider:** A line shape is blurred and curved into a rounded profile, safe-transformed to the top, mirrored on the Y axis, and added with linear dodge at low opacity.\n4. **Ridges:** A square tile sampler with a line-shape mask is stretched by increasing its Y size. It's blurred, levels lower its white point, then it's moved to the strip centers with a safe transform and max-lightened in.\n5. **Split masks:** Custom half black/white nodes, placed where the strips sit, separate the upper and lower pattern zones.\n6. **Columns:** Two vertical-line tile samplers are combined. The second is masked by the first, the thin lines are subtracted at low opacity from the thicker ones, then levels, a slight blur, and a softened mask blend it in with max lighten.\n7. **Ornament (trees):**\n   - A base tree is built with an IE curve node. Branches are separate IE curves, blended with levels and max lighten, then blurred and tightened with a curve.\n   - Leaves start as a squashed ridged bell, shaped by a histogram scan, multiplied by gradient linear 3 and 1, warped with a paraboloid, transformed, mirrored, then inset with blur and curve. Smaller stacked copies are added above.\n   - Four leaf copies are placed with transform 2D. The tree is duplicated, mirrored, and layered at varied scales with levels and max lighten. A square shape and transform 2D trim tiling seams.\n   - Grass follows the same bell-and-scan method, is scaled with transform 2D, direction-warped with gradient and curve nodes, and max-lightened in behind the trunks.\n   - A mosaic grayscale driven by Gaussian noise distorts the shapes so no two look identical.\n   - Tree detail comes from a mosaic slope blurred against itself (4 samples, 1.29 intensity, blur mode), then blurred and subtracted at low opacity.\n   - A histogram-scan mask of the trees is kept. The trees are blurred and expanded with levels, subtracted to cut an outline, then the unblurred trees are added with linear dodge at low value, so back trees sit in the metal and front trees stand out.\n8. **Swirl:** A square tile sampler is safe-transformed 45°, inverted, and warped with Gaussian noise. A cartesian-to-polar grayscale produces the swirl, which is squashed with transform 2D and moved into the top section. Its placement hides the tiling seam. A scan, bevel, blur, and levels shape it, then it is copied in with a slightly blurred mask after levels raise the black and lower the white.\n9. **Edge chips:** A slope blur driven by a blurred crystal 1 is min-darkened over the chain, with levels deepening it. The columns are masked out.\n10. **Bumpy wear:** Clouds 1 at scale 5 is blurred at several strengths and copy-blended with a Gaussian noise mask.\n11. **Overall variation:** A low-opacity multiply uses a mask that is white between trees, mid-gray in the swirl gaps, and near black elsewhere.\n12. **Spots:** Gaussian spots 1 goes through a histogram scan, a slope blur driven by clouds 2, and levels, then is subtracted from the chain.\n13. **Final step:** An autolevels node normalizes the height. The article warns that later height edits can break anything that relies on it, so finish height changes first."}]},"uuid":"43674833-7717-4704-b2a2-152ed2bef034","timestamp":"2026-10-11T15:17:02.891Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"d114e947-70bf-4091-9e92-e5eb5f53ac3b","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":null,"branch":null,"default_branch":null,"status":null,"visibility":null,"error":"pending"},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"06d0a3a1-73e1-48f6-bfac-bc4199173d93","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main","slug":"deep-stirring-lark"}
