uld be fractions of that half-width; export a test image and measure the displacement in pixels before trusting this (11 October 2026)\n- SOURCE (normals): Signed normals match Karma and SOP geometry (components can be negative, length 1) and Offset normals are the same vectors scaled into 0 to 1 (centered near 0.5 with length 0.5) as shaders usually expect for normal maps; the convention is chosen by Type Info on `layerproperties`, Conversion on `convertnormal` or Normal Type on `heighttonormal`; the page says nothing about OpenGL versus DirectX Y direction, so that must be checked on the node (LOCAL Copernicus1.md line 1573 notes that a DirectX normal needed flipY in a Principled Shader) (11 October 2026)\n- SOURCE (tips): `copcache -c` in the textport clears GPU and memory usage; right-click Inspect in the Composite Viewer reads pixel values; the Performance Monitor with an OpenCL COP timing option gives GPU timings (11 October 2026)\n- LOCAL: height, normal and roughness exports should use `rop_image` with colorconversion raw to avoid gamma (Copernicus1.md line 1521), and a measurement of a baked layer is safer through exported PNG files than through `layer.allBufferElements()` (line 1543) (11 October 2026)\n\n## Part B: Javier Perez and professional height methodology (11 October 2026)\n#### B3.11 Stan Brown (80 Level, Grinding Gear Games) (11 October 2026)\n- Source: https://80.lv/articles/creating-tarnished-metal-with-ornament-in-substance-designer/ . SOURCE: the height starts from a uniform color at 0.5 in absolute 16-bit, because the higher bit depth prevents stepping and the neutral midpoint lets values be added or subtracted; divider strips come from two squares subtracted, bevelled and run through a curve for a center dip, a histogram select isolates the dip as the mask of a Tile Sampler of paraboloids (Y raised for ovals, rotation about 40 degrees) blended by Max Lighten; the top and bottom divider is added with Linear Dodge at low opacity; ridges use a