from a blurred one and boosting with a Level; Flood Fill is essential for clean masks and drives gradient-map color variation; Histogram Scan is the main mask tool and thresholds heights to create holes; Height Blend supplies masks and can merge layers without clamping (11 October 2026)\n- SOURCE parameter tips (same article): tie the Non-Uniform Blur intensity to the pattern count so it falls as patterns multiply; narrow the value ranges between layers so a background stands out and layers combine without clamping; read height values with the 2D view Histogram tool; when blended heights give unclear masks, build a low-radius ambient occlusion, sharpen it with Levels and blend it into the mask to recover outlines for Flood Fill; finish with AutoLevel to fill the range and use the community Inflate node by Ben Wilson for a convex, voluminous bark (11 October 2026)\n\n#### B3.13 James Ritossa (80 Level, Painter and Designer brass) (11 October 2026)\n- Source: https://80.lv/articles/learning-substance-professional-tips-and-tricks . SOURCE: dust is added thinly to nearly everything with a Dust Soft 2 node, dirt gathers in crevices with Dust Occlusion masks, an edge rust smart mask controls wear with edge wear balance 0.69 and contrast 0, grunge in roughness removes gloss from edges, an edge scratches mask is softened by a Dust Soft 2 mask, a Mask Editor with contrast, balance, AO and grunge inputs drives a peeling effect, MatFinish Rough adds subtle roughness variation; layered materials such as paint chipping over metal are built by thinking about how the layers wear in real life; the brass took about 15 minutes; it is an interview about surface wear, not a height-map chain (11 October 2026)\n\n#### B3.14 Substance Days sessions read (schedules only) (11 October 2026)\n- Source: https://80.lv/articles/substance-day-at-gdc-2018 . SOURCE: Substance Day at GDC 2018 on 20 March 2018 listed a keynote by Sebastien Deguy and Wes McDermott, Tommy Alvarez and Steve Hendricks (I